        <p>fevers,: two are known co possess- vernmage pro- perties, and one is used for poisoning bats.</p>
        <p>ELECTRIC °SHOCKS</p>
        <p>How is one to know whether an electric) shock is likely to prove fatal or not? In con- sidering the case of a fallen trolley wire carry- ing current at a pressure of 500 volts, the severity of the shock will depend altogether, says Mr. Charles R. Gibson in Electricity 01 To-day: its Work and Mysteries Described in Non-Technical Language," upon the resistance offered to the passage of the current from the-1 wire through the body to earth. The resistance of the body alone will not prevent the current getting through, but if the person is standing on the paved street, and especially if the stones be dry, there is very considerable resistance placed-. in the current's path, and the shock will not be fatal, if it is even severe. Sometimes when an overhead wire has fallen down upon the paved street, the wire has remained "alive," for the path offered the current does not enable suffi- cient to rush to earth to operate the safety de- vices. A fallen line should never be handled , with the bare hands until one is assured that the line is dead. If it is necessary to handle the line, and rubber gloves are not available. it , should be grasped with a folded newspaper, a cap or -tobacco pouch covering the hand. With these precautioas a live wire can immediately be rendered dead by lifting it into contact with the rails.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>KEEP PEPS BY YOUR BEDSIDE.</head>
        <p>KEEP PEPS BY YOUR BEDSIDE.</p>
        <p>They will Soothe and Cure the Worst Cough.</p>
        <p>"Keep Peps by your bedside," is n motto whi;3ll sufferers from throat, lung, and chest troubles, should never forgot. The magic of modern science has at last imprisoned the pure healing balsamic breath of the rich pine-forests, in the form of pleasant and pllatable little tablets called Peps. With the characteristic thoroughness of modern achievenymts, Peps not only bring the breath of the pine forests to every home, but in a highly concentrated form, ffo that sufferers at home can even enjoy more of the healing pine-air than they who can afford to stay at the famous pine-forest resorts of Switzerland, Norway, or the Black Porest. _ .</p>
        <p>If you suffer from bronchitis or asthma, keep Peps by your bedside for they will soothe and allay that 'persistent tickling cough, which keeps you awake at 'night, and disturbs the rest of everybody in the house. If yob are troubled with the drv,hacking cough which speaks of deep-seated lung-troabie, keep Peps by your bedside, for there is nothing which will bring such comforting relief, or check so consistently the wasting .rid weakening catmed by incessant coughing. If the children are croupy, weak-cht.,sted, or troubled -with bronchial coughs, whooping coughs,or wheeziness, mothers should keep Peps by the bedside, for children take the little tablets readily, and they are quite safe .and free from all dangerous drugs. Keep Peps by your bedside, and take them freely if you have any symptoms of catching cold or influenza. 'Taken in time, you will throw off the infection in the earliest stage, and have good reasons to bless the wisdom of keeping a box of Peps always by your bedside.</p>
        <p>Keep Peps by your bedside, and take them when others in the house are suffering from colds, sore throat, and influenza. The germicidal and antiseptic pine- essences in Peps will safeguard you against all infection. Keep Peps by your bedside, hut be sure that no imita- tons or substitutes are foisted upon you. See that the reeistered name—Pops—is on every box you buy. Sold by all chemists and stores at is. lid. or 2s. 9d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>CHURCH CLUBS LEAGUF.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S V. TRINITY —At Bewsey-road on Mors- day. Score : ST. PAUL'S IST.</p>
        <p>TRINITY IST.</p>
        <p>T. Hill -- -.---- 74 J. Well W. Bennett 73 P. Gandy S. Briley 53 E. Hesketh T. Addison F. Gardner H. Collin 75 H. Evans</p>
        <p>Majority for St. Paul's Ist, 201. At Bold-street. Scoro : TRINITY 2ND. ST. PAUL'S 2N.D. J. Hesketh • 75 H Parkes 3O J. Harding 75 W. Pennington B. Heath 49 H Pennington 3. W. Dyke A. Roylance S. Jenks . 62 T. Rigby 75</p>
        <p>Majority for Trinity 2nd, 72. Matches for Thursday, Pebruary 14th. IST DIN-ISIOY. Padgate v. St. Clements. Trinity v. St. George's. 2ND Divisios. St. Clement's v. Padgate. St. George's v. Trinity.</p>
        <p>L. t't N.-W. RAILWAY INSTITUTE v. WARRINGTON CHURCH LEAGUE. A billiard match between the London and North- Western Railway Institute and the pick of the Warrington Church League was played at the Institute on Tuesday evening, February sth, and as will be seen from the scores below resulted in a very close finish. The mach was a most enjoyable one, and the form dis- played by some of the cuists was surprising. This as the first match that the Institute have played with any local team, and the visitors expressed their surprise that such a splendidly equipped Institute had been got together in so short a time. At present the Institute Committee are completing a Miniature Rifle Range,con- structed out of three railway coaches, and are also laying a bowling green for the benefit of their members near Arpley Station, so that there should be no Jack of interest in the welfare of the Institute for a very long time to come. Score :</p>
        <p>CHURCH LIA.GUE. T. Horrabin NV. Owen 6l J. Wolfenden lOO J. Jenkinson G. Parker A. Hale A. Allman T. Lowe 6l E. Hesketh lOO J. J. Millington E. Robinson T. Kennedy J. C. Noble H. Griffiths - - 1017 1039</p>
        <p>INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE CLUB HANDICAP. The final in the Conservative Club Christmas handicap was played on Thursday evening between Messrs. C Oldfleld and H. Collin. The latter received 15 more start than his opponent, but wa,s beaten by 41 points. WIDNES v. WARRINGTON LIBERAL CLUBS.</p>
        <p>At Widnes on Saturday evening. Scores :</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. P. Quinn 9OO J. Davies 125 J. Quinn 2OO W. Patrick 59 H. Laws J. Nain F. Barber W. T. Mitchell BO E.J. Mitchell l5O F. Wright H. Mercer BO E. Jakeman l5O J. Wright M. Quinn f 3. Rogers ....... ........... 150 A. Sanders - - 1177 1401 1101</p>
        <p>WIDNES.</p>
        <p>76 Majority for Warrington on both games, 66.</p>
        <p>Licences Transferred.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court, on Monday, before the Mayor (presiding), and other magistrate?, the follow- ing licences were transferred : Off-licence, 223, Wmwick-road, from Mary Ellen Jones, deceased, to William Jones; Prince of Wales, from Joseph Buckley, deceased, to David Vernon ; Boar's Head, from Alfred Wood, deceased, to Phcebe Wood; Causeway Hotel, from E. Aukland, jun., to J. E. L. Kinsley ; King's Arms from T. B. Potter, deceased, to Agnes M. Potter; Talbot Hotel, from Robert Walker to Thomas John Weir ; and the off-licence at 11, Brick-street, from Lloyd Jones to William Jones. Mr. Browne said he had been instructed by Messrs. Creenall, Whitley and Co., to apply for permission to make certain alterations at the Patten Arms Hotel, for the purpose of making it into a first-class family and commerial hotel. The idea was to do away with the present bar, and make it into an inquiry office; and i to place the bar n a more convenient place between the billiard-room and the smoke-room. At present persons crowded routid the bar, and anyone who had to dine or go to the coffee-room must necessarily pass the bar. Mr. Browne also applied for certain structural alterations to be made at the Glassmakers' Arms. Each application was agreed to:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>NO BETTER PROOF.</head>
        <p>NO BETTER PROOF.</p>
        <p>Time tries all things, and it is wonderfully encourag- ing to find that good news, which we scarcely dared believe, is confirmed after five years, more especially when the news is of great practical importance to so many of us. yaarg ago Doan's backache kidney pills cured me of kidney disorders,snd I have had no return of the troubles," says Mrs. E. Dolooze, 13, Dolman's-lane, Warrington, " I have had a number of people ask rue about my cure, and I have always told them what benefit I received from Doan's pills. Mine is indeed a lasting cure." Here are the facts of Mrs. Do,looze's cure, which she gave us some years ago:—" lor several years I WAS subject to kidney complaint and at times my back was so bad that I could hardly walk. There were dreadful pains round my loins, and I used to come over giddy. My limbs ached and swelled, I seemed full of wind, and my kidneys were disordered. Do44aln,s coubaldckgaecthenokthidinnegvto do me any good until I used these I felt so much better rilith. Afatltejastaeknicnog some of to keep some of the pills by me and if I feel the slightest pain I take a dose before going to bed, and the pain is gone by the morning. (Signed) MAiLyDoroOZE. " keep your kidneys well, and they will keep you well," is the best of all rules of health. When the kidneys are well they can carry out their work of filter- ing out the kidney poisons. When they are ill, you are ill, and you will suffer from Rheumatism, Dropsy, Back- sche, Headache, Urinary Troubles, Nervousness', Pains in the Limbs and Joints, and Broken Sleep. Doan's Backache Kidney Pills cure all Kidney and Urinary Disorders, Ivcature they act solely on the kidneys and bladder. They make you well and keep you well. Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per bot (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence). Of all chemists and stores, or post free, direct from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Ovford-street, Lomion, W, Doan's are the pills that cured Mrs, Dolooze,</p>
