he motion was carried by 15 votes to 12. tion. It is true that the fruit jelly is harmless, which INCREASES OF SALARY. ' is more than can be said for many of the adulterations The Markets Committee resolved that the salary of and practices that go on,in spite of the Food and Drugs Mr. W. H. Wallington be increased from £l4O to a Act. Fishmongers for instance might well be corn- maximum of 150 per annum. I pelled to distinguish between smoke-dried fish, and that Councillor STOTT asked why the Committee had which is cured by chemicals. Nowadays it is difflcule refused an application by George Squires, a weighing to buy a haddock, bloater, or kipper, which does not machine attendant, for an increase of wages, when they recommended an advance of £lO to an aficial who had ' had several increases of late. Councillor ByANS said the Committee considered the wage of 245, with uniform was ample for the light work which Squires did. Councillor STOTT said he had no objection to Mr. Wallington's advance. The minutes were approved. On the recommendation of the Gas Committee the salary of the chief clerk (Mr. E. J. Smith) was raised from £l5O to £l6O per annum. SHIP CANAL MATTERS. The Rivers Committee met for the purpose of con- sidering the Manchester Ship Canal (Various Powers) Bill, 1907, preparatory to a conference with the Traders of Warrington. The DEPUTY-MAYOR, in moving the adoption of the minutes, said the Committee had gone through the Bill exhaustively and had arrived at the conclusion that a deputation should wait upon the company. The deputation did so last Friday and suggested certain alterations which, if the company met their wishes, would practically be all they required. To put them- selves in order the Council bad to petition, but that only meant an expenditure of five guineas. Councillor WILDING : Do we understand that the five guineas ends our liability. Th DENITY MAYOR said that would end their , liability if the suggestions they made were carried out. If something important cropped up they might have to appear against the Bill, but the matter would previously come before the Council for reconsideration.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
        <p>Councillor PE3IBERTON : The Council ought to have, some assurance that no money will be spent on oppos- ing the Bill until the matter comes before the Council. The MAYOR: That is so. Councillor PEMBERTON : You know the serious posi- tion we are in concerning other mr,tters. However, so long as we have that assurance I am satisfied. The minutes were approved. NEW GYMNASTIC INSTRUCTOR. The Gymnasium Committee considered the appoint- ment of a successor to Mr. Kinsley.—Resolved that the Assistant Instructor (Mr. A. Mottram) be appointed to the position of Instructor at an annual salary of £l5O. Alderman EVANS, in moving the adoption of the minutes, said Mr. Mottram had been connected with the institution for many years, and was appointed assistant in 19.4 at a salary of £2 2s. a week. The Committee had no hesitation rn'appointing him instruc- tor, and they told him he must not apply for an increaso for some years to come. The minutes were approved. THE ELECTRICAL ENGINEER'S SALARY. The Electricity and Tramways Committee recom- mended that the salary of Mr. F. V. L. Matthias, chief engineer, be advanced from £3OO to £350. The MAYOR moved the adoption of the minutes and said the committee unanimously agiked to the increase. Out of over twenty towns, Warrington's electrical. engineeer was the worst paid and the profit on the undertaking last year as £2,760. Councillor LORD moved that the recommendation be referred back. Mr. Matthias, he said, had been very generously treated by the Corporation. He had been raised from a secondary position to his present office, and it was rather premature for him to ask fora further advance.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILDING seconded and said the Corpora- tion were paying ample for what titey received at the hands of the electrical engineer. The street lighting seemed to be only in thexperimental stage. Councillor PEMBERTON said Mr. Matthias' salary had advanced from £lOl to £3OO in seven years. He com- pared Warrington's electrical undertaking with Stock- port's, which showed a gross profit on outlay of 12.50 while Warrington's profit was only 9.90. Considering their enormous capital and high charges, the Warring- ton works ought to be making. a great deal more prat. Up to now the concern had given practically nothing to the relief of rates. The capital was £84,000, and the charges were higher than in many other towns. The salaries at Stockport amounted to £6OO a year, while at Warrington they totalled .£l,OOO. On a vote being taken there voted FOR TIIF: AMENDMENT, AGAINST. Councillor Barton The Mayor „ Skelland Alderman Bolton „ Chantler Pierpoint „ Sankey 17 Bennett „ Dickinson Forshaw „ Wilding Francomb • Lord Evans Pemberton-9 Cowls. Ellison Fulton-Smith Dr. Joseph N ield 9, Wilkinson Toole Stott Hawthorn Wilson , Clegg-17. The minutes were therefore approved. THE SOBDIERS" MEMORIAL. AN "UNPATRIOTIC" RESOLUTION. Alderman BENNKTT, alluding to the Committee's refusal to pay for the foundation of the memorial, said it seemed to him extremely ungenerous and ungracious that the committee should refuse to do their little part as they had done in the case of every similar memorial, and relieve the Nlemorial Committee to the extent of fixing the monument and providing the base and the necessary surroundings. He thought most towns would have been not only willing but extremely glad to have the opportunity of taking custody of such a noble memorial. It was essentiOly a work of art and a very noble tribute to a brave man and a gallant regiment. It would only have been showing a modicum of patriotism if the Council had thanked the Committee for their exertions and done their part by providing a site. However, he was inclined to think the Committee might take a wiser and more generous view at a later stage. He wished to utterly repudiate any part or lot in a resolution which seemed to be unworthy of a patriotic town. Councillor STorr said he wished to associate himself with the resolution and he could give very good reasons why he voted against a grant being made. The minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>YORKSHIRE CHOIR STRIKES.</p>
        <p>At Leeds on Tuesday it was announced that the choir of Cast Word Parish Church had gone on strike, owing to the prevailing dissatisfaction as to the appointment of a now choirmaster.</p>
        <p>SAW HIS FATHER KILLED.</p>
        <p>William Caunce, a Lancashire and Yorkshire main line and goods-yard inspector, was killed on a crossing near Preston on Tuesday, by some waggons which were being shunted. Deceased's son was a witness of the sad occurrence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>GIRL'S LEAP FROII A BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>GIRL'S LEAP FROII A BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>A sixteen-year-old weaver named Alice Allinson on Tuesday jumped from the old tram bridge at Preston into the Ribble, a distance of 31 feet. A young student at Harris Institute, named Cuthbert Harrison, plunged into the water and brought her, with assistance, to the side. She struggled and exclaimed, "I want to die."</p>
      </div>
