      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>POWER LOSSES IN SHAFTING.</p>
        <p>taste of boracic acid, or other chemicals, so different are they to the fish when it used to be cured by the natural process, and served as useful breakfast dishes. "Women and Agriculture."—There is to be an exhi- bition of the produce of "Women Gardeners and 'Armen "at the Botanio Gardens, in London, during the summer. It will be interesting as showing the I practical results of the application of women to the minor rural industries, about which we have heard a good deal theoretically during the past few years. It has been taken up enthusiastically by the Countsss of Warwick, who established the College at Studley Castle for the training of yonug women in agricultural pursuits. Many women are born gardeners and will work untiringly for the pleasure the results afford them, but it seems the general experience of these who are disposed to try and win their living from the land is that the work is too laborious, and the results inade- quate. Except on a few special lines, commercial horticulture and husbandry cannot be profitably con- ducted upon a small scale and the opportunities of the girl students appear to be more in the direction of obtaining situations as advisers than in obtaining a livelihood from the land by their own labour and cultivation. Lilac, Lilac Everywhere.—ln the millinery world there is a tremendous rush for lilac, in every shade</p>
        <p>Most manufacturers will be surprised to learn how much power is lost in the shafting of their factories. An interesting series of tests was re- :.ently made in Cleveland, Ohio, in sixteen dif- ferent works using from 8 to 400 horse-power, to determine what percentage of the power was ab- sorbed by the shafting. It was found that in one-quarter of these factories 48 per cent. of the power was used to drive the shafting, that the general average was 56 per cent., and that in one factory 807 per cent. was thus lost, leaving but 193 per cent. to drive the machines. It is needless to say that these shaftings were mounted in the ordinary babbitted bearings. The importance of using anti-friction bearings is thus emphasised; for even if the first cost of anti-friction bearings is quite large, the saving in power which they are sure to effect will in</p>
        <p>most eases repay me a year.</p>
        <p>tnuiai ouriay tat ies</p>
        <p>GUM AND BACTERIA.</p>
        <p>Mr. Greig Smith reports in the Journal of the Royal Society of New South Wales the results of his investigation of the part played by bac- teria in the formation of various vegetable gums. The action of the bacteria appears to be more complex than might have been supposed. There are, for instance, two kinds of gum arabic, one soluble in water, the other insoluble, and Mr. Smith finds that they are produced by two distinct kinds of bacteria. By the cultiva- tion of suitable species of bacteria it is possible to augment the production of gum by certain trees. Under ordinary circumstances some species of gum-making bacteria live and multi- ply without the production of an appreciable quantity of gum, but the product is markedly increased by furnishing tannin to the micro- organisms.</p>
        <p>mute ins germ-Kw.ng power, ana in a- nOW sense "the blood is the life."</p>
        <p>LORD KELVIN'S SEISMIC THEORIES.</p>
        <p>Lord Kelvin, speaking at a meeting of the Royal Society of Edinbargh, dealt with the theory of earthquakes. After illustrating the development of a globular world by the con- densation of a vast number of atoms in the original nebula. Ile shewed that when the density became 6o great that the atoms began seriously to hustle one another, there was the first step to the formation of concrete matter. We had a 'vast fluid mass at a very high temperature, which gradually lost energy by radiation across its boundary. Volcanoes we would continue to have so long as there was any large •amount of melted rock in the interstices, or vesicles, of the solid matter which at present certainly consti- tuted much the greater part of the earth's whole mass. Even after all the liquid material had been expelled from the interior, and had frozen into solid lava. there</p>
        <p>MUST STILL BE EARTHQUAKES,</p>
        <p>twenty,. of the shrinkage of the hot solid into- rm.. 'Winer' must leave cavities 'oetween it and the comparatively cool outer crust. The carry- ing- away of heat from the interior became slower and slower by the augmentation of the thickness of the rock through which it had to escape by conduction. Thus, after volcanoes ceased, there was a prospect of earthquakes on an increasing scale of magnitude, and recurring at greater and greater intervals of time, through twenties or hundreds of millions of years, until the very central region was cooled 'to some temperature, perhaps much below red heat, and the whole earth was so solid at nearly equal temperature throughout as to experience no disruptions by further cooling and shrinkage. This, it seemed, nmst be the flit tire history of the earth, unless at some time or other it was shattered and re- melted by collision with some other great body. The theory of earthquakes AITHOUT THE INFLUENCE OF LAVA</p>
        <p>whkh he had suggested was very easily under. under-toed. The whole crust—that 5, 10, 20, or 30 kilomkres of the earth's surface—was cool and hard, the material hotter and hotter in the interior. Slow' though it be, there was an escape of heat. The interior must be shrinking more than the hard outer criFt. In fact, the hard outer crust would be dragged inward. The earth was, as it were, hide-bound bv a hard outer crust, with a hard interior shrinking and pulling at it. That meant making cavities. The solid earth being undermiped in some places seemed to be an explanatioif of earthquakes, even as we had them now, but he did not mean to say that it -was the explanation.</p>
        <p>The most torrid section in the world is the Great African Desert, where, in the hottest days of summer, the mercury rises to 150 deg. Fahr. Yet a day when the thermometer has re- corded such a feat is often followed by ts slight that is tmcomfortably cowl.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>ASTHMA CAN BE CURED.</head>
        <p>ASTHMA CAN BE CURED.</p>
        <p>A Noted Physician_ Will Prove This To All</p>
        <p>Sufferers.</p>
        <p>The astonishing statement that Asthma can be cured, coming from so well known authority as Dr. Rudolph Schiffmann, will be of interest to asthmasufferers. Tne experience of most Asthmatics has been that little if any relief has been afforded them by the methods heretofore employed, and, in fact, the disease has up to now been regarded as incurable. This noted physician I has, however, after a lifelong study of Asthma and kindred diseases, discovered a remedy which not only I instantly relieves the severest cases of Asthma and I Bronchitis, but has actually cured thousands whose cases has been pronounced incurable. So complete is Dr. ' Schiffmann's confidence in his remedy that he requests this paper to announce that he offers to send a liberal sample package of " Schiffmann's Asthma Cure " free of charge to all persons sending him their name and ' address, plainly written on a post-card, within the next 4 days. i He believes that an actual test will be the most con- vincing, and in fact the only way to overcome the ' natural prejudice of thousands of Asthmatics who have heretofore sought relief in vain. Athough most chemists , in Great Britain have sold his Asthma Cure ever since •, it was introduced to the British public, still the Doctor fears there are some persons who have never heard of it, and it is with a view to reaching these that he makes this liberal offer.</p>
        <p>An opportunity to test, without cost, a re-uedy so celebrated and promising so much certainly should be , eagerly grasped by every sufferer. ' Address Dr. B. Shiffmann, British Depot, 32, Snow Hill, London, B.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>CLIMATE: PAST ND PRESENT.</head>
        <p>CLIMATE: PAST ND PRESENT.</p>
        <p>In the Monthly Weather Review. Mr. F. M. Bail argues that the popular belief that the cli- mate is changing is not supported by an exami- nation of some of the oldest records available, such as Angot's dates of vintage days since the fourteenth century, and temperature averages at St. Petersburg (since 1743), Philadelphia (since 1758), and St. Paul. Minn. (since 1822). Geology, on the other hand, teaches us that the climates must have changed many times. Mr. Bail discusses the general factors which deter- mine climate, with special reference to the changes in the distribution of land and sea, changes of elevation, to Croll's theory, to T. C. Chamberlin's hypothesis that refrigeration and glacial sparks might be due to a depletion of the atmosphere of carbon dioxide, water vapour, and dust particles, and to the changes in the winds that would result from change in the con- figurations of the continents.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED CURE FOR OPIUM HABIT.</p>
        <p>It is now stated, on the authority of the Cura- tor of the Pharmaceutical Society of Great Bri- tain, to which specimens of the plant in flower and fruit have been presented. that the plant which is said to be used as a cure for the opium habit in the Straits Settlements has been identi- fied as " Combretum sundaicum," Miguel. It accords well with Miguel's description, and there can be no doubt, it appears, that it belongs to that species. It is a woody climber, with oppo- site leaves, in size and shape somewhat resem- bling that of the pear tree, and bears globular clusters of small white flowers arranged in panicles, the flower being followed by a red fruit about' an inch long, furnished with four longitudinal wings. The properties, of the Com- bretacece, the natural order to which it belongs, am van' little some are used' in..-TraJarial</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>THE ELECTRICAL ENGINEER'S SALARY.</p>
        <p>The Soldiers' Memorial.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Coun- cil was held at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening, the Mayor (Alderman Smethurst) presiding. There were also-present :—The Deputy Mayor (Alderman Bolton) ; Aldermen A. Bennett, John Evans, W. J. Forshaw, R. W, Francomb,W. Pierpoint ; Councillors T. H. Ashcroft T. Barton, T. Burton, T. Chantler, W. Clegg,J. G. Craik, E. Cunnington, J. Dickenson. D. S. Duncan, James Evans, L. Ellison, J. Hawthorn, Dr. Joseph, J. Lord, J. J. Nield, J. Pemberton, J. Sankey, J. Skelland, T. S. Steel, J. F. Smith, F. Stott, 0. Toole, G. T. Wilding, J. Wilkinson W. J. Wilson ; Messrs. J. L. Whittle (Town Clerk), CT. Hallaway (Deputy Town Clerk), J. Fair- hurst (Borough Treasurer), T. Long‘lin (Borough , Surveyor), J. Deas (Water Engineer), F. V. L. Matthias (Electrical Engineer), J. M. Murray (Director of Education), and F. G. Ruddock (Borough Analyst). GENERAL PURPOSES COIIMMEE.</p>
        <p>TI\IE OF MEETING ALTERED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>"THE BLOOD IS THE LIFE."</head>
        <p>"THE BLOOD IS THE LIFE."</p>
        <p>Dr. Andrew Wilson had in a recent number of Cornhill an interesting article on Opsonins. First he explains briefly the discoveries of Dr. Waller and Professor Metchnikoff concerning the white corpuscles or phagocytes of the blood, whose function is to attack and destroy germs which have gaised admittance to the body, be- sides contributing to the healing and repair of wounds and injuries. Their action is best illus- trated, he says i in the process known as inflam- mation. But this is only the , prologue to the history of opsonins. Later on it has been dis- covered that while the phagocytes did accom- plish their work in ,the direction indicated, they were aided by some outside source. This source, Dr. Wilson explains, is represen- ted by the plasma or blood-fluid itself. Drs. Wright and Douglas, as a result of their investi- gations, have come to the conclusion that the blood-fluid exerts some influence on the phago- cytes in the performance of their work. They are of opinion that the real source of the power possessed by the blood-fluid or plasma is to be found in substances contained in it called op- sonins, which perform their share of the blood- destroying work, not by stimulating the phago- cytes to the attack, but by acting on the microbes, weakening their powers, and making them an easy prey of the white blood-cells. Thus the nnsonie index nf an individual is the mea-</p>
      </div>
