      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>ST. ELPHIN'S PARK.</head>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S PARK.</p>
        <p>PURCHASE OF FRONT LAND AUTHORISED.</p>
        <p>Lively Discussion</p>
        <p>A lengthy discussion arose at Tuesday's meeting of the Town Council upon a proposal by Alderman Bennett that the Corporation should take stops to acquire a portion of St. Elphin's Park adjoining Manchester-rOad. The proposal had been previously discussed at a meet- ing of the General Purposes Committee and rejected by a narrow majority, and on Tuesday this decision was reversed.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT moved:—"That the Town Clem be authorized to offer to the trustees of the Clergy Widows' and Orphans' Fund the sum of £3,000 for the plot of land at the front of St. Elphin's Park conditionally, upon the trastees of the Training College undertaking not to call upon the Corporation to provide a street or passage thereupon."—The Alderman, in the course of a lorg speech, said the plot in question consisted of a good building depth running across the front part of the Park immediately contiguous to the Manchester- road, and occupied an area of about 7,985 square yards, considerably over an acre and a half. It was purchased by the trustees of the Clergy Widows' and Orphans' School who owned the Clergy Daughters' School and portions of the back land adjoining, many years ago. , with a view to the erection of a number of semi-detached houses for the benefit of the widows of the clergy, but the scheme was never carried out, as it was no doubt considered that if the land were built upon, the ;value of the adjacent premises would be depreciated. The land had been valued for the trustees by two lead- ing firms of valuers, and they were advised, as trustees, i they would not be justified in selling for a sum qppre- ciably less than the amount of the valuation. The Cor- poration had already purchased from them some 625 1 yards of land to enable the Street Improvement Com- mittee to ultimately widen Manchester-road to sixty ' feet. The price they paid was 12s. Hid. per square yard. Whenever the road was widened, the plot of land now under discussion would, of course, adjoin it, and might, therefore,be presumed to be worth a similar figure. The price the trustees asked, however (in accordance with the valuation), was only Bs. 4d. per square yard. It worked out to a total sum of £3,302 18s. 4d., and they had written to say that was the lowest figure they could accept. He proposed that they should offer them a lump sum of £3,000, as he was not without hope that they might, in order to close the matter, be willing to meet them to that ex- tent. At £3,000, the price would work out at 7s. fid. per yard, not much more than half the price already paid for the front strip, or to an annual ground rent, calculated at 25 years purchase, of a little over 34d. per yard. The only serious objections he had heard to the purchase, were, first, that the price was too high, and, second, that the sale was subject to certain unacceptable conditions. As to the conditions, the offer he asked the Council to make was subject to their removal, or, at any rate, to their modification. As to the price, it was based upon two valuations specially made for the guidance of the trustees, and although it might, perhaps, appear a trifle high, it was not dispro- portionate to prices paid for similar land in other locali- ties. As to its history, St. Elphin's Park was the oldest part of Warrington. There was a tradition that the Saxon King, Oswald ,was buried beneath Mote Hill, the site of the old Clergy Daughters' School. A church had stood in that immediate locality for something like a thousand years. In the time of William the Conqueror, Lord Lilford's distant ancestor, the earliest of the Lords of Warrington, erected here the earliest Norman stronghold in order to protect the passage of the Ford. We were not too rich in lovely views in Warrington,and on these grounds alone the Park, in his opinion, was worth preserving. But to the claims of beauty and association, he proposed to add the further claim of utility. There were, he knew, some starvbd and stunted souls who cared nothing "for the ashes of their fathers , and the temples of the gods "—(laughter)—and who were absolutely beauty-blind. He should ' be sorry to think that there were any in the Council. But the argument of utility would certainly appeal to them all. And he claimed that the Park was worth preserving, if only on the grounds of public health. It was 195 yards in length, including the passage, the strip adjoining Manchester-road was 147 feet in depth at one end and 142 feet at the other, and was big enough for a bowling green a tennis lawn, and a gymnasium. And now a word in reference to the opinion of the three adjacent wards. He had before him a petition signed by 2,838 persons. Every coun- cillor in each of the three wards had signed it. It had the support of the clergy of all denominations. The schools were all in favour of its plea. It had the support of practically all the leading manufacturers, who were, of course, by far the largest ratepayers Rylands's Naylors, the Faircloughs, the Firth Com- pany, Locker and Sons the leading tanneries, Thewlis and Griffiths, the Castle Rubber Company, Richmonds, Forshaw and Son, Armitage and Rigby and many more. Nearly all the principal tradesmen in the wards had signed it, sad many of the largest property owners. And men and women of every rank and sect and creed and party had given enthusiastic evidence of their support. He had only met with four or five refusals, and every other worker told a similar tale. Councillor CRA tk seconded, and said he had more than once expressed his views with reference to the purchase. They were not only voting for the purchase of a strip of land, but for keeping open the country, which was a matter of vital import- ance to the people of the Ward. If they bought the land and held it, if ever the back land was built on—which was extremely unlikely—they would always have the front land as a valuable asset—(hear,hear)—and it would always be worth more 'than the money they were asked to pay for it that day. They would have 60 years in which to pay back the cost. (Hear, hear). He did not wish a bowling green to be put down so long as the country was preserved. Living as he had done nearly all his life in the Ward he thought his opinion on the matter ought to be taken notice of, and that was that it would be for the benefit of the residents if the motion were carried.</p>
        <p>Councillor ASTICROFT asked if the signatures were from the burgesses of the ward only? Alderman BENNETT : No, the resident ratepayers, manufacturers and workers of the ward. Councillor Asncrtorr : I understand that it was taken to the works for signatures. Alderman BENNETT : If people worked in the ward they signed it. Councillor PEMBERTON : Did the men who live in the ward know what they were signing? Councillor CRAIK : Yes, sir. Councillor PEMBERTON said he was astonisked at the unfair manner in which Alderman Bennett had brought the mitter before the Council. He admitted the desir- ability of preserving St. Elphin's park but what had the people of the ward done that the Corporation should give them barely an acre and a quarter of land whilst in other parts of the town four or five acres been had acquired? Why should the Council inflict such an injustice upon Fairfield Ward ?He ventured to say that the question had not originated with the idea of having an open space for Fairfield Ward. When the Clergy Daughters' School was removed the Training College only bought the back land, the cheapest land. The Clergy Daughters' School wanted money and naturally they turned to see what they could sell and they were quite right in trying to get the best price they could but, on the other hand, must the Corporation buy the land simply because the Training College happened to the there? It was their duty as councillors to act in the best interests of the ratepayers generally. He did not like the way the matter had been brought before the Council. It should have been brought before the Street Improvement Committee.</p>
        <p>Alderman BIEN'SETT : I brought it before the General Purposes Committee. Councillor PEMBERTON wished other matters to be inquired into. They did not know what covenants would be in the deeds; they did not know what the restrictions were end • it WM un-businesslike for the Council to buy land without knowing the restrictions. One of the local papers said that they hoped the Council would buy the field. That was the impression of 95 per cent. of the people who signed the memorial. They thought they were going to buy St. Elphin's Park. A Voiou : For £3,000? (Laughter.) Councillor PintsErrroN : Certainly. Oh, it is all very well, but what did you give for Victoria Park ? Councillor CRAIK : £ll,OOO. Councillor PEMBERTON : It cost the Corporation £lO,OOO or £2OO an acre. Here it is proposed that we should pay £2,000 an acre. The DEPUTY MAYOR: But We had several thousands back.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMPERTON, continuing, said Fairfield Ward should have an open space but he did not see why they shonld only have a narrow strip of land. They should have two or three acres. If the Training College desired an open space they should offer land to the Corporation. Alluding to the petition, he said that if he went round the ward on the morrow and told his own tale he would be able to get three times the number of signatures against the proposal to purchase. (Cries of" No, no.") Alderman PIERPOINT said his politics had always taught him—and he would not further introduce politics into the discussion—to attach importance to the voice of the people. It was not the arguments of Alderman Bennett that influenced him. He was not influenced by the " Ashes of their fathers and the temples of their gods "—(laughter)—but the petition had been signed by 3,000 people who desired the purchase, and that was sufficient for him.</p>
        <p>Alderman FORSITAW said he was in a similar position to that indicated by Alderman Pierpoint, and he believed in backing up the voice of the people. When the matter was brought before the General Purposes Committee they were informed that the price was sd. per yard, and now it was stated to be only 31d. Also the conditions upon which the Council thought they could not see their way to purchase were being removed, and under the circumstances he should support the motion. Councillor WILDING urged that the Council should remove the petition from their minds. He contended that the petition had been signed by 99 per cent.of people</p>
        <p>who did nlt !mew what they were signing. (Cries of "Oh, oh," and laughter). He said that with all due respect and as an inhabitant who walked through the town more than most of the members, there was no guarantee that an open space would he kept. One man told him that Alderman Bennett had slid that the Park was to be bought, and he added, "I wish I had an opportunity of buying; I should build a house in the centre." (Laughter). All that kind of rot had been pushed down the thr ytts of t!le peaple who had signed the petition. Alderman BouroN urged that the transaction should only be looked at so far as the Corporation was concerned from a busin3ss point of view, as they were handling the money of the ratepayers. Sentiment about William the Cenqueror had no value to the present generation in offerin; land for sale in Warrington. (Hear, hear.) Were they paying too much? That was the question which had to be asked. It was only worth 31. a yard. If a reasonable sum had been asked, he lid not think anyone would have objected to buy it as a speculation. When a Corporation bought land they should not pay too much for it,and load their successors with a debt. He was surprised that Alderman Bennett and others who had spoken had forgotten the fact that in that ward they had the biggest open space in the town in a Cemetery of 72 acres. (Laughter.) That was an open space and always would be, and if it was not enough for one ward it ought to be. (Laughter). Councillor TOOLE aupported the motion of Alderman Bennett, and said the laud was cheap at:.£3,000. Backed up by such a petition the Council would be safe to make. the investment on behalf of the ratepayers.. (Hear, hear). _ _ _</p>
        <p>, . Councillor NIELD said the nine representatives of the three wards most concerned were highly in favour of the scheme, and he contradicted the statement of Councillor Pemberton that 90 per cent of the people, or even less, had signed the petition without knowing what they were putting their names to. They were fully alive to the fact that they were not buying the whole field. The heads of all the great manufacturing concerns in the immediate vicinity were in favour of the scheme, but those on the cross bench at the Town Council whose policy was always a policy of negation and obstruction and never construction.—</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD and PENIBERTON rose to object. the latter claiming his right to speak on the point of order, and contending that what Councillor Nield had said was totally untrue. Councillor Neild ought to be called to order as he had no right to make such a state- ment.</p>
        <p>Councillor Loan: I ask the Mayor to ask him to withdraw.</p>
        <p>Councillor NIELD observed that if he had said any- thing offensive he was sorry and would withdraw. The head of his concern, a sound man of business, would not lend his support to a scheme which was not in the best interests of the town or of the ward in which his works were situated. (Hear, hear.) The leaders of the manu- facturing concern-4 had lent their support to the scheme knowing the purport of the memorial, and believing that the purchase of the site was in the best interests oi the ward.</p>
        <p>Councillor EVANS pointed out that all he wanted for the rateyayers was the best possible conditions that could be obtained for them. As Councillor Pemberton had said, they did not as a Council know sufficient of the conditions of the lease. There might be some very interesting and important points that ought to have the consideration of the Council before they committed themselves to the purchase of the land. He was only surmising, but it was a very important matter in con- sidering the question of the price. He, on casually looking over the petition, found that it had been signed by persons living in Lovely-lane, Elwell-lane, Stafford- road, Thelws.ll-lane and Fearnhead. (Laughter.) If he could have convinced himself that the petition was a bona-fide one from Fairfield ward and the wards interested, he should have felt a great deal like Alder- man Pierpoint, and wonld not have had another word to say about the matter. He suggested that further time should be given for the consideration of the matter. His only object in opposing the scheme was that they might not as a Corporation make any more bad bargains. (Hear, hear).</p>
        <p>• „ Councilor ASIICROFT said he knew for a fact that boys had signed the petition. Councillor NIELD and CRAIK denied this. Councillor BURTON asked who had got the petition up. If the College authorities, he said, objected to buildings being erected on the field they would have taken every possible care to secure the land themselves. It seemed as though an unseen hand had been pulling the strings from behind. The land it was now proposed to buy was not suitable for a recreation ground, while the price to be paid for it was too high for anything of that description.</p>
        <p>Councilhir HAWTHORN observed that Councillor Nield's insinuation with regard to the members on the cross bench could not not apply to him. He was not present at the General Purposes Committee meeting when the matter wa.s debated, and he was, therefore, in the dark, as to possible conditions which might be attached to the purchase. There was no question as to the desirability of an open space but it did seem that £3,000 was a large sum to pay for a small strip of land. There was a general impression that the Council had been extravagant in regard to recent purchases. Small shopkeepers found they had a E eve re struggle to make ends meet with the present high rates, and the matter should be seriously considered and discussed by the Street Improvement Committee before they spent such a large sum. He moved that the matter be referred to that Committee for further consideration and negotiation.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR ruled the motion out of order. Councillor LORD asked what guarantee had they that they should not ultimately be required to purchase some land immediately behind the plot before them in order to keep the land open ? They ought to have an agree- ment with the owners of the land behind that they would sot be called upon to pay another £3,000. Alderman BENNETT, in reply to the discussion, said the matter had been before the General Purposes Com- mittee, and at the last meeting was defeated by a smart majority. There was not a full attendance of .the mem- bers, however, and he felt so strongly on the matter that he decided to bring it before the Council. If ever there was a bons-fide petition the paition presented that night was the one. There was an absolute anxiety on the part of the people of the three wards to sign it. Someone had suggested in a similar way that there was someone behind this. He was glad to say Councillor Nield, Councillor Craik, and himself got up the petition and if they ever did deserve the thanks of their con- stituents it was on this occasion. He believed there was a genuine desire on the part of the College authori- ties to meet the Council without doing anything suicidal, but they could not expect them to tie them own hands. The voting was as follows FOR THE MOTION. The Mayor Alderman Pierpoint „ Bennett „ Evans „ Forshaw Councillor Craik „ Fulton Smith „ Nield Wilkinson Sankey „ Clegg • Wilson Chantler Ellison f Duncan f f Dr. Joseph tt Steel 9? Toole „ Stott-19. The motion was declared carried.</p>
        <p>AGAINST. Alderman Bohol) Councillor Evans „ Barton „ Skelland „ Ashcroft „ Dickenson „ Wilding „ Hawthorn „ Burton Lord „ Pemberton • Cunnington-12.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEltnia-To-N ; -I•;;;;Pose you intend to borrow this money. If you do, I hope you will ask the Local Government Board for it before you spend it.</p>
        <p>Resignation of of the Rev. J. S. Hughes.</p>
        <p>About a fortnight ago the Rev. J. S. Hughes, who had been pastor at Hill Cliffe Baptist Chapel for nearly 14 years, handed in his resignation at the annual Meet- ing of the church, but the members refused to accept it, and desired Mr. Hughes, with practical unanimity, to continue his work. In view of this Mr. Hughes made the following statement after the evening service on Sunday :—" I desire to take this opportunity of expres- sing my profound thanks to you as a church for your continued confidence in me, as your unworthy pastor, so strikingly indicated in the voting on my resignation at the annual meeting. I regard it as no small tribute that,after the wear and tear of doge upon 14 years work amongst you, the voting should be practically so unani- mous. I have taken your expressed wish into thoughtful and prayerful consideration, and have come to the conclusion that, however difficult and painful it is to me and to you to sever my connection as pastor of Hill Cliffe Church, so dear to my heart, I feel that for several reasons I must do so, and nerefore ask you to regard my resignation as final and irrevocable. While I fully appreciate your generous estimate of myself and services, I intend closing my somewhat prolonged and perhaps not uneventful ministry here on the last Sunday in March,and pray that God may ever guide, protect, and prosper this ancient church." The decision has. occasioned great regret.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>/</p>
        <p>Cyclist Injured In a Collision.</p>
        <p>A somew-hat serious accident occurred on Warring- ton Bridge on Sunday. About four o'cic.ck John Mullaney, 236, New Chester-road, Port Sunlight, was riding a bicycle over Warrington Bridge, when in attempting to pass in front of an electric car he was run into by a motor car, driven and owned by Mr. J. G. L. Eccles, of Withy Grove, Bamber Bridge, near Preston. Mullaney was knocked down, but fortunately escaped being run over. His nose, how- ever, was badly cut, and he was bruised about the body and severely shaken. Mr. Eccles drove the injured man to the Infirmary in his car, and Ifullaney was attended to by Dr. Brown.</p>
