      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>FOOTBALL. NORTHERN UNION LEAGUE,</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL. NORTHERN UNION LEAGUE,</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF' SATURDAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P. Halifax 1 3 11 Leeds I 3 11 Hull Hull K. lt 7 838 Batley Wigan Salford Barrow Broughton R. The matches Hunslet v. Huddersfield, York v. Bramley, Leigh v. Rochdale Hornets, Wakefield v. Oldham, and Widnes v. Warrington were postponed on account of frost. , HULL v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>These clubs played a most attractive game on the Boulevard Ground, Hull, before about 5,000 spectators. [During the first half the teams were remarkably equal, j and in the opening stages each made a bold and long- ' sustained attack. Bell opened the scoring with a try for Hull, having got possession from a cross-kick near the touch line' but Rogers failed at goal. Rhodes dropped a goal after snatching the ball out of loose play, and Burchell taking a pass from Wallace, placed Hull further ahead with a capital try, which again Rogers failed to improve. Richardson opened the Run- corn account with a penalty goal, and from a rush by .the visiting forwards Padbury added a try, which Richardson failed to convert, This brought the half- time score of Hull 8 points to Runcorn 5 points. There was no slackening of the pace during the second half. Flanagan crossed the Hull line and equalised the score. Bell and Major scored two smart tries for Hull, the latter of vihich wus converted by Rogers. Hull kept up the pressure, but the defence was good, and Butterworth well deserved the cheer which greeted him when, taking the ball from Jolley, he dodged both Taylor and Rogers and placed it over the home line. Jolley failed to improve it. Towards the close the visitors gave a really smart display, and were often dangerous. Result :—Hull (2 goals, 4 tries (18 points) ; Runcorn, 1 goal, 3 tries (11 points) Teams : _ _ _</p>
        <p>Hull—Taylor, back; Bell, Cook, Harrison, and E. Rogers, three-quarter backs; Wallace and Burchell, half backs; Carroll, Major, Osborne, Carvill, Banks, and Rhodes, forwards. Ruricorn—E. Harrison, back; Deakin, Butterworth, Moran, and Flanagan, three-quarter backs; Jolley and Watten, half backs; Walker Richardson, Padbury, Williams, Dixon, and Kennedy:forwards. Referee, Mr. Gibson (Salford) •, touch judges, Messrs. Emmott (Leeds) and Goldsmith (York).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Warrington v. Oldham.</head>
        <p>Warrington v. Oldham.</p>
        <p>The postponed match between Warrington and Oldham was played on the Oldham ground on Monday after- noon. Though the turf had been covered with straw the snow and frost had got through, and the surface was hard and lumpy. However, it was pronounced fit,! for play, and the teams turned out in the presence of 1,600 spectators. Both sides were practically at full strength, the only prominent absentee being Fish. The ' Warrington men wore black bands round the left arm in memory to Mr. R. W. Neil, whose funeral took place on the same day. Teams and officials : Warrington: Berry, back ; Brooks, Dickenson, Isherwood, and Hockenhall, three-quarter backs; S. Lees and Jenkins, half backs; Preston, Thomas, Heath, Harmer, Shugars and Naylor. forwards. Oldham: Thomas, beck; Tyson, W. Dixon, B. Dixon and Cash, three-quarters; A. Lees and Yewlett, half , backs: Avery, Ferguson, Smith, Vowles, Longworth, and Wilkinson, forwards. Referee, Mr. F. Renton (Hunslet). Touch judges) Messrs. J. Houghton (St. Helens) and Lowe! (Broughton Rangers). Preston set the ball in motion and for a while the Warrington men kept Oldham busy defending. Smart passing by the halves got the threequarter line in motion, but a bad pass to Dickenson spoiled the move- ment. Oldham relieved with a strong forward rush, but Berry returned powerfully and Thomas had difficulty in saving the home line. Tyson made a weak punt and • G. Thomas marked neatly in front of the posts, Preston failing to kick an easy goal. This escape roused the home lot and some fast dribbling and rushing almost broke down the Warrington defence. Lees relieved with a punt to centre, where R. L. Thomas fielded well land dropped a fine goal. Berry nearly let his side down through misjudging the flight of the ball, Hockenhall just beating Tyson for ?possession. Some scrambling play ensued near the Warrington line. The forwards got possession, and heeled out, but Jenkins made the fatal error of passing along the line. Dickenson missed the ball and Yewlett rushing up scored a soft try which Ferguson easily improved. The same player soon after added another goal from his own mark. Warrington got well inside the Oldham quarters, and some rather tall kicking by Isherwood, Berry, and Brooks was smartly dealt with by the two Dixons and Lees. War- rington pressed hard, but the defence always prevailed, and safety play was the order of the home men. Dixon marked near the centre line, and Ferguson, with a ponderous kick,imade the Oldham score eleven points to nil when the interval arrived. On•resuming Warrington men forced the pace splen- didly, and though Cash checked the invasion, and Ferguson kicked well inside the visitors' quarters, the visitors were not long before the game hovered near the Oldham goal. A long dribble by Isherwood, quite non-plussed the Oldham backs, and near the quarter line Hockenhall joined issue,and fielding smartly dodged through and scored. Preston landed a goal. Warring- ton were now playing a strong game, and another smart rush by the forwards placed the home backs in diffi- culties, and after R. L. 'Thomas had failed to check the opposition near the line, Heath and Thomas took the ball over, and 'scored in a good 'position, but this time the Warrington captain failed at an easy place. The visitors continued to be dangerous and twice Brooks was pulled up on the right, whilst Hockenhall was checked when he appeared it almost certain to cross. By dint of good forward work the home men got the upper hand again land the kick and rush game being carried out to the I letter, BerrY only partially stopped progress, being injured in attempting a daring save. S. Lees was hurt, but pluckily continued, and while Berry was absent, the ball was taken over, and \Towles scored. Frequent marks to Oldham afforded Ferguson many opportunities to try his skill in the goal-kicking line, but all proved abortive. Faulty fielding by Berry let in Oldham on the left, and Cash scored an unconverted ti-v. Result: Oldham,four goals and three tries(l7 points); Warrington, one goal and two tries (eight'points).</p>
        <p>?DUCE soultr AND PittSON GATE MISSION During the month of January, the missionaries of the Police Court and Prison Gate Mission of the Liverpool Diocesan C.E.T.S. have visited the courts on 240 occa- sions, and have conversed with 1,237 persons in the courts and cells—of whom 5Y6 were afterwards visited in their abodes. The magistrates have enlisted the aid of the missionaries for 123 men, women, and young per- sons, and 90 at the suggestion of the Court officials and the police; five women have been taken to the Society's 'Women's Temperance Home, 318, Upper Parliament- street, Liverpool ; 43 women, girls, and boys have been restored to friends, taken to homes, or provided with employment; 24 tickets for work at the Society's Fire- wood and Mat Factory at Bootle have been given to men, and other employment found fol., jive.—At the Mission Room near Walton Gaol, breakfast, advice &amp;c., were given to 627 persons on their release from the Prison ; clothing was furnished in 38 instances, to enable the citizens to take work • 285 persons: on dis- charge from prison and the pollee courts, visited the Society's Offices, and the homes of the members of the staff outside Liverpool, to be helped in various ways; 203 letters were written in the efforts to help these people. In 21 instances, the names and addresses of persons were sent to ministers of religion in four instances the loss of employment was regained on the At Slough on Tuesday Mr. William Stanley and his intercession of the missionaries; 95 meetings were wife were buried in the same grave. The husband died addressed; the pledge was administered to 159 persons; after a long illness, and a few days afterwards his wife rom -25 men and women were visited in their homesf the prisons, &amp;c. passed away..</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, Februayy 9, 1907.</p>
        <p>LALL RIGHTS RESERVED. J</p>
        <p>ana tnere Is late i stiouta negruage yea. 1--" he paused suddenly, his face and his hjs grew deathly white, his eyes had a terrible look in them, and well they might. A man he had believed dead was coming up the drive; • his brother—whom he could have sworn he had killed on the night previous, to his weading, ivhOile death, because he had gained his wife through it, he hAI scarcely regretted. "What is it?" Mrs. Cole said. "Something has alarmed you ? " But for the moment John Brandon was speech- less. By the time he had in a measure recovered himself Dick Brandon and Charles Dignal were in the room. Dignal took off his hat, looking at Mrs. Cole as he did so.</p>
        <p>"You'll be surprised to. see Mr., Dick, sir," he said. "He's been with me for a matter of two weeks and more, suffering in some way. He don't know himself, so to speak. I found him the night before your marriage, Mr. Jelin, lying injured in a ditch. I thought it better not to worry you, but just to take him home and nurse him. The doctor thinks he'll do well now, and that in time his memory will come back to him. I wouldn't have troubled you even now, only the police are plaguing me, thinking he's the convict as escaped the night before you were wed. I hope I haven't done wrong, Mr. John ? " 'Mrs. Cole was very sharp; in an instant she had put two and two together. This man had been injured on the night previously to John's marriage with Celia; injured, probably, by John. His terror when he saw the man he no doubt had believed dead, pointed to that. The convict, somehow, had become aware of John Brandon's crime, and so had forced him to take him in, clothe him, pass him off as his guest. it all seemed perfectly plain. And this point also struck her in a flash. If her surmise was correct her hold upon Vernon Salisbury was doubly strong, because John Brandon had no longer any reason to fear him. He had injured his brother. For an assault Dick could bring an action against John. That was no business of anyone else's—he had not killed him.</p>
        <p>John Brandon pulled himself together. Now the first shock was over he got back his power of speech, but the steady, unknowing regard of his brother's eyes seemed to him almost more terrible than the stare of death would have been. Here was Dick, alive though injured. Cmmon humanity, and more still public opinion, would demand that his brother looked after him.</p>
        <p>He forced himself to go to him, take his hand. "Why, Dick, old fellow!" he said. "What's wrong with you?" Dick stared at him fixedly. "Dick ? " he repeated. "Ts that my name? I couldn't remember. That man "—he looked at Charles Dignal—"says you're my brother. I don't think that can be true. As you look at me there is fear in your eyes. That's not like a brother is it? Perhaps I forget." _ _</p>
        <p>Charles bignal siepped forward. "Mr. John is surprised," he said. "He thought you were far away out of England. You came home without letting anyone kno-w, and then somehow or other you got injured. None of us "—he looked at John—"know how."</p>
        <p>Dick looked round the room, and passed his hand wearily across his brow. "Perhaps I shall remember," he said; "perhaps it will .come back. I seem to know this room." It was not wonderful that at his words the face of John Brandon turned paler than ever. If he remembered he would know that his brother had tried to kill him, would know that he had stolen his wife. Was it not possible that if that remembrance came back murder might be done indeed?</p>
        <p>_ • Mrs. Cole thought it more than probable, and being a woman who avoided disagreeables if she could stole from the room. In the.hall she met Celia. The girl's face was flushed, her eyes sparkling, her whole expression was full of excitement. She caught Mrs. Cole's arm. "I heard a voice," she said. "I am sure I know it—it is the voice of Dick Brandon, whom we supposed dead. Where is he? Tell me quickly, where is he ? "</p>
        <p>She gave the woman's arm a little shake. Mrs. Cole caught her hand. It was best she should know. The truth could not be hidden from her. It would upset John's happiness, of course, but surely he deserved that. "I will tell you, of course," she said, quietly. "Don't get excited. Yes, you heard his voice. He has come home. He did not die, but he is ill, very ill, not—not the sort of man now whom you would have cared to have married, however dear he may have been to you. He's in the library. Mr. Dignal brought him here. He has forgotten everything. He does not even remember his brother!" _</p>
        <p>The colour that burnt in her cheeks, the light in her eyes, was beautiful. The husband of the past weeks, all that had been, all the budding affection, was forgotten; she thought only of her lover.</p>
        <p>She laughed softly, and threw up her head. "He will remember me," she said. 'Don't hold me. Let me go, you hurt my wrist ! " Mrs. Cole still held it. "You will remember that you are Mr. John's wife ? " she said. A shadow crossed the girl's face. "I don't know," she said. "Let me go!" She went onward, opened the door, and entered. John made a step forward, then paused. For an instant she looked at Dick, then, like a bird to its nest, swiftly, unhesitatingly, she went to him, flung her arms round his neck, and kissed him. • "Dick ! " she said, "Dick! Don't you know me? I'm Celia. Dearest, dearest, you must know me ! "</p>
        <p>He looked at her steadily, then something in his brain seemed to snap. For an instant every- thing, the room, the people, the furniture, swam about, turned over. A dizziness came over him. He tottered, would have fallen if she had not held him. He felt faint, sick and dazed, and then as suddenly as the feeling had overpowered him it cleared away, the mist driftwi by, the sickening, faint sensation left him. Xs looked into her eyes.</p>
        <p>"Why, of course, I know you;" he said, in a perfectly natural voice. "My sweetheart Celia, my wife to be! My darling, I have come home to marry you!" Like statues of stone John and Charles Dignal stood watching them; neither daring to move, both with the same thought in. their hearts. Would he remember next all that had happened? Who had injured him? If he did, what .would happen then?" Dick looked round at his brother and Charles Dignal, a quizzical, whimsical smile on his face. "I have been away from her a long time," he said. "You might give us a moment alone." And - then there was .a silence. Celia's eyes travelled slowly from Dick's face to that of her husband. She shiyored slightly, clung closer to Dick, who still looked with a smile on his face, from them to the door.' Suddenly John strode forward, and took Celia's arm. .wal Jig blow, to YOU. Dick," he said.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>THROAT AND NOSE AFFECTED</head>
        <p>THROAT AND NOSE AFFECTED</p>
        <p>By Influenza—Torgue and Throat Swollen and Burning—Was Un— able to Breathe— Large Scarlet Pimples Covered Back of Tongue —Found Swallowing Difficult.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>IMMEDIATE RELIEF BY CUTICURA OINTMENT</head>
        <p>IMMEDIATE RELIEF BY CUTICURA OINTMENT</p>
        <p>"I have had a terrible attack of in- fluenza, and my throat and nose had given me shocking pain. One night I awoke with my tongue and throat so swollen and burning that I was unable to breathe or swallow. I applied with an ivory paper knife a piece of Cuticura Ointment to my tongue, and to my sur- prise it melted and percolated down my throat without any effort to swallow, and immediately thsre was relief. The Cuticura Ointment lessened the swelling and found a passage. I melted pieces of Cuticura Ointment in warm wat^r and injeeted it through the nost! This not only gave relief but stopp—i the dreadful headache. In two days all pain was gone and also my chest relieved. Cuticura Ointment leaves no bad taste and dissolves and penetrates without any effort to swallow, and it moistens and cools the throat. "I think it is wonderful, for relief is almost immediate. I have used Cuticura for years for myself and children, but I did not think it could be used internally. My worst trouble was my throat. Large scarlet pimples like small peas covered the back of my tongue and down my throat, making breathing painful and swallowing difficult, but the Cuticura Ointment worked like magic. I would have been dangerously ill had it not been for Cuticura. It also saved me, probably, a doctor's bill of about £2O. Use my letter if you wish, I only want this fact known to every one. Mrs. Eugenie de Parravicini, 13 Alexander Road, Wim- bledon, April 19 and 25, 1906."</p>
        <p>/iorE.—Thls letter Is published to show the value of Cuticura Ointment in the treatment of the mucous membrane.</p>
        <p>Complete External and Internal Treatment for Every Rumour ot Infants, Children, and Adults consists of Cut&amp; curs Soap (Is.) to Cleanse the Skin, Cutienra Ointment (2s. dd.) to lift.] the Skin, and Cutioura Pill' (Chocolate Coated, Is. led. per vial of G)&gt;, to Purify the IlloOd. • Single Set often Cures. Said throughost the world. Depots: London, NeWbery, If Charterhonee Sq.; ?arts, Rorts, 5 Rue GO Is rebt. Potter Drug a Chem. Corp., Sole Prop.. fer/LoGoll Fres, A Book tor Mothoro."</p>
