      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>WRITTEN IN SCARLET.</head>
        <p>WRITTEN IN SCARLET.</p>
        <p>1 I By ALICE MAUD MEADOWS,</p>
        <p>Author of "One Life Between," "A Million of I Money," "The Extreme Penalty," "For Love 1 of a Lady," "I Charge You Both," etc.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXIX. FOUND.</p>
        <p>Although he had been up all night, Charles. Dignal did not discontinue his search for Dick Brandon with daylight, and at twelve o'clock -he ran his patient to earth, found him sleeping like a little child in a hollow in the wood. He roused him gently, so as not to frighten him. The sick man opened his eyes and looked about him in amazement.</p>
        <p>"Where am I?" he said. "I don't understand. Who are you—and who am I? Ah ! quick change passed across his face--"I remember. I've got away; escaped from prison; you won't take me back, will you? You're my friend? Please, please, don't take me back ! " lie spoke like a child ;he looked round him fearfully. Dignal took his hand. ' "I won't take you back, of course," he said. "No one shall interfere with you. Come home with me, you'll be quite safe there—quite safe from everyone. I and my daughter will look after you." : The face of the sick man softened. "Yout daughter?" he said. "The pretty, gentle girl who took such care of me? I like your daughter." "And she likes you," Charles Dignal said. "It's natural for young lads and lasses to like one another. You'll trust me, won't you?" Dick Brandon rose at once. "Oh, yes, yes, indeed! "he said. "I'm frightened here they've been looking for me all night. What did Ido to be put in prison ? I can't remember." I Charles slipped his arm through that of Dick Brandon. It seemed to him, if possible, it would be a good thing to make him understand things a little.</p>
        <p>"You've been ill, you know," he said, "very ill; you understand that, don't you? Though you're stronger and better now. You'll be right as ninepence soon, and when folk are sick they sometimes imagine things. Well, you've imagined that you have been in prison—it isn't true. A convict has escaped—you thought you were that convict, because your brain is a bit muddled; but it's not true, and I want to prov-C to someone that it's not true—to a silly fool whc has been running his head against a brick wall, and who, seeing sparks, thinks he has done something brilliant." Evidently Dick Brandon did snot take in all Charles Dignal said. He looked puzzled, but he walked quite willingly with the man. - "I'm glad it's not true," he said, "but, you see, I can't remember. I might be anything. When I get better I shall know who I am, shall I net?" •</p>
        <p>-"When you get better—yes," Dignal returned ; and then neither of them spoke until they reached the cottage. Warder Clark was patrolling outside. Charles Dignal was glad of it. There was no similitude whatever between Dick Brandon and the convict. "Even such a fool as Warder Clark," Charles Dignal said to himself, "would see that at once." He swaggered forward, however, looking pompous, nearly bursting with conceit. "So you've thought better of it," he said, "and a very good thing for you. I—why this is not our man! What do yop mean by these tricks? I won't have it! Do you recognise who lam ? " Charles Dignal grinned. "Oh, perfectly," he answered—"the biggest fool under Heaven. 1 never said this was your man. I know nothing about your man, thank goodness ! This is my invalid guest, and in a day or two, when you know who he is, even you may realise what a silly fool you have been." Warder Clark grew crimson with rage; it seemed more than likely he would have ar, apoplectic fit. "How dare you I" he said. "You may have other men whom you are hiding; how do I know you've not? Any way, I shall keep a strict watch upon your house' so don't try any tricks on me ! " "Oh, go to bed," Dignal said, "you make me tired. If it's all the same to you, I'll deny myself the further luxury of your conversation; it's just as well to mortify the flesh." He led Dick Brandon onward as he spoke, and disappeared into the cottage. Ruth, who was in the sitting-room, jumped up, her eyes sparkled. "Oh, father," she said, "you have brought him back! How very, very glad lam ! " Dick Brandon looked at her in a very appre- ciative manner. "I'm very glad to be back, he said, softly. "I like you!"</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXX. TRY TO REMEMBER.</p>
        <p>John Brandon had intended as soon as he reached home to have an interview with his sister; to tell her he .quite approved of her engagementwith Paul Clifford,that she was to take no notice of Vernon Salisbury's threats. He had thought things over, and felt sure the man would never throw away his own liberty for the sake of revenge. He was quite safe—to do him justice, he would have acted in precisely the same manner if he had not been.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Cole, however, forestalled Olive, who was still in the garden with Paul. She left Vernon Salisbury, with another warning that he should not attempt to get away, and after John Brandon had watched his wife flit upstairs, followed him into the library, _</p>
        <p>"I'm so sorry Olive recalled you," she said, almost abruptly. "It was not necessary, because I• know who the man is, and can make him do just what I like. Why you should harbour a convict I, of course, do not know; but the man isn't really dangerous. I have drawn his claws, so to speak." John Brandon sat clown slowly; he was utterly amazed. How had this woman discovered so much, when the police was scouring the•country in vain? "How did you find out ?" he asked. "Believe me, it is my misfortune, not my fault, that the man is here. He—he--found out a secret of mine—a secret that would have parted me from Celia. He came here the night before the wedding, and threatened me. What could I do? I had to give in to him, but I would kill him before he should spoil Olive's life." He looked as though he would do it too; his hands were clenched, his brows contracted. Mrs. Cole smiled. "Oh, but I don't want him killed," she said. "It's beyond belief, I suppose, but I've taken a fancy to him. I don't want him to go back to prison; after a'. you must confess he's a little too good to live thi• life of a convict. But you need not be afraid of him, he's not dangerous." John Brandon drew a deep breath of relief". "How can I thank you? "he said. "I came home almost distracted; my sister I was determined should not be sacrificed. I saw my happiness— my great happiness—being snatched from me. If you can indeed influence this man, there is nothing I would not do for you."</p>
        <p>Mrs:Cole looked at him curiously. He did not mean that, of course: but he might be willing to do a great deal. "You will say I am mad," she said; "perhaps I am, because love is a madness. Well—l am not ashamed to confess it—l've fallen in love with thiA man—this convict. I want "—she had the grace to colour—"I want to marry him. I am not rich, he, of course, has nothing. We should have to leave England—to go to some country where the Extradition Law does not prevail. If you would help me financially I should be very glad. I ask it only as a favour, not in any way as a right." He was astounded, but he Was relieved. Love, he knew—all the world knows that—carries folk to great lengths, but that this cultured lady should be willing to marry a convict seemed beyond belief; for all that, it was patent she was not joking. "I will help you financially, of course,.' said, "but lam afraid you will regret it. Trk man has evidently been a scoundrel; in all probability prison life has ' done nething to reform him—it seldom does. If you marry him, you will, I am afraid, be laying up for yourself serious trouble."</p>
        <p>She laughed excitedly. "That I must risk," she answered. "I know just as well as you do that I am a foolish woman, but a fool and his folly are hard to part. I may take it, then, that you will help me substantially if I get this man away—take him out of your life for always ? " He drew a breath of relief. "You may indeed!" he answered. "Ask what you will, and you shall have it. Perfect happiness, but for this man___, is within my grasp. My wife's love is win% Take away the one menace to my utter</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <p>"out-1 fovea nor zoo. ono is my Wire, has been my wife for more than two weeks. You must let her go. It is a pity Celia has forgotten she is bound to me- -that only this morning she said she loved, me."</p>
        <p>He pultbd her gently. Dick did not let her go. "It's untrue!" he said. "Celia, say it': untrue. His wife! Why, you are to be mine, not his! How can he say such words? Celia, it is not true ?"</p>
        <p>She hid her face upon his shoulder. "It is true," she said. "Oh, forgive me, forgive me! I always loved you, but they told me you were dead, Dick, and you never wrote, unless—" her expression changed, grew hard.' "Try and remember, were you ill when you came home, or--" She did not finish the sentence. She looked at her husband, looked at him with dislike, almost with horror, in her eyes. "Celia," he said, "you are my wife!" She kept her head turned from him. "Try to remember," she said again' "try—try; perhaps we have been cheated out of our happiness. Did you come home in time? Did you hear I was to be married to—him? Did anything happen? Surely, surely you can remember ? ' He looked from her to his brother. "No," he answered, and John drew a deep breath. "I don't remember any more. What does it matter F As for the rest, you are joking with me. It is not possible you have married him promised, as you were, to me. If you have "—his hold on her relaxed, he laughed excitedly—"what fools two men have been to love you, and the sooner I go away again the better. Is it true ? " He looked at his brother. Almost forcibly John put his arm round his wife. "It is true, he answered: "We thought you were dead!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>CHAPTER XXXI. GOOD-BYE.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXI. GOOD-BYE.</p>
        <p>It happens sometimes in life that with a handful of trumps an interruption occurs which makes it impossible to play them. To Airs. Cole it seemed she had every trick of the game, so far as Vernon Salisbury was concerned. She would go to him, tell him Dick was alive; and thus take all the wind out of his sails. wia nothing to fear John would have no reason for harbouring him, no reason for not sending ti the prison to say where the convict was. Th. man had not in any way endeared himself to hig host.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Cole was smiling very pleasantly U herself as she went towards the room where shr had left Vernon Salisbury, when it suddenly occurred to her also that since John Brandon had nothing to fear from the convict, there was no reason whatever why he should pay her a sum of money for helping him out of a hole. The thought was not a pleasant one, because her own income was very small. She began to wonder whether a marriage with a convict, handsome though he undoubtedly was, would be worth while. After all the risk would be tremendous, and the man did not even care for her. -</p>
        <p>Her buoyant, quick step grew more lagging. She opened the door of the room with a good deal of hesitation entered it, and found it— empty Vernon hesitation, had made another bold dash for freedom. He no more wanted to be married against his will than he wanted to go back to prison. She looked round, scarcely believing her eyes, hardly thinking he could hare dared to disobey her. Then a note pinned to the tablecloth attracted her attention. She unpinned it and read;</p>
        <p>"Dear lady," it ran—" Forgive the discourtesy, but freedom is dearer than anything else to me, and lam making a dash for it. You, I am sure, will not be so heartless as to wish I may be re-taken. Thanks to a friend, who shall remain nameless, I have money, quite sufficient if I have luck, to take me out of England, after which I can either work or live on my wits. You may wonder' considering your extreme kindness to me, that I should have bolted. That I have seen a man whom I supposed to be dead walk up to this house has, in a measure, influenced me. Neither you nor I can draw upon John Brandon as regards financial help. "Tis true 'tie pity, and pity 'tis 'tis true.' I have little fear of being re-taken. The man goes safely who goes boldly. I shall neither challenge nor avoid observation. I will not tell you from what port I shall set out, but I do hope to sail to-day. Good-bye. If your inclinations run in the direction of a second husband—and I think they do—l. hope ere long he may be yours. For myself, it is perhaps right I should tell you I have a wife already. The confession would never have been made if John Brandon had given me his sister for a wife. Good-bye once more. I shall always remember your goodness, but I think we shall never meet again. "VERNON SALISBURY."</p>
        <p>Mrs. C.4.:1e sank down into a chair, the letter clasped in her hands, a look of horror on her face. But for an intervening Providence she might have forced this man into bigamy. What an escape! What a fortunate escape! She looked at the last words of the letter, "I think we shall never meet again," then, with a strange little sound that was half a laugh, half a sob, she tore it into atoms. And Vernon Salisbury's words came true. They never did meet again. So far as Mrs. Cole knew he got safely from England, and was never Ii ard of more. As , a matter of fact, with his wife and children, under another name, as a wealthy man from America, he has returned to England, and the world, which worships money, respects him very highly. As for charity subscriptions, his name figures very largely in them. That name i is—but discretion s the better part of valour.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXII. "AFTER ALL, I AM YOUR WIFE."</p>
        <p>, There are few of us such fools that we do not bow to the inevitable. Dick Brandon left Streatley as suddenly as be had come back to it. With colossal ingratitude he did not even trouble to visit Ruth Dignal and thank her for her kindness to him in his illness. Or it might have been that he had no knowledge of it. For two days. and two nights Celia shut herself up in her room, refused to see her husband, or even to speak to him through the locked door. On the third day, growing weary of solitude, she began to make excuses for him, excuses that were in reality selfish. She wanted to forgive him because he really had made her very happy. Probably she was a woman with no depth of. character—the sort of shallow, beautiful animal- creature that men love, and would sell their souls for. Any way, she missed John's love and caresses. She craved for her liberty; she wanted to see her friends, to shew off her fine clothes, and parade her husband's utter infatuation. "After all," she said to herself, "whatever he did—whether it was as bad as I suspect or no —he did it because tif his utter love for me. A man, of course, hat no right to do. wrong for the sake of anyone, but really I ought to be the last to blame him. Perhaps, only perhaps, when he knocks at the door to-day I'll unlock it, and her wkat .excu.ses he has for himself. Tama Diak</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>SEQUEL TO WARRINGTON r FATALITIES.</head>
        <p>SEQUEL TO WARRINGTON r FATALITIES.</p>
        <p>Firms Fined for Breaches of the Factory Act.</p>
        <p>I The sequel to two recent fatalities was heard at the IWarrington Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor (in the chair), and Messrs. John Fairclough, F. W. Monks, J. W. Hayward, H. Thornton, W. Roberts, L. W. Reynolds, G. Pilling, and T. Looker. In the first case Messrs Joseph Crosfield and Sons Ltd.were summoned for having on Friday, the 4th January, being the occupiers of a wharf, the same being a factory within the mean- ing of the Factory and Workshop Act, 1891, at Bank Quay, allowed two hatches of the s.s. George, owned by the firm, and lying at the wharf for loading or unload- ing, not to be fenced or securely covered.—Mr. Byrne, barrister (instructed by Mr. R. Evans), appeared for Messrs. Crosfield, who were also represented by Dr. , Markel, Mr. H. Roberts and Mr. Mosse. Mr. Augustus Lewis, chief factory inspector for the district, prosecuted, and pointed out that the hatches were left uncovered on the evening in question when I the vessel had been unloaded. John Ray, the captain of a flat which was lying alongside the George, was crossing the deck of the steamship in order to reach his own vessel, when he stumbled and fell into one of the uncovered holds. When found an hour later by his son he was dead. As the vessel had been unloaded it was incumbent upon the firm to have the hatches covered, and as the offencehad resulted in the accident they had rendered themselves liable to a fine of £lOO. Mr. Byrne said the firm had given instructions for the regilations to be obeyed,but on this occasion some- one had blundered. It was the first accident of the kind and the firm regretted it most keenly, as Ray had been a faithful servant for forty years. During the last two years Messrb. Crosfield had spent .C 2,000 to Isecure the greater safety of their servants. The relatives of Ray had received full compensation. r A fine of 20s. was imposed, the bench being of opinion that the firm had done all they could to comply with the regulations. Rylands Bros , Limited, Church-street, wire manufac- turers, were summoned for employing Philip Hansbury, a boy under eighteen. beyond the legal hours. It was stated that the boy, who should have ceased work at six p.m, was working between 7.30 and eight o'clock, when be was caught in machinery and killed, instantaneously. Mr. F. G. Taylor, for the defence, said overtime was being worked to complete an urgent order, but the foreman acted in contravention of the firm's instruc- tions in employing the boy after hours. Substantial compensation had been paid to the parents although the firm were only liable for the funeral expenses. Defendants were fined 40s.</p>
      </div>
