        <p>The Examiner Saturday, February 9, 1907.</p>
        <p>SHIRE, then the MARQUIS OF HARTINGTON, and candidate for a Lancabhire constituency, had a big and little loaf displayed before him on his platforras, and beat Mr. FARRER ECROYD SO corn pletely:out of the field that he has hardly ever since been heard of.</p>
        <p>Always in future, it would seem, we `ii hall have a Protectionist agitation, under one name or another, whenever there is a depression of trade. There are some Chamberlainites who are obviousjy unhappy because the country is at present so prosperous. Prosperity under existing conditions fs the practical and should be the conclusive refutation of all their theories. Had Mr.CHAMBER- LAIN. carried his cause to triumph a while back the condition of trade which we now find so satisfactory would, if it had existed, which is not quite certain, have been attributed to the influence of Tariff Reform ; and it would have been difficult to refute such a conten• tion, for the causes of good or of bad trade are sometimes exceedingly obscure. The one certain I thing is that a period of high national prosperity will be followed by one of depresaion. That is ale invariable experience, for the reason that one tends to produce high prices, the natura and inevitable result of a brisk demand leading to a diminution of consumption and a general slackening of trade. The most we can hope for is that this time is still a long way off. When it does come it will, doubtless, be attributed to what Mr. BALFOUR'S friends call "our one sided system of Free Trade."</p>
        <p>licensing industry—monopolies which had gone on increasing in number and power ever since the first was created by the limitation by law of the number of licensed hous-,:s in a district. He criticised the provisions of the last Act, which con- ferred practically a freehold on licences, and urged the enactment of a time limi!.; to the oporition of the compensation clauses. This is part of the League's policy, and is coupled with the provision that thereafter new lieenc-2s shall only be granted on pap-Lent of the full monopoly value. A temper- ance measure will probably he introduced by the Government in the nest Session of Parliament,and in the meantime the Temperacca Legislation League is performing a valuable s.srvice in com- bining the different shades of public opinion on temperance reform into one active and determined whole.</p>
        <p>The American Ambassador's MR. BRYCE AND figure about the North ANGLO•SAXON Sear WAS an apposite one. UNITY. He gave the health of Mr Bryce at the complimentary banquet of the Society of Pi!grims with that tact and judicionseess of phrase which are chat acteristic of Mr. Whitelaw Reid. He pointed out that whatever contributes to secure Anglo-Saxon unity helps to establish the peace of the. world, and in these days, Mr. Rid pursued, the diplomatist who did not steer his course by that North Star had become an anachronism. We are at a period in the world's history when the value of international peace is evident and realised. The life of nations no longer moves in a narrow circle. The life of the Anglo-Saxon race, of all, has the widest field, and shou'd, therefore, occupy it with exemplary composure and a pacific temper. In that amicable spirit lies its security. The British Empire and the United States must not misunder- stand each other. Their duty to humanity forbids it. Their friendship is one of the supreme guarantees of the amity of civilised nations. None better than Mr. Bryce knows this. He realises the traditions and the fair promise of the United States ; he knows the greathess,wealth and enter- prise of the Anglo-Saxon race in the New World. He has made them a subject of f xact study, and he knows the common heritage of the two peoples in character, politics, and the arts of peace and war. He has written a book about the govern- ment of the American nation which has become accepted by them as an authority, and his mind moves with equal confidence in her past, her present and her future. He takes to America a message of true sympathy in weal or woe, and it is a measure which, as Mr. Bryce himself says, we trust will stand unbroken over all the ages that are yet to come.</p>
        <p>The Councillor who bought A " TRADING" nine-shillings worth of scrap EXAMPLE. from the Corporation corn- . _ .</p>
        <p>mifted a breac-h of the Non- Trading principle quite unthinkingly. He has since returned the scrap and asked that the 9s. may be sent to the Infirmary. The " Observer " was quite gleeful about the matter last Saturday and foolishly asked what Mr. Dodds thought about it instead of concluding more naturally that Mr. Dodds would think, as he has always thought, that " trading " is wrong! If some of the old " traders " would follow this Councillor's example and refund the monies they haye improperly received from the Corporation there would be no need to make further appeals on behalf of the Infirmary Extension Fund and the South Lanca- shire Memorial.</p>
        <p>We feel impelled to express THE LATE our deep regret at the DR. GORNALL. unexpected death of Dr. Gornall, the able and esteemed Medical Officer of Health for the Borough. He was a man all cpuld admire. He followed his father—a very greatly respected official—in the duties of superintending the public health and like him died "in harness." There was another sad similarity be tween the two. Bath were stricken with a mortal diseaseand both knew for some years before death came that their days were numbered in quite a literal sense. Yet neither ever thought of sacrificing duty for the sake of prolonging life. Dr. Guest Gornall, early in his career, practically rained his constitution by the heroic fight he made against the small-pox epidemic, and since his accession to the respon- sible post of Medical Officer he worked with .an energy and devotion which have brought him to the grave at the early age of 40. Warrington can ill afford to lose so faithful a servant, and a large circle of friends is sore-stricken by his removal</p>
        <p>There was little. of import</p>
        <p>THE ance to engage the atten,tion BREWSTER SESSION'S. of tho Mayor and the fifteen other magistrates who attended at the annual Brewster Sessions yesterday. No new drink licences were applied for, and the brewers were conspicuously absent from the scene of so many forlorn hopes. All that the new and enlarged Bench had to adjudi- cate upon was a couple of billiard licences, one of which was vary properly granted and the other very properly refused. Even the Chief Constable's annual report is devoid of any sensational feature. We note that 333 persons were charged with drunkenness during the year. a decrease of one on the previous year. Ever since 1900 the drunkards' roll has maintained a steady average between 300 and 340. The symmetry of the figures causes us to wonder whether the number of arrests for drunkenness is dependent on the state of the poll, or whether the figures are really reflective of the town's drunkenness. That there is plenty of it anyone with " half an eye" can see for himself any night in the week and often enough in the daytime. Our police are too considerate of the individuals who " sup" not wisely, and keep on "supping "as long as an indulgent landlord will supply them. They cumber the pavements and make the late tram-cars hideous with their noisiness and noisesonaeness. It would be to the benefit of the town if the percentage of arrests per thousand was considerably higher. We are living in a fool's paradise if we imagine that Warrington is becoming a sober town because the police returns do not exceed a respectable level.</p>
        <p>According to the historian Sr. ELPHIN'S St. Eiphin's Park was a PARK. battleground in the days of Cromwell. On Tuesday it was the battleground in the Town Council, and the proposals of those who would purchAse the front land in order to preserve the open space from the builder were carried after a severe struggle. The opposition was not so. much to the scheme of providing en open space here as to its small size and large price. £3,000 for an acre and a half seems a decidedly stiff figure, and we hope that before completing the deal with the Training College authorities the ,Corporation will endeavour to secure the preservation of the back-land also. A frontage of 14'2 feet wide will not be worth much as an open space if the ground behind passes into the hands of someone who is indifferent to the view of Cheshire and the fraih breeze from the neighbouring wooded heights of Lyrnrn and High Lagh. Then, as was pointed out by Giuucillor Pemberton, there may be cove:ianti in the deeds which will require consideration. We hope suffici- ent care will be taken in the_ inveitinew; of the money to ensure that the Corporation of Warring- ton will never regret its bargain.</p>
        <p>The meeting on Monday TEMPBRANCE promoted by the Ternpdr- _</p>
        <p>LEGISLATION. ance Legislation League was not largely attended, but it attracted a representative gathering of those to whom the cause of temperance is dear. It is rare indeed that one saes Churchmen and Nonconformist ministers on the same platform, but they sat side by side in full force and sympathy on Monday under the chairmanship of the Rector of Warrington. The great opportunity afforded by the Tenapetance Legislation League is that of consolidating the efforts of all those who desire a curtailment of the drink I traffic and a taxing of its monopolies, whether they be pledged abstainers or not. Mr. Henry Vivian M.P. delivered one of those forceful, logical, and closely-reasoned addresses for which he has already become famous. His explanation of what the League proposed was masterly and made many converts among those who had a strong sentimental objection to "dis- interested management." This is the rock upon which the temperance forces have been in danger of splitting, but under the leadership of men like Mr. Vivian and Sir T. P. Whittaker such a disaster ought to be easily avoided and the path to legisla- tive reform made clear. Mr. Vivian pointed out that the League proposed to give a plwer of lozAl veto to the community, and if the veto was not exercised then surely "disinterested manage- meat" was better than letting a licence go to the trade with the immense driving force of capital and the desire for inflated dividends behind it. He vigorously attacked the monopolies of the</p>
        <p>• • • _ ' - •'jl 14.f1e _ - _ .or</p>
      </div>
