      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>What does Fairfield Ward want with another open space when it has got one of 72 acres at the Cemetery? Alderman Bolton suggested that the inhabitants were all welcome to it and ought to be satisfied. i7s _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. James Houghton, of Cronton near Widnes who has just died was a publican, churchwarden, clerk to the Bola Parish Council, clerk to the Ditton Parish Council, overseer for Crouton, and clerk to the Crouton Parish Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <p>* * *</p>
        <p>There ought to be a cha-perones' room attached to all the public dancing places, where a good fire could be kept up for these self-sacrificing ladies. The general rule is that the mothers and aunts sit round thinking of pneumonia.—" Globe?' * Humility has never been the leading national feature of the Welsh. Yet the "South Wales Echo," after stating that the ice at Newport was "about two miles in thickness," made no boastful comment, but (says "Punch") modestly added that it was" perfectly safe."</p>
        <p>* The Rector on Monday evening said they expected a " bull "from Archdeacon Madden when he spoke, and the genial Irishman did not disappoint expectations. In disclaiming any bias on the question of political clubs he said: "One party is as bad as the other— probably worse," and then turned to the Chairman for approbation. * * * _</p>
        <p>The Earl and Countess ol Carlise, who are united as defendants in a civil action, are not so united in politics. The earl is a Liberal Unionist, and the countess (according to the "Pall Mall Gazette ") an incorrigible Liberal. Moreover, their two Parlia- mentary sons sit on opposite sides of the Commons— Viscount Morpeth as a Unionist for South Birmingham and his younger brother, the Hon. Geoffrey Howard, as Liberal member for the Eskdale division.</p>
        <p>o A case of overcrowding in London was recently brought to light in which there were sixteen persons living in three rooms. Criticising the landlord, a correspondent says "he must surely be the gentleman who, long ago, had as tenants four large families,each renting a corner of one small room. When questioned about overcrowding, these gregarians said they would be very comfortable indeed if the neighbours in the middle of the room didn't take in lodgers." * * Hearty congratulations to Councillor Hawthorn— who once represented the " Examiner " and now represents the ratepayers. He had to fight hard to win even in Whitecross, for the Conseivatives put forth huge efforts to get a footing in the Liberal stronghold. There were many evidences late in the day of free ale or some such voting inducement, and the tendency to this sort of thing seems on the increase in Warrington. We hope to see, ere long, the com- pulsory closing of public-houses on election days in the districts affected.</p>
        <p>Italians kept Deputy-Judge Strachan busy at the Warrington County Court on Thursday, the litigants in a case which lasted the greater part of that day being of that nationality. Some witnesses required an interpreter, but one of the gentler sex discoursed so fluently in English that his Honour paid her the com- pliment of remarking: "This lady speaks English very well, considering the time she has been in the country. In fact she has acquired a fine Lancashire dialect !"</p>
        <p>* * * Two members of the Warrington Society, at any rate, are desirous of beholding a supernatural visita- tion and one, Mr. L. Greening, even wishes to make the acquaintance of a ghostly visitor. The other, Alderman Bennett, however, is a strong believer in discretion, and in a discussion which followed a lecture on "The supernatural " on Thursday, said that having assured himself that he had beheld a departed spirit he would take to his hetqs. We would have thought that, like Hamlet, the Alderman would have commanded his visitor to speak, in the hope that it would reveal some unwritten incident of days long past. 4.• _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>What changes doth Father Time bring about to be sure ! Only a couple of years ago it was the lot of the latest recruit to the Council Chamber,—our esteemed member of the Fourth Estate, Mr. Joseph Hawthorn, to report the speeches of the other members. It is now his turn to be reported himself. We may fairly presume that his past experiences with the pencil will prevent him inflicting upon the scribes of to-day speeches of inordinate length. In his maiden effort on Tuesday, Councillor Hawthorn as brief and to the point. Most of the speakers were neither the one nor the other, and the Mayor had to appeal to them to occupy as little time as possible with their remarks. An hour and a half was expended in settling the fate of the piece of land fronting St. Elphin's Park.</p>
        <p>Considerable interest -has been awakened in Temperance circles throughout the cotton districts by the announcement that the forty-third annual meeting of the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hope Union to be held in Manchester on Saturday, February 16th, 1907, is to be presided over by the first Band of Hope lad who has become Lord Mayor of Manchester. We understand that a tablet commemorative of this interesting fact is being erected in tne Band of Hope headquarters and will be unveiled that day. The Mayors of Bury, Middleton and ilawtenstall and many prominent public men have also promised to attend the gatherings, when Mr. W. W. Edwards, F.C.S., the Temperance Scientist, is .to inaugurate a Scientific Temperance Campaign which wil during the next six months be carried on in all parts of Lancashire, Cheshire and North-West Derbyshire. Already con- ferences of educationalists, medical men, sanitarians, and others have been arranged at which Mr. Edwards, who is the author of the many standard health Text Books, will open discussions on questions affecting public health.</p>
      </div>
