      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <p>Ther3 has been a sudden THE CONDITION check to the very remark- OF TRADE. able increase in the national revenue. Mr. ASQUITH will be in a position to propwe a very satisfactory Budget ; but his surplus vill not be quite so large las we hoped it would be. There is a great deal of accident in such matters. There seems,for instance, to be no connection between the falling off in revenue and the present condition of trade. Trade was never more prosperous than it is at the present time, except, perhaps, in the early seventies, when we were deriving vast advan- tages from the state of things produced by the conclusion of the Franco-Garman War. Conser- vative propagandists are fond of contending that Liberal Governments are bad for trade. There are at the Central Conservative offices, it is pro- bable, some tons of mural literature intended to I explain to the elector that trade booms occur only when a Tory Goverment is in power. Yet it was under a Liberal Government that we had the exceptional experience of 1871, 1872, and 1873 ; and it is again under a Liberal Government that I we find another wave of national prosperity I sweeping over a grateful country. That Governments do much for trade, one way or another, we do not believe ; but it is at least satisfactory to find periods of prosperity coincid- ing with the attainment of the Liberal high-water. When Mr. DI3RAELI won his great and unanticil pated victory in 1874 trade began to be very bad indeed, and continued to be bad for many years thereafter, notwithstanding the fact that the eminent Conservative statesman thought he die corned some favourable movement in chemicals, which at that time, by the way, were ruining the chemical manufacturers. It was then that we had the first decided return to Protectionist views on the part of a section of the Tory host. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S agitation in these later years exactly followed the course of that which was raised by Mr. FARRER ECROYD nearly thirty years ago. The present DUKE OF DEvoN-</p>
      </div>
