      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>ODDFELLO WS' DINNER.</head>
        <p>ODDFELLO WS' DINNER.</p>
        <p>Presentation to Bro. Bradshaw.</p>
        <p>_</p>
        <p>The sixth annual dinner in connection with the Prince of Wales Lodge of the Independent Order of Oddfellows, was held at the Pelican Hotel on Thursday evening. The proceedings were of an enjoyable character and great interest was added by the making of several presentations to Bro. T. P. Bradshaw, P.P.G.M., in recognition of his services to the War- rington District of the Order. An excellent repast was served by Mr. Chalmers after which the evening was spent in speech-making and song. The chair was occupied by Bro. William Holbrook P.G.M. of the Warrington District, who was supported by Bros. Lot Brooks,P.D.G.M., T. P. Bradshaw P.P.G.M Frank Webster, C.S. ; Alban Boyle P.P.G.M., T. Watson P.P•G.M. ; Past Grands W. Knowles, T. Wadsworth, W. H. Smalley, J. Wright, P. Lester, E. A. Spann, J. P. Forbes, and G. F. Freeman (Hon. Secretary), After the usual loyal and other toasts hed been honoured th 3 Chairman, on behalf of the Warrington district, pres3nted Bro. Bradshaw with a gold jewel, in recognition of his services to the district. When they thought that Bro. Bradshaw only joined the ranks of Oddfellows six years ago they would agree that he had made unrivalled progress. (Hear, hear.) Bro. Brad- shaw passed through the chairs of his Lodge and in addition to serving in the district office 3 and the offices of the Widows and Orphans Fund he had acted as the representative of the Friendly Societies of the town, which numbered some 6,000 people. In every capacity he had displayed great tact and ability. In pinning the jewel to Bro. Bradshaw's breast the Chair- man wished him long life and happiness. t ACCIDENT AT FOOTBALL. Bro. L. BROOKS, Prov. D.G.M., on behalf of the t ~ Peasley Cross, two of the players Prince of Wales Lodge, presented a beautiful framed On past officer's certificate to Bro. Bradshaw, in recognition PrimitivesSatur and during a match between Earlestown collided and one of the Peasley men fell to the ground. of his energetic work on behalf