      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>WARRINGTON –</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON -</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>Page. 2. Town Council. Local Billiard Matches. 1 District News. Women's Chat and Science Notes. 3. "Written in Scarlet" (conclusion). Factory Act Prosecutions. Football. Board of Guardians. Rural District Council. Whitecross Election. Funeral of Mr. W. R. Neil. Children's Corner. 7. Women's Realm. Railway Time Table. -Coming Events. The annual ball in connection with the W.arriogton Market Tenants'Association will be held on Thursday next. On Monday the Warrington Junior Liberal Assbcia- tion will indulge in a debate with the Progressive Labour League. The proceedings will take place at Gregory's Cafe, and the subject will be "Is capital essentially antagonistic to labour ?" The annual meeting of the Stockton Heath Liberal Association will be held in the Providence schoolroom on Monday evening. We would remind our readers that the Warrington Musical Society will give a concert in aid of the Infirmary Extension Fund in the Parr Hall this (Saturday) evening, The programme will be an exceptionally attractive one and will be submitted entirely by members of the Society. Personal. Among the successful candidates at the recen, Intermediate Law Examination held in London, was Mr. Frank Carlisle of " Kenilworth " Grappenhall who is articled to Mr. H. J. Longton, solicitor, King- street, Warrington. The Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman an.d Mrs. Smethurst) attended the reception given at the Town Hall, Liverpool, on Monday, by the Lord Mayor and the Lady Mayoress (Mr. John Japp and Miss Japp), to Sir Alfred L. Jones, K..C.M.G. and Sir Thomas Hughes and party on their return from Jamaica. The Chief Constable (Mr. Luke Talbot), has left Warrington this week en route for Egypt. It is hoped- that the voyage and change of climate may restore him to health. Earthquake in Jamaica. - We are requested by the Mayor to state that on the invitation of the Lord Mayor of Manchester he has consented to act on the Executive Committee appointed to raise a fund for the relief of the sufferers by the earthquake in Kingston, Jamaica. It 'appears that great distress exists"not so much amongst the working _classes as the small shopkeepers and others who have lost everything." His Worship wishes us to state that he will be plea to receive subscriptions and to forward same to General Fund. General Ruler's Visit __The arrangements for General Boiler's visit to Warrington on Febttary 21st, are almost completed. The General will stay on the night of the 20th, with Colonel Legh, of High Legh Hall, and on coming to Warrington the following day he will be met at War- rington Bridge by the band of the let V.B. South Lancashire Regiment and escorted to the Town Hall where he will arrive about one o'clock. The lunch will probably take place at 1.30 in the Parr Hill and invitations have been sent to subscribers. The unveiling ceremony will ba'performed at three o'clock PUBLIC! BENEFIT.--lINDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCA.SHIR,E.-Athrt. The Metric System Advocated. The advantages of the metric system over our present involved system of reckoning, were displayed to a large audience at Messrs. Crosfield's works on Wednesday evening by Mr. S. Jackson, M.A. —Mr. Jackson pointed out,in answer to the popular argument that the Decimal Association advocated the adop- tion of a foreign system, that a metric system was first advocated by an Englishman in 1708, and that it was not until many years later that France adopted it. Mr. Jackson proceeded to fully explain the system, showing by means of lantern slide illustrations, how enormously its adoption would simplify calculations. " The Super-natural." There was an excellent attendance at the lecture room at the Old Academy on Thursday, when a lecture on "The Super-natural "was delivered by Mr. W. B. Alldritt, to the members of the Warrington Society. Mr. Lin. Greening presided. In his address, Mr. Alldritt dealt with the claims of spiritualists in respect to table rapping &amp;c ,all of which he said were proved to be mere trickery. He thought, however, that there was something to be claimed for telepathy and that they would probably learn more about it in the future.—An interesting discussion followed in which Mr. Greening, Mr. 0. Lofthouse, Mr. C. Madeley, Alderman Bennett, Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins, and Mr. A. E. Bent took part. Italians at Law. At the County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Deputy-Judge Strachan' K.C., Carlo Raffo, 4 aged 17, claimed the sum of £l2 from Bernado Mo nfredi, ice cream dealer' Church-street, being the balance of wages alleged to be due. Both parties are Italians and as the plaintiff is unable to speak English an interpreter was requisitioned. Mr. Wingate Saul, 'instructed by Messrs. Cobbett, Wheeler, and Cobbett, Manchester, appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr. T. S. Steel defended.—The case for the plaintiff was that he was engaged by the defendant at a monthly wage of 1.65., with food and lodgings. In cross-examination by Mr. Steel plaintiff said that from time to time he had received money from he defendant, and also clothing. Plaintiff said he remembered the defendant taking him to Widnes and paying for many drinks,and when they returned he (plaintiff) signed a document foregoing his claim to the 1,12. He did not, however, thoroughly understand it.—Mr. Steel submitted that the agreement which was made quite voluntarily by the plaintiff debarred him from his remedy in that Court.—After an exhaustive hearing his Honour-gave judgment for the plaintiff for £ll.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <p>Ragged School and Mission. On Sunday evening the pictures shown at the lantern service were, The stations of the Cross." Mr. S. Gough described them and the lessons were most instructive. Miss J. Miller sweetly sang an illustrated hymn. Mr. Blakemorp accompanied and Mr. Frank Owen manipulated the lantern.</p>
        <p>Foundry Fire at Warrington. Some excitement was caused at noon on Wednesday when it became known that a fire had broken out at Whyman'sFoundry,Dallam-lane.The Fire Brigade were quickly on the scene and found that a wooden structure used as a warehouse was alight. Although the fire- men found difficulty in 'combatting the outbreak at first owing to the dense smoke, a few hours sufficed to extinguish it. There was not much damage. Taxation of Land Values At the eighth meeting of the Warrington Branch of the E.L.T.L.V. in Gregory's Cafe on Wednesday, the last rehearsal of the "Story of my Dictatorship," I. and IL took place. The performance will be given next Friday, February 15th, in the Co-operative Hallf Cairo-street, at 7.45 p.m. A short report was made o the starting of a class to read Henry George's" Science of Political Economy," with 21 students, on Saturday last in Gregory's Cafe. WOOD CFMINEY PIECES, Fire Grates and Tiles unrivalled seletion, inspection invited.—.T. S. GA.RLAND, Builders' Ironmonger, Stc., 51 to 55, Bridge sL, Wirrington.—Advt. Shorthand Writers' Asseelation. The weekly meeting of the ab3ve was hell at the Winrnarleigh Cafe on Wednesday night, when there was a goDd.attendance. After speed practice a paper was read by Mr. T, Collins, F.C.I.S , on '• Banking and Commerce."—The paper proved to be most interesting and instructive, Mr. Collins handling his subject extremely well. Several of the members favourably commented uppn the paper, and a hearty vote of thanks was accorded the Lecturer. N.S P.C.C. Work. - -</p>
        <p>- - The Warrington aud District Branch of. the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children has during the quarter ended 31st December, 1900, inquired into 74 complaints of neglect, ill-treatment and other wrongs of childhood, of which all were found to be true, affecting 223 children and 110 offenders. Action was taken as follows : —Sixty- nine cases were warned, 5 were prosecuted (convicted 5, discharged nil). The Society's Inspectors made 432 visits of sfipervision.</p>
        <p>UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS. Advt.— Popular Penny Concert. An excellent programme was given at the popular concert in the Bold-street schools on Saturday. The Rev. J. Manning presided over a crowded attendance. Songs were given by the Misses Cox and Carrie Hastings, and Mr. L. Aukland, whilst recitations by Miss Gertrude Maddock, of Frodsham, were a feature of the evening. Mrs. Foreman was the accompanist. The cinematograph portion was in the hands of Mr. William Parkinson, of Legh-street. The pictures were much enjoyed, being very clear and well-defined. During an interval in the programme, a sh-brt address was delivered by the Rev. E. J. Gawne. British Women's Temperance Association. A meeting of the above Society was held on Tuesday afternoon in Bold-street school, Mrs. Garnett pre- siding over a good attendance. Mrs. F. Coop, of Southport gave a report of her visit to the Boston Convention. Her account was most interesting, telling of women from all parts of the world meeting together to talk of work they were each doing in their own country. Temperance work is on the increase and is taken part in by all degrees of men and women. Those who wish to go with the flowing tide must first show the example then strive to win others to their cause. Miss Bessie Dutton proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Coop for tie pleasure that had been derived from the reading of her paper. Mrs. Pitt in seconding said she hoped the report would be an incentive to them to go on with the good work. A report of Cottage meetings was given, when Mrs. Simpson told how she had taken 15 pledges at their gatherings of poor women and she hoped that willing workers would come and take part in the great work that was waiting to be done. Two new members were enrolled.</p>
        <p>LA.DrEs' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Adtv.</p>
        <p>Ambulance Ball.</p>
        <p>The third annual ball in connection with the War- rington Corps of the St. John Ambulance Brigade was held at the Conservative Club Assembly Room on Thursday evening and proved a great success. The necessity of maintaining a trained body of ambulance men mast be obvious and in appealing for generous support for this effort the committee pointed out that the onus of carrying on the work falls mainly upon the members, who, in addition to making themselves efficient first-aid men at considerable sacrifice of time, have also to pay an annual subscription. The attend- ance numbered about 100 and Chief Surgeon Dr. Adams was present during the evening. Mr. A. Starkey's band played an excellent selection of music. The duties of M.C.'s were discharged by Superinten- dent Baldry, Third Officer Taylor and Sergeant Holloway. The room had been very tastefully decor- ated by Mr. W. Evans, and presented a pleasing appearance. The catering was in the hands of Mrs. Shaw, of Church-street, and gave satisfaction. Mr. A. E. Spann was an able secretary to the Ball Committee.</p>
        <p>STRANGE'S A.l. Crystal LAMP OIL in barrels or drums delivered free.--Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt. Richmond's Fire Brigade Dinner. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The second annual dinner of the combined" A "and. "B " brigades was held on Monday evening* at the Mersey Hotel, Mersey-street. Mr. H. P. Hughes (Warrington Cashier) presided, and the vice-chair was occupied by Mr. John R. Burnett, Works' Manager, and superintendent of the Fire Brigades. In addition to members of the brigades, the party included the Foremen, and other friends who had been invited as guests. After the tables were cleared there was an entertainment provided entirely from among employes of Messrs. Richmonds. The programme opened with a pianoforte solo by Mr. J. W. Pemberton. Songs were contributed by Mr. H. Hill, Mr. H. Wyatt, Ur. Fred Taylor, Mr. J. Mairs, Mr. T. Edelsten, Mr. W. Aiker4tead, and Mr. J. Hogan Recitations, in the Lancashire dialect, were given by Mr. J. W. Pemberton and Mr. H. Fairbrother. A much appreciated feature of the entertainment was a series of short selections of high-class, vocal and instrumental music, given by Mr. John Bytrnett, on his Zonophone. Votes of thanks were accorded t i Mr. H.P. Hughes for presiding, to Mr. Burnett for his large share in the programme to Mr. J. W. Pemberton for his valuable services as soloist, reciter, and accompanist; and tu Mrs. Butter- worth for the excellence of the catering. Thanks were also given to the Captain of " A " Brigade (Mr. H. Warburton) ; to the Captain of " 13 " Brigade (Mr. Clem. T. Cox, who is also hon. sec. to both brigades), and to the Committee (Messrs. Walter Wrench, J. Barnes, W. Kirk, and John Aikenhead), for the admirable arrangements that had been made to organise such an enjoyable eveniug. The proceedings terminated with the singing of the National Anthem. TABLE, FLOOR, AND HANGING LAMPS in large variety. Latest patterns.—J. S. GARLAND, Iron- monger Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. Church Society Conversazione.</p>
        <p>The annual co-nversazione in connection with the I Church Society was held in the Parr Hall on Wednes- day evening and proved successful and enjoyable. A I large gathering listened to an interesting musical pro- gramme which had been arranged by Mr. T. D. Amos, choirmaster of Holy Trinity Church. There were songs by Miss Chambers and Mr. L. Aukland, humorous items by Mr. Williams, of Liverpool, selections by a quartette party, and solos on a gramo- phone lent by Mr. T. Boothroyd. During an interval an address was delivered by the Rev. C. Harris, organizing secretary to the Liverpool'eathedral fund, who referrea briefly to the education questionnnd said the common cause of the children's danger had bound Churchpeople together as nothing else could have done. Men and women of all shades of opinion had stood shoulder to shoulder and the Church of England had emerged stronger for the conflict. The rev. gentleman proceeded to speak of the Cathedral scheme, pointing out what a great purpose the build- ing would serve and how enthusiastically the work had been taken up. Canon Morley Stevenson (presid- ent of the Society) said they had anticipated that an Education Bill would be brought in to remedy the grievance whi.-1 existed in some parts of the country whore Nonconformist children were unable to get instruction in the faith of their parents. It was right that that grievance should be remedied but when the Government brought in their Bill and tried to remedy a grievance by creating a much greater one by saying that the Church of England people were not to have Church teaching in the schools they had built for the purpose, there was only one thing to be done, and the Church of England rose almost as one ' man to resist the measure. During. the evening light refreshments were served, the catering being in the ...hands of Atkinson's Cafes, Limited.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, Foruary 9, 1907. CONGREGATIONALISM IN WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, Foruary 9, 1907. CONGREGATIONALISM IN WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Warrington and the Ship Canal. We understand that an import3nt Conference is about to take place between the Corporation and the Rivers Preservation Society with regard to the Ship Canal (Various Powers) Bill, 1997. The time for petitioning expires on the 19th inst. Death of Mr. John Clarke. The death occurred at his residence, 270, Manchester-road, on Thursday, of Mr. John Clarke. The deceased gentleman, who was 58 years of age, was at one time licensee of the Ring-o'-Bells, Church- street, and subsequently of the Coffee House Inn, Horsemarlret-street. For many years he was a chorister at the Parish Church. Ho leaves a widow and eleven children.</p>
        <p>Whist Drive. Students of the Warrington School of Art held an enjoyable whist drive in Atkinson's Cafe, Bridge- street, on Saturday evening. The ladies' prize was won by Miss Jackson, and the consolation prize by Miss Davenport, Mr. G. S. Pendlebury won the gentleman's prize, and Mr. J. Welsby the consolation prize. D mcing and guns were afterwards indulged in until 12 o'clock,</p>
        <p>" HERALD " Krrcam RINGES and " Peveril " Fire Grates are hard to best.—Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. Warrington Junior Liberals Honoured. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the C Attica of the Lanca- shire and Cheshire Young British Liberals'Federation was held at Manchester on Wednesday, when the following officers were elected: Chairman, Mr. C. L. Williams (Stockport) ; vice-chairman, Mr. H. Gray (Warrington) ; secretary, Mr. J. A. Bogle (Stockport) ; assistant secretary, Mr. F. G. Lloyd (Manchester), and treasurer, Nfr..ll. W. Guest (Warrington). Football Club Dinner. •</p>
        <p>A successful dinner-and social in connection with the St. Barnabdti A.F.C. took place in the Imperial Hotel, Bewsey-road, on Tuesday night. An excellent repast was served by the host and hostess, Mr. and Mrs. Partridge, who kindly, gave the proceeds to the funds of the club. After dinner a number of toasts were submitted, and a musical programme followed. Mr. J. Etnson contributed several humorous sketches, and songs were given by Messrs. H. Bennett, H. Hawthorn, W. Duncan, W. Skidmore and A. Bennett, and Mr. F. Ford Very ably rendered two cornet solos. The duties of accompanist were performed by Hr. H. Seed. Crosfleld's Ball.</p>
        <p>. The girls of the milling department at Messrs. Cresfield's Works held their fifth annual ball at the Parr Hall last evening. The function was highly successful and enjoyable, the attendance numbering about 200. The hall was beautifully decorated and a charming scene was presented. The works orchestra provided a choice selection of music. —The m. 0,8. were Messrs. F. J. Lord and F. Smith. During intervals vocal items were contributed by Misses E. Barnes, Carrie Hastings, V. Hewitt and J. Willett.</p>
        <p>Liberal Ward Meeting. The members of the Latchford Ward Liberal Committee vs ere on Thursday evening entertained to tea by Councillor Ellison and Mr. W. Roberts. An excellent repast was served in the Temperance Hall, Knutsford-road, after which the annual business meeting of the Committee was held,Councillor Ellison presiding in the absence of Mr. J. Hughes Hill. The following officers were elected; chairman, Mr. W. Roberts, vice chairmen, Alderman Pierpoint, Coun- cillor Ellison and Messrs. J. H. Hill, T. Barnes, W. Bibby, W. Adams, W. Hayes, and W. T. Wilkinson, secretary, Mr. F. Atkinson, assistant secretaries, Messrs. J. Wolfenden, J. Adams and J. Walker. Mr. J. Hughes Hill was cordially thanked for his services as the chairman of the Committee during the past year, a similar compliment being paid to Councillor Ellison and Mr. W. Roberts for their hospitality. "Shall I Pray for My Dead ?" This, was the theme of the address given by the Rev. J. F. Matthews at the Men's Meeting at Toft's Assembly Room on Thursday pvening. —Broadly speaking, said the speaker, Christendom was divided into three camps, holding different views on the sub- ject of prayers for the dead. The Greek Church did , not teach purgatory, and prayers for the dead went by the board. The Roman Church taught and practised both, , whilst Protestants taught that Heaven awaited the righteous, whi!e punishment was the lot of the wicked. It argued no middle course. The doctrine 'of prayers for the dead implied an intermediate state. Why one intermediate state? Why not a hundred? It seemed much more reasonable that when men died those who had done good passed at once into the full enjoyment of Eternal life in the presence of God, while the wicked eompanied with the. retribution due to their transgressions. Surely it was much better to teach the infinite love of the Father, who wisheth not the death of any sinner, but stretcheth out his hand to save and uplift the worst. Let them make the pros- pect of life for each man here worthier and better, that he might walk straight and live'an upright, clean, god, pure life. They would have no need of the cold comfort of praying for the dead, but the certainty that if their living were with God they were beyond the need and reach of any prayers. If they were not human, affection and regard for them might find its deepest peace in the fact that "He doeth all things well."</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER TUB CLOCK F3R BEST BOOTS IN LANCA.SRIRB.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Death of Mr. W. T. Crank. Warrington people heard with regret of the death, which occurred on Saturday, of Mr. W. T. Crank, a valuable member of the Town Clerk's department. Mr. Crank had for some time been suffering from phthisis but it was only a few weeks ago that he left work in order to take a short holiday and his end oame as a painful shock to his fellow employees. Mr. Crank was an official with about 16 years' service in the Corporation, having spent ten years at the library prior to joining the Town Clerk's staff six years ago. As chief assistant at the Library he ably assisted Mr. Madeley in the changes which took place when the Lending Library was made free and by his unvarying courtesy earned the esteem of the many borrowers. Later, he took charge, under Mr. Madeley, of the Technical Instrnction classes which had been com- menced. On transferring his services to the Town Clerk s department he was appointed to act as record clerk and his work, which was of a technical character requiring a knowledge of detail, was always carried out in . a highly satisfactory manner. The funeral took place on Thursday afternoon at the Cemetery prior to which a service was held in St. Paul's Church, conducted by the Rev. W. Bracecamp. The relatives and a large number of friends of the deceased were present. Many more friends and fellow officials awaited, the arrival of the cortege at the Cemetery, the following members of the Council being also present --the Mayor (Alderman Smethurst) Alder- man Bennett and Councillor Chantler.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Services for Sunday. Bold-street Wesleyan Church: Preachers, 10.30, Rev W. Mellor ; 6.30, Rev. S. Pitt. Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church : Preachers, 10.30, Rev. S. Pitt ; 6.30, Rev. E. J. Gawne. Wycliffe Congregational Church: Preacher, 10.30 and 6.30, Rev. J. Yonge. Latchford Baptist Church: Preacher, Rev. J. F. Matthews. Subjects, 10.30," The liberty of Christ 6.0 "On this rock." Golborne-street Biptist Church: Preacher, Rev. H. A. Procter, M.A., Liverpool. Subjects, 10 30, " The achievements of Christianity " ; 6.30, "What shall we 0 with Christ ?"</p>
        <p>Wyc e P.S.A. : A special open meeting of this Society will be held under the presidency of Mrs. Yonge. An address will be delivered by Mrs. Moores, and selections will be given by the Co-operative Prize Male Voice Choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>A new departure was made at this Church on Sunday, and proved a success. It took the form of a popular service, which was held in the afternoon, under the presidency of Dr. James Bennett, of Padgate, and was largely attended. Several of Mendelssolm's choruses were given by the choir, the principal soloists being Mrs. Arthur Booth, Misses E. Taylor, and M. Gandy and Messrs. H. Plumpton and J. Clayton. An address on Mendelssohn was given by the Rev. J. Manning. A collection was taken on behalf of the choir fund.</p>
        <p>------ Primitive Methodist Anniversary. The church anniversag in connection with the Legh- street Primitive Methodist Chnrch was held on Sunday the preacher for the day being the Rev. J. Gillender, of Lymm. The morning text was "All Scriptures are inspired by God," the preacher giving an emphatic denial to the theories brought forward by some men that the Scriptures are not divine. In the afternoon a capital musical service was rendered and was much appreciated, Mr. J. Griffiths presiding over a fair attendance. At the evening service Mr. Gillender's text was, "Yet this man is worthy to receive more glory than Moses, inasmuch as the builder of the house hath more honour than the house'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Proposed New Church at Cockhedge.</head>
        <p>Proposed New Church at Cockhedge.</p>
        <p>For some years the members of Wycliffe Cockhe-dge Mission have felt dissatisfied with the accommodation afforded them in the present-Mission Hall. The need for a sew arid more commodious building was keenly I recognized, and steps were taken towards the erection ! of a new Church. 'or this purpose a building fund ' was opened and zno was quickly raised, and daring the 1 week a sale of work has been held with a view to : augmenting this sum. On Thursday the sale was opened by Mr. S. R. Armitage, Mr. H. Roberts presiding over a large attendance. The Rev. H. WALL briefly explained the object of the sale. They were all agreed that a new Congregational Church was needed. In the past they had met at the ' Mission, not under the best of circumstances, and had week by week carried on a successful work. They were not, however, satisfied with what they had been doing,! and wanted to launch out in a larger sphere. It was hoped that by that sale they would raise sufficient to enable them to purchase a site, and commence building operations before long, (Applause). Mr. H. ROBERTS said that seeing the population of Warrington had increased by 34,00 since the Mission started he thought there was sufficient justification for the erection of another Congregational Church. There must be in that added population a very large number who were sympathetic to Congregationalism and they ought to get hold of then). (Applause.) In-introducing Mr. Armitage, Mr. Roberts spoke in high terms of the support Congregationalism in Warrington had I receilied from the Armitage and Rigby families. I Mr. 'ARMITAGE at the outset, expressed regret on behalf of Mrs. Ziba Armitage that she was not able to be present. Some seventy years ago his uncle and his father were greatly impressed by the need for their people to have a place of worship nearer their homes than Wycliffe, and the Mission was built. At that time there was no Nonconformist place of worship in the district and they came their as pioneers, and a noble work had been done. (Hear, hear.) So many Churches had sprung up since then that it seemed as though they were almost superfluous. They were broad- minded enough to recognize that there were otht,r means of grace besides Weir own and they were per- fectly willing to leave the field to others and to seek a new field for themselves. (Hear, hear.) His only hope was that they would not go to) far away. He always felt that the great spting of religious feeling was expressed very largely by the free-will offering of the people. It did not do for them to place the responsi- bility upon one or two willing shoulders and he hoped , that when they got the new church opened they would , all do their best and strive to become self-supporting as quickly as possible. (Applause). The Rev. H. WALL proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Armitage and the Chairman for their services. Mr. W. BBSWICK seconded and the vote was heartily aacorded. _ _ _</p>
        <p>A brief reply- by both gentlemen concluded the speech-making and the business of the day commenced.</p>
        <p>THE STALLHOLDERS. The stalls,which contained a great variety of articles and presented an attractive appearance, were in charge of the following ladies : Ladies' Sewing Class :—Mesdames Ashton, Shaw, Daniels, Wallington, Berry, and Hibbert. Sunday Sunday :—Misses Clayton, Eva Wakefield, and Joynson, Mesdames F. Morrall, F. Berry, G. T. Rushton, and J. Pheonix.</p>
        <p>_ Congregational:—Mesdames McClure, North and Reid. Novelty Stall :—Mesdames Syers, and Crozier. Plants, Flowers, and Fruit :—Nfr. J. Pearson, Mrs. Pearson, Misses Pearson,Lucy Pearson,and Annie Pearson. Refreshment Stall :—Mesdames Hackett, Dell, and Warhurst. Misses Senior, Mary Senior, Gertrude Clayton, Amy Allen, Dorothy Hankinson. Bran Tubs :—Misses Winnie Evans, and Ann Senior. Weighing Machine, Electric Battery, Rifle Range : Mr. J. W. Brown. Thursday's sales realized £55.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>YESTERDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</head>
        <p>YESTERDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>_ There was again a large attendance at the opening ceremony yesterday afternoon, which MS performed by Mrs. Rvlands, Massey Hall. The key. J. YONGE presided, and in introducing Mrs. Rylands, spoke warmly in support of the object of the sale.</p>
        <p>Mrs. RYLANDS expressed the pleasure it gave her to be present and to show her sympathy in the work which they were doing. he believed that missions did a necessary and good work which the larger churches and chapels could not do. They often found that people who lived in the back streets awl the slums would not attend large places of worship but attended missions, where they felt more at home. She therefore felt that missions deserved the support of all Christian people, and she hoped the .object of the sale would be attained. (Applause). Votes of thanks were heartily accorded to Mrs. Rylands and the Rev. J. Yonge for their services. On both days the proceedings at the bazaar were rendered additionally attractive by musical items which were contributed by Miss Lloyd Williams' Juvenile Orchestra, Miss Bailey, and Mr. E. Dell. Selections on the gramophone were given by Mr. W. Berry. Interest- ng shadowgraph perforrninces were also given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>VISIT OF MR. J. A. SEDDON, M.P.</head>
        <p>VISIT OF MR. J. A. SEDDON, M.P.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening last a meeting of Mr. Seddon and his supporters was held in the Padgate day schools. There was a very good attendance, chiefly consisting of the artisan class. Mr. T. Plumb, President of the Warrington Trades Council, occupied the chain—The Chairman, in the course of his opening remarks' stated the objects of the meeting and also that Mr. Seddon desired to give an account of his stewardship as their representative in Parliament and also claimed that the results of the past session justified the existence of a Labour Party. Mr. Seddon, who met with a very hearty reception,in e course of a long speech which was full of very interesting and instructive matter, then proceeded to give his experiences in the Mother of Parlia- ments at Westminster. He also gave particulars of the various acts of Parliament, over 60 in number, which had after strenuous work been passed through Parliament and stated that if it had not been for the presence of the Labour party of which he was a member, several of the Acts undoubtedly would have been much weaker in effect on behalf of the artisans and labouring classes than they were now. He paid a tribute to the work of Sir Henry Campbell- Bannerman, the Prime Minister, but did not hesitate to say that there were several other members of the*Cabi net of whom he did not feel so sure. With regard to the Acts passed he specially nomad the Trades Disputes' Act and Workmen's Compensation Act and very naturally spoke with pride of to his own efforts on behalf of the shop assistants whom he had after considerable effort managed to get in:luded and recognised. With reference to future legislation he stated that Old Age Pensions would certainly be placed in the forefront of the Labour programme and pointed out that Old Age Pensions had been proved to be a practical success • in New Zealand, where they had after the system had been in vogue some years, actually raised the pensian from 75. 6d. per week to 10s. per week ; therefore there would be no question of the wealthiest country in the world, viz. England,being able to carry such a scheme with effect. He claimed it would lead to a great reformation in our Poor Law and Workhouse system, which, had proved to have been to some extent a failure. Mr. Seddon touched upon several other matters and after speaking for an hour and 20 minutes, then stated he was prepared to answer any questions that any member of the audience desired to ask, for he did not wish anyone afterwards to say he had not given them an opportunity. No questions were asked.</p>
        <p>A vote of confidence in 1.41 r. Seddon as their repre- sentative, and thanking him for his services and emphasing the great need there was for further Social' Reform, was then moved by Mr. E. Osborne,who after 1 paying a tribute to Mr. Seddon stated that in his opinion nationialisation of railways, canals, Scc., and further extension of the principle of municipalisa- tion would lead to the betterment of the people. The vote of confidenn was seconded by Mr. E. i England who•in the course of his remarks stated that part of the work of the late Government was nothing less than a species of political quackery and that he I rejoiced in the presence of a Labour Party in Parlia- ment, for they had to acknowledge that they had justified their existence, and would act as a spur to the present Government in bringing about Social Reform. The vote of confidence was then put to the meeting and carried with acclamation.—On the motion of Mr Seddon a vote of thanks was passed to the Chairman. MEETING AT PENKETFI. There was a crowded attendance at the Wesleyan Schools, Penketh, on Thursday evening, when Mr. J. A. Seddon addressed his constituents. Mr. Atkin presided. At the outset a vote of confidence in Mr. Seddon was passed on the motion of Mr. J. Foden, seconded by Mr. J. Haley and supported by Councillor Stott. Mr. Seddon replied at length. Referring to the open- ing of Parliament he said that if there was no mention in the King's speech of old-age pensions, it was the intention of the Labour Party to move an amendment that some measure in that direction should be intro- duced. Mr. Seddon then reviewed the work of the past session, The proce6dings were of an enthusiastic character.</p>
      </div>
