      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S TEA MEETING. lOn Monday evening the first annual congregational and society tea meeting and entertainmentin connection with St. Paul's Wesleyan Church, Runcorn, were held in Brunswick-street. There was a large attendance at the tea, the tables being presided over by several of the ladies of the congregation. The number was augmented at the entertainment, when the Rev. T. Galland Hartley presided, and a very excellent miscellaneous concert was given by the following :—Mrs. Wood, the Misses Davenport, Birkett, and Simkin, and Messrs. F. Holt, Pilgrim, Leathwood, Newsll, Johnson, Birkett, Morriss, Kniveton, Taylor, Collinge, and T. N. Johnson. The choir also sang part songs. The conductor was Mr. Robert Yates, and the pa:lllst Mr. Reginald Johnson. During an interval the Chairman delivered an address.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. ALTERATIONS AT THE WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present—Alderman Norman (presiding), Rev. E. R. Nunn, Messrs. T. Baxter, S. Woodward, T. Wright,J. B. Chadwick, C. E. Linaker, jun., T. Wood, P. Collins, W, M. Morris, J. Hallows, J. Whitley, J. Spruce, S.Taylor J. H. Davies, H. M. Davies, A. Broome, G. Egerton Warburton, H. Talbot, A. G. Guest, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assists-it clerk), _W. R. de Prez, T. Cook and J. Pearson (relieving officers) and J. Cunnah (master).</p>
        <p>WORKHOUSE ALTERATIONS.</p>
        <p>Tenders for alteratiors at the workhouse and for the erection of a new maternity ward and nurses' home, were consid&amp;ed and that of Mr. H. Fairclough,Warring- ton, of £1,925 accepted. The tenders submitted were: Mr. C. W. Davenport, Stockton Heath, £2,095; Mr. F. Warburton, Stockton Heath, £2,000 10s, ; Mr. J. Clarke Jones, Frodsham, £1,923 95.; Mr. S. Appleton, North- wich, .22,013 155.; Mr. G. Gleave, Frodsham, £2,493 55.; Mr. J. Littler, Frodsham, £2,402; Mr. J. Dolan, War- rington, £1,940; Mr. James Kirkham, Runcorr, £2,204; Mr. J. E. Garitt, Birkenhead, £2,073.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>COST OF ASSESMENT APPEALS.</head>
        <p>COST OF ASSESMENT APPEALS.</p>
        <p>The Elham union requested the Board to pass the following resolution: That this Board desires to draw I the attention of the Local Government Board to the heavy expense which frequently falls upon the ratepayers when it is found necessary to defend appeals to I Quarter Sessions for reduction of assessments. It being the rule that if even only a fractional part of the reduction claimed is secured such a decision carries with it the whole of the costs of both sides, this Board desires to suggest. (1) That all notices to the Assess- ment Committee of claims for reduction of assessment should contain a statement of the amount of reduction claimed and full information on the grounds on which such claim is based. (2) That the ! appeal from the decision of the Assessment Committee should be, not as now, only to Quarter Sessions, but on the demand of either side shall be taken to a cow taf arbitration composed of one arbi- trator nominated by the appellants and one by the , Assessment Committee sittinc, with an umpire who ! shall be an °Mahal of the Local Government Board, the Idecision of such court to be final and to have the force of a judgment of tha High Court. The scale of fees and remuneration to be fixed by the Local Government Board for arbitrators, advocates and witnesses. The matter was referred to the Assessment Committee.</p>
        <p>Messrs. J. L. Jones and Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun., were appointed delegates to attend the annual poor law conference.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee reported payment in out- relief of £7O to Mr. CJok, ,C3O to Mr. de Prez, and £2.5 to Mr. Pearson. gin RUNCORN PET CY SESSIONS, MONDAY. Before 'Jr. J. o,3kleston (in Vl° chair), and Mr</p>
        <p>W. Ti,nmins.</p>
        <p>! SIMMAX GA.NturNG"...---iiral-t-e-C-- Edwards, Richard ' Bushell, Joseph Hayes, Thomas Rose, Ernest Booth, . Mark Water worth, James Arthur Burgess, and William. IKirby, whose ages ranged between 18 and 14, were summoned for having played cards for money on i the 27th ult. Hayes and Rose pleaded not guilty.— P. C. Barber said he saw all the lads playing a game of , 1 cards called banker for money in a dismantled house near the Fairground. Hayes and Rose were on their kneesin the ring, and he caught them and obtained the other lads' names and addresses.—P.C. Wynne gave corroborative evidence.—Rose elected to be sworn, and I said he was passing through the house to get on the Fairground when the police appeared, and he was knocked down in the rush and captu red.—Martha Hewitt, his aunt, said the.boy had left the house only two minutes.—Haves contended himself with saying that Ihe did not play cards.—Rose was given the benefit of the doubt, and the other defendants were lined 9s. i including costs. . _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>THE LODGER AGAlN.—Henry Payne, Mersey-view, I Weston Point, was summoned for having used obscene language on the 28th ult.—P.C. Bell said he was called to the defendant's house, and found that he and the lodger had been fighting. They were quarelling over I.the wife. Defendant used most disgraceful language to his wife and the lodger.—Defendant said his wife had gone to where the lodger went and neglected her home. He was aggravated to such an extent that he was not aware what language he used.—A fine of ss. was imposed. ALLEGED DESERTION OF CHILDREN.—WiIIiam Gough, a boatman, was again charged with having neglected his children by deserting them.—The Magistrates' Clerk (Mr. F. Johnson) said the case had been several times adjourned. The children were in the Workhouse, and defendant promised to bring them out. He had no accommodation on the boat for them,and the Guardians had now decideid to adopt the children, and there was an order on defecdant to contribute (is. a week toward their maintenance.—Defendant said he had paid the money, but he still desired to bring the children out of the Workhonse.—The Chairman advised him to con- tinue the payment. —On the application of Inspector Rogers, of the 'N.S.P.C.C., the case was withdrawn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON</p>
        <p>STEAM ENGINE MAKERS' SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The members of the a hove of the society held their annual dinner and smoking concert at the club rooms, Prince of Wales Hotel, Earle-street, Earlestown, on Saturday, when there was a large muster of members, which included Mr. Seddon, M.P. Bro. F. U. Dawtry presided and also gave an address. A good concert was enjoyed by the company. Great credit is due to Mr. and Mrs. Naylor for the excellent dinner served by them.</p>
      </div>
