      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF SIR DAVID GAMBLE. END OF A STRENUOUS CAREER.</p>
        <p>St. Helens has this week had to mourn the loss of its most prominent and worthy citizen in the person of Sir David Gamble, Bart., K.C.8., who on Sunday celebrated his eighty-fourth birthday, and during the night following he passed quietly away at his residence, Windlehurst, St. Helens, where he had been lying ill for some weeks. Towards the end of last year he con- tracted a chill, but recovered sufficiently to permit of his making occasional visits to his office, and hopes were entertained that he would soon recover his normal health in spite of the burden of his years. Complica- tions set in, and on lhursday a serious operation was decided upon and carried out with the object of relieving the patient from the pain that he was an Tering. This was successfully achieved by Dr. Thomas, of Liverpool, with Dr. Masson, of St. Helens, but unfortunately, the shock to the system was too great,and Si. David quickly sank and died on the night of his eighty-fourth birthday. Sir David Gamble's life is a striking illustration of the success of energy and vigorous devotion. Whatever he took in hand he pursued until he had achieved the object in view. Simultaneously with his efforts for progress in the domain of commerce, he laboured for the public good, giving St. Helens, the town of his adoption the benefitaal his rare ability in the adminis- tration of its local affairs, and showering upon the inhabitants many splendid benefactions which laid them under a lasting debt of gratitude. He was an ideal citizen, with a single eye to the common good. Mr. David Gamble was born in Dublin on Fetraary 3rd, 1823. He was created a Companion of the Bath in 187 a baronet of the United Kingdom in June, 1897, and a K.G.8., a little over a year ago. Sir David_ was of a generous disposition to those with whom he was immediately connected, and on his eightieth birtheay, in 1903, he made his office staff and everybody connectal with his estate handsome presents as" a thank-ofrering for a long life." Mr. Gamble came to Liverpool in 1828, and a few months later settled in St. Helens where he joined the father of Mr. E. K. Muspratt. In St. Helens at that time the plate, crown, flint, and bottle glass trades were already established, but the works started by Mr. Gamble and Mr. Muspratt were practically the found- ing of the chemical industry in the borough. After a few years of profitable partnership, Mr. Gamble and Mr. Muspratt separated, Mr. Muspratt to start else- where, while Mr. Gamble remained in St. Helens. The Gerrard's Bridge Works and afterwards the Hardshaw Brook Works were acquired and developed. When the movement was started for the incorporation of the borough of St. Helens and its extension to include Sutton and Parr, which parishes declined to join the Commisioners' district in 1845, Colonel Gamble was foremost in promoting the ideas of the inhabitrnts. His great services were then recognised by the Town Council, which came into haing cn the incorporation of the borough on the sth March, 1868, and he was elected the first Miiyor—a position he has filled four times. He was Mayor from March, 1868, to November, 1870, and on the 14th July, IS7C, he was elected alderman on the resignation of _Alderman Cross. To this position he has been unanimouely re-elected ever since. In 1889 the inhabitants of the town subscribed £1,035 for the pinpase of paying some. tribute to Sir David for his long and faithful services, and in that year he was pre- sent- 1 with a splendid portrait of hirrself, painted by Herkomer, at a cost of 900 guineas. The present- ation was made by Aldeman AUK( 3hnie, at that 'time Deputy-Mayor, who had originated the movement. During this particular year Alder- man J. C. Gamble occupied the mayoral chair. What Sir David did to further the success of the Volunteer movement is well known. Very few towns in Englend have supported the movement more heartily than St. Helens, and the distinguished position the town holds in this matter is very largely owing to the support he gave to it fron. the creation of the Volunteer Forces in 1860. In Queen Victoria's Jubilee year Colonel Gamble received the honour of a Companionship of the Bath in recognition of his services to the Volunteer movement. He retired from the battalion in December, 1887, with the rank of honorary colonel. As a yachtsman he was widely known, end his prolonged and successful com- medoreship.of the Royal Mersey Yacht Club will be affectionately remembered by its membrs. The club was founded in 1814, and Sir David Gamble, who was elected a member in 1858, was its fifth commodore. Sir David was over known as a true friend of educa- tion, and every denomination always rezeived most willing help from him in this matter. Being a tboroush Churchman, he always gave largely to all the parishes ia the town; in fact, his benefactions were ever hand- some. St. Mark's Church, Cowley Hill, was erected in a great measure as a result of his generosity, and in the building of Holy Trinity and St. Peter's, Parr,Sir David made himself responsible for the success of the under- takings. Windle elementary schools, which serve the thickly-populated neighbourhood of Cowley Hill and Gerrard's Bridge, were built by Sir David in 1868, and, with subsequent enlargements, cost over £3,000. These schools ever since their establishment were maintained in a high state of efficiency by the Gamble fa.nily until they were taken over by the Corporation. He was one of the promoters of Cowley Middle-Class Schools,which were a great boon to the town, and have been extremely successful in educating capable chemists and glass- makers. The crowning act of Sir David's public career was the building and presenting to the town of the Gamble Institute. This huge building, with the land rid furniture, cost £35,000, and was opened by the Earl of Derby on November sth, 1696. It at once placed St. He'ens in possession of almost the finest and best- equipped free library and technical school in the kingdom. He was created a baronet in June, 1897, on the occasion of Queen Victoria's diamond jubilee, but still con- tinued to devote himself to the public work of the town as closely and whole-heartedly as ever. Sir David was appoint ad a county magistrate on July 11th, 1865. He was for a long period a liberal supporter ef the Lancashire Agricultural SoClety, and made himself practically responsible for the success of the show which was held at St. Helens in 1901. He was also almost the oldest membar of the Royal Agricultural Society of England, his election dating back to 1850. As a polititian, Sir David never cared much for Parlia- mentary honours. He was a Liberal and was president of St. Helens Liberal Unionist Association from its formation until the last annual meeting,when Sir David resigned, his Free Trade principles preventing him following Mr. Chamberlain who posed as the leader of the Liberal Unionists. After the last redistribution of Parliamentary seats, and St. Helens for the first time was given a representative at Westminster, he was induced to come forward as the first Liberal candidate for the borough against Mr. Seton-Karr. He was defeated by fifty-seven votes in a poll of 7,413, and "retired apparently without a sigh." On the 26th January, 1817, Sir David married Miss Elizabeth Haddock, daughter of Mr. Thomas Haddock, colliery proprietor,and there were eleven children. The eldest son is Mr. Josias Christopher Gamble, an alder- man, ex-Mayor, justice of the peace, and freeman of the borough of St. Helens, and who succeeds to the baronetcy. - RAILWAY COLLISION AT Si'. HELENS.</p>
        <p>About 11.15 on Tuesday morning a collision occurred at St. Helens Station. Bernard Victory, engine driver, of Chatsworth-street, Edge-hill, was taking his train from the station to the goods yard when another goods train was being shunted across the main line. The train driven by Victory crashed through the centre of the other train and ca,ried some of the waggons in front of it. The train was going down the bank from Thatto Heath at a good speed, and the driver found it impossible to pull up. It continued for a distance of forty or fifty yards before it came to a standstill in the goods yard. The driver and the fireman jumped from the engine, the former sustaining ft fractured collarbone. He was attended by Dr. Bassett, who ordered his removal to the Providence Hospital. Considerable damage was done to the waggons and goods, and the engine driven by Victory was badly smashed in front. A breakdown gall% was soon on the scene, and in a couple of hours the line was cleared.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>FATAL TRAMWAY ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>FATAL TRAMWAY ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>A shocking accident happened in Robins-lane, St. Helens, on Saturday afternoon, to a child named Raymond Ralphson, who was struck by an electric tramcar, and had his neck broken and his head terribly injured. The child was only one year and eight months old, and the son of Henry Ralphson, labourer, of 117, Robins-lane. At five minutes past four, while the child's father was standing at his own door he heard a shout, and on looking into the road saw his little son going across in front of an approaching tramcar driven by Joseph Hough. The father dashed out to try and save the child, but was too late, and it was dragged some little distance. The father received slight injuries, and was naturally terribly upset when the child's body was recovered to find that it had been killed in a most horrible fasbion.—On Saturday after- noon two children named Roberts were knocked down by a tramcar in Higher Parr-street at the junction of Findson-street, but they were fortunate to escape with a few bruises.</p>
        <p>Prevailing Modes.—The panel is playing a very. important part in the world of dress,so also is thlbleg-o mutton sleeve. The former notion adds to theleight and slender appearance of the wearer, and so long as the latter is allowed to fall gracefully and softly . with- out stiffening of any kind, this also is quaint, and iinsually becoming. So far there is little fear of the huge inflated sleeve becoming pop ular, and none what- ever of the crinoline appearing once again. Twice a year, in the spring and autumn usually—this bogey is trotted out, but it is soon trotted back again for another six months' seclusion. For useful coats and skirts, striped grey and white tweeds are to the fora, than which nothing looks nicer, while for dressy gowns and wraps, almond pink, pastel blues, pastel greys, and almond greens, are the chosen colours.</p>
      </div>
