      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>ia.</head>
        <p>ia.</p>
        <p>WHITECROSS WARD ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Liberal Victory.</p>
        <p>The election to fill the vacancy in the representation of Whitecross Ward on the Council resulted on Monday, as was generally Axpected, in the victory of the Liberal nominee, Mr. Joseph Hawthorn, a well-known journalist, of Lovely-lane. The contest waq a short and sharp one, as it was not until the eleventh hour that the Conservatives decided to oppose Mr. Hawthorn and brought forward Mr. James Broadhurst, blacksmith, of Liverpool-road. Both gentlemen entered with spirit into the contest, which, whilst conducted energetically was fought in ths best possible spirit. For many years the Ward has consistently returned Liberals to represent it on the Council, and the Tories brought all their powers to bear in a determined but unsuccess- ful effort to gain the seat. The Ward numberef some ' 1,400 voters and these were energetically canvassed by both sides but the poll was only a light one. The result was declared from the Town Hall shortly after eight o'clock and was:</p>
        <p>Hawthorn 535 Broadhurst</p>
        <p>Majority</p>
        <p>Whilst the majority was regarded by the successful candidate and his workers as satisfactory the Liberal vote showed a considerable decrease, largely accounted for by the fact that confident in their ultimate success many Liberal electors refrained from visiting the polling booth. It is unnecessary to point the moral to be drawn from the figures and we would urge that in future contests' all Liberals should malie a point of recording their vote. At the conclusion of the counting a vote of thanks was, on the proposition of Councillor HAWTHORN, seconded by Mr. BROADHuRsT, passed to Alderman Pierpoint (the presiding officer), and the officials who had conducted the election. Alderman PIERPOINT announced the result from the Town Hall steps to a fairly large crowd. Councillor HAWTHORN said the fight had been a short and a close one. He thanked every man connected with the Liberal party in Whitecross Ward who had endeavoured to do his duty that day. They had main- tained Whitecross Ward as a Liberal stronghold against the strongest opposition which the Conservatives had been able to bring against them. He thanked all his workers and all who had voted for him, and he hoped his conduct in the Council would merit their approval. He would endeavour to serve them faithfully and well.</p>
        <p>Mr. BROADHURST said he thanked those who had voted for him for recognizing a fellow-worker. He had not been successful, bat he was - defeated man, because he stood in the same posit:a as he did before. Mr. Hawthorn was a gentleman of the first water, and be (Mr. Broadhurst) had a great respect for him for the way in which the contest had been fought. The organization of the contest on the Liberal side was, of course, in the hands of Mr. J. Howes. jun., the Liberal agent, to whom and to the friends of the candidate much of the credit for a successful issue is due.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>FUNERAL OF THE LATE MR. W. R. NEIL.</head>
        <p>FUNERAL OF THE LATE MR. W. R. NEIL.</p>
        <p>Impressive Ceremony at Grappenhall. The funeral of the late Mr. William Russell Neil which took place at Gmppenhall on Monday, was the occasion of a striking demonstration of respect. The deceased gentleman, who was a director of Messrs. -Fletcher, Russell and Co., Limited, was everywhere held in the highest esteem and there was a large gather- ing at Monday's ceremony. The works of Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co., were closed for the day and it was a tribute of the popularity of Mr. Neil that the majority of the workpeople attended to witness the last rites. A short service was held at" Waverley " conducted by the Revs. J. L. Milne and T. Mackay, Pendleton, after which the cortege left for Grappenhail Church. The chief mourners were :—Master Willie Nell (eldest son), Messrs. Alexander Neil (father),John, Archibald A., and Alec C. Neil (brothers), T. A. Riley, Eccles (brother-in-law), William Russell, sen., Swißton; Alex. C. Russell, Eccles; John Clarke, Pendleton, and James Smith, Derby (uncles) ; W. Russell,jun., Pendle- ton ; W. A. Russell, Swinton ; R. and A. Morrison, Pendleton; D. Morrison, Warrington ; Fergus C. and' Alec. Russell, jun., Eccles ; James S. Russell, Swinton; and Henry L. Morley, Bristol (cousins), Dr. Leadbetter, Dr. Parker, Rev. J. L. Milne and Rev. T. Mackay. Amongst others present were representatives of the William Football Club, Masonic Lodges, and a large number of friends of the deceased gentleman. An impressive service was conducted in the church by the rector (the Rev. H. V. Pigot) who also officiated at the graveside. Mr. A. Woods, the organist, gave an effective rendering of the Dead March in Saul. A great many beautiful floral tributes were placed over the grave. The funeral arrangements were carried out by Mr. H. F. Greenough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>ra-mg</head>
        <p>ra-mg</p>
        <p>CATHOLIC " SOCIAL " AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The annual tea and dance in connectton with St. Mary's R.C. Church, Widnes, was held in the Drill Hall on Monday evening, over 500 being present. An admirably served tea was presided over by the ladies of the congregation. The hall had been charmingly becorated by Mr. James Kiddie's staff, the platform deing arranged to represent a wooded grove. Mr. Alf Barber's orchestra played a programme of 16 dances after tea, and a larg6 number took the opportunity of "tripping the light fantastic." A large number of M.C.'s worked hard for the pleasure of all. Alderman S. Qnind was present during a portion of the evening. Mr. John Williamson made an admirable hon. secretary.</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S MEN'S BIBLE CLASS. An organ recital was given in St. John's Chureh, Earlestown, on Sunday afternoon by Mr. E. Leyland, asissted by the following artistes :—Mr. J. Mather (tenor), Mr. C. El. Lea (baritone), an a male voice party. The organist played in good style the following pieces : " Darnley " and " Aurelia," variations on a hymn tune, Melody in C (West), Toccatta in G (Dubois) ; solo, by request, " The abbey chime" (Lyle) Prelude and Fugue in E Minor (Bach), Trumpet March (Jude). The organitt and singers gave the congrega- tion a musical treat. The collections will be devoted to the curate's fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>nra</head>
        <p>nra</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 9, 1907.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED NEW BYE-LAMS. Epidemic of Measles.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Bewsey Chambers on Wednesday when there were present: Mr. A. Brooks (presiding), Dr. Nlurray,Messrs. G. Mather, G. Thompson, W. Houghton. J. Backhouse, E'. Longton, J. Ball, R. Forshaw, J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sato,: (assistant clerk), F. W. tierfoot (inspector), B. S. Newcombe (surveyor), H. El. Burnage (assistant surveyor), and Dr. Sephton (medical officer.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>COUNTY COUNCIL GRANTS.</head>
        <p>COUNTY COUNCIL GRANTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. NEWCOMBE reported that the Council hal expended .C 3,212 lie. sd. on behalf of the County Council, in respect of road work. The County Council still owed £2,050 but this would be paid within the course of the next few weeks. The County Council had also made a special grant of £lOO towards the cost of macadamising Croft secondary roads.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>WARBURTON BRIDGE</head>
        <p>WARBURTON BRIDGE</p>
        <p>Mr. NEWCOMBE reported that he had had a confer- ence with the Surveyor of the Bucirlow District Coun- cil in respect to the proposal that the two authorities should take over Warburton Bridge and abolish the tolls. They were quite agreed and it now remained for the joint committee to consider the matter and make a proposal to the Manchester Ship Canal Company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>LIGHTING OP VirINWECK-ROAD.</head>
        <p>THE LIGHTING OP VirINWECK-ROAD.</p>
        <p>The CLERK said he had received a communication from the Winwick Parish Council in respect of the pro- posal that the Winwick main road should be lighted, The Parish Council had resolved "that as no applioa- tion in favour of lighting the road had been received from any resident in the parish or neighbourhood it is not desirable at present to increase the rates as we have cause to know lately of the very largo increase." The overseers of the parish had also written: "We have been waited upon by many influencial ratepayers pro- testing against the expense of lighting the road and therefore respectfully ask the Council to postpone the matter for the present." Mr. LO'SGTOV said the proposal was unnecessary. Other roads wanted lighting more badly than the Winwick-road. It was only the As3lum Authorities who wanted the work doing. The question was adjourned for the attendance of Mr. J. W. Knowles who first introduced it to the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>EPIDEMIC OF MEASLES.</head>
        <p>EPIDEMIC OF MEASLES.</p>
        <p>Dr. SP.PWPON reported that an epidemic of measles had broken out at Pa.dgate and he had recommended the closing of the schools for a fortnight. Measles had also broken out at Orford and Great Sankey and in these districts also the schools had been closed.</p>
        <p>Upon a communication from the local education authority the question was raised as to whether the schools ought to he closed before the consent of two members of the Council had been obtained. Dr. SerwroN said that for 30 years he had been in the habit of closing the schools whenever he thought it necessary. Now he had to report the matter to the Clerk to the Council and then get the signatures of two members of the Council. This involved a delay which they could not afford in the case of a severe epidemic. Mr. HOUGHTON thought the order, as it meant a delay of two or three days, was preposterous and absurd.</p>
        <p>Dr. SEPHToN reported that for the month ending January 2nd, 29 births and 17 derails had been reported. The death rate was equal to 17 per 1,000 as against 25, the average of the past three montis and 29'1 for the corresponding period of last year. Fourteen of the deaths occurred at the County Asylum excluding which the death rate propPr for the district was 301 per 1000. That was the lowest estimate he had ever had the pleasure of reporting. Only one case of infectious disease was notified. (Eh ar, hear.) For the month end- ing February 6th there had been 35 births and 39 deaths. Ihe death rate was equal to 39 per 1000, and as against 22, the average of the past three months. and 82.3 for the corresponding period of last year. Fifteen deaths Recurred at the County Asylum excluding which the death rate proper for the district was equal to 24'9 per 1,000. Dr. Sephton asked for the instructions of the Council in regard to closing the schools. It was decided to conform to the requirements of the Education Committee.</p>
        <p>In reply to Mr. Mather, Dr. SENITON said he could not accept responsibility for epidemics of any air-borne disease such as measles. Measles was not a notifi- able disease and he did not think much could be done if it were made notifiable as parents frequently refrained from calling in a medical man. In respect to Hollins Green, the district pentioned by Mr. Mather, there was there a mixed community. They had people there from Cadishead, Glazebrook, and Manchester, and infection might be introduced from those places. During the past four months there had not been a single death in the district with a population of 11,900 from an infectious disease. (Hear, hear). PROPOSED ALTERATIONS OF THE BYE-LAWS. The CRAIRMAN reported that he and Mr. Newcombe had gone very carefully through the model bye-laws of the Local Government Board and of two or three adjoin- ing authorities, but it was to the model bye-laws that he desired to draw attention. They made a very import- ant departure from the old idea 9f building the same class of house in a rural district as was built in a town. In the model bye-laws, assuming there were good foun- dations and proper sanitary arrangements, a builder was allowed to use less expensive material, in fact he might build with wood provided there was plenty of land round. He did riot think himself that there was nay great objection to wooden or corrugated iron buildings. Mr. Newcombe and most surveyors loolced upon that sort.'of thing with horror, but he thought Mr. Newcombe had come round to believe that there was something feasible in the idea of cheap houses for farm labourers. There was nothing very terrible about a wooden house, and he could speak from experience that they were very comfortable.</p>
        <p>It was decided to include this regulation in the new bye-laws which are being framed for the Council. BRIDGE WIDENING.</p>
        <p>The SURVIIYO4 reported that he had applied to the County Council for a special grant in aid of the cost of widening of Mary Ann's Bridge. Mr. Crompton Hall was greatly in favour of the proposal. He had repre- sented strongly the matter of Sankey Ltridge to the County. Mr. Hall promised to take the question up and asked him to strengthen his hands by taking a census 'ofltraffic there for one week' This had been done and the return was of interest :—Heavy 2-wheeled carts, 495; Lurries, 1234; Heavy motors and engines, 36; cycles, 1601; other traffic, buses, vans,ecc., 1391 ; motor cars, 1132; total of all traffic, 4072. The Corporation of Warrington would be approached with a view to the jointanthorities negotiating with the London and North-Western Railway Company.</p>
        <p>UNABLE TO STANDALONE. Spinal Paralysis. Physical Weakness, Loss of Flesh. Another Extraordinary Cure ' DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS. Mrs. WEEKLEY, Wigtoft, ar Boston, Lincs. writes : For a long time my son iffered from Spinal Weakness. [e lost so much flesh that he ias nothing but skin and bone. We had several doctors but ley all said there was no cure. Was always in pain, and MIS carried home twice since last Christmas. Many; times he could not 'stand alone, anyl was in bed for seven weeks. He has taken your Tablets a , few weeks and they have acted wonderfully. He can get up and dress himself, and last Sunday he went to Mn. WEEKLEY. church for the first time in 10 weeks. He has gained flesh and strength rapidly. He has walked in the village daily and everyone is amazed at the good the remedy has done. DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS will cure you as they cured Mr. Weekley. They create healthy flesh, strength and vitality as no other medicine ever did, and they cure the most pronounced cases of LEANNESS OF BODY WASTING DISEASES GENERAL WEAKNESS ANEMIA WRECKED NERVES INDIGESTION PARALYSIS OF NERVES MAL-NUTRITION LOST OF FLESH KIDNEY WEAKNESS and all diseases arising from physical and nerve exhaus- tion. We could no more explain the marvellous action of this extraordinary tablet than you can explain the chemical phenomena which produce electricity. It is the tablet everybody requires, leid. and 2s. 9d. at all Chemists and Drug Stores everywhere, or post free from the famous Dr. Cassell's Co. Ltd., King Street West, Manchester. Advice free by letter.</p>
        <p>BY. .. ne, " .. •;'. ~.... Wi ,flop o_., .• / / 1- H s':•:, ( V i,•:::...4. th, W r•;•:•: ',: ' . '::::4/ r'' If .',\\ 1.'::.... '.• 1,, ' '\''' . &gt;'/I X . • -/ '''' •7:: Ali</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. , ,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. , ,</p>
        <p>The Cost of Deputations.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday under the presid- enoy of Mr. J. Billinge. There were also present : Mrs. Clay, Mrs. Wilkinson, Miss Broadbent, Dr. Murray, Messrs. W. Hickman, A. J. Jolley, R. Forshaw, S. Davies J. Payton, T. H. Ashcroft. G. Thompson, A. Brooks,' W. Houghton, J. Backhouse, W. C. Brooks, W. Flannagan, T. Ritchie, J. C. Sutton (clerk), J. F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superintendent of Padgate Schools).</p>
        <p>' THE ILLNESS OF CHILDREN AT PADGATE. At a meeting of the Cottage Homes Committee a report was submitted from Mr. J. Carter Bell, county analyst, on the sample of cheese which was suspected to be the cause of sickness among the children at Christmas. The Superintendent was directed to send a copy of the analysis to the contractors who supplied the cheese, and invite their observations thereon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>LECTURES TO NURSES.</head>
        <p>LECTURES TO NURSES.</p>
        <p>The House Committee resolved that the doctor should give lectures twice weekly to the senior nurses and once to the juniors, supplemented with lectures bY the suporintemdent nurse on practical nursing as hitherto. The course of instruction will extend from October to May. This was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>SUGGESTED EVENING MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>SUGGESTED EVENING MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>Letters were read on behalf of the Warrington Labour Representation Committee, the Trades and Labour Council, and the Independent Labour Nay forwarding resolutions to the effect thatas personal attendance is necessary when making application for relief and also to make it possible for workingmen to attend as Guardians, it NV as desirable that meetings of the Board should be held in the evening . _ .</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said the nlatter would be one for the newly-elected Board to consider, as the retiring repre- sentatives would go out of office in April. lle did not think it would be right to commit the new Board. Mrs. WILKINSON moved that the question be con- sidered at th 9 next Board meeting. Mr. ASHCROFT said that probably the object of the request was to bring forward more Labour candidates at the forthcoming elections, if they could be assured that the meetings would be held in the evening.</p>
        <p>Mr. HOUGHTON said it would be impossible for many applicants for relief to attend in the evening, particu- larly during winter, as some had to come several miles. After further discussion the matter was allowed to drop.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>COST OF DEPUTATIONS.</head>
        <p>THE COST OF DEPUTATIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Bolton had given notice to move that a return of Ideputations in the year 1906 be made as follows :—(1) Of journeys by guardians and offlcera, (2) of the place, , (3) the purpose, (4) the names of the persons forming each deputation, and(s) the expense of each such journey as charged in the accounts. In the absence of Mr. Bolton, who had met with a slight accident on his way to the meeting, Mr. BROOKS moved the resolution and Mr. ASHCROFT seconded. The CLERK submitted a long statement which he had prepared. He pointed out that the annual cost of deputations to the Central Poor Law Conferenee 'End the meetings of the Association of Poor Law Unions was 25 guineas. Tn 1906, however, there was a special additional Central Poor Law Conference on the subject of legiilation affecting children and therefore the cost of the deputations to the two organizations came to the•exceptional total of 45 guineas. There were two meetings each year of the district conference and in 1906 the cost of deputations, consisting of the chairman of the Board, the chairman and vice-chairman of the Padgate Schools Committee and the clerk, was £2 65., fora one day conference and £5 4s.fora two days'confer- ence, making a total of £7 10s. It was the duty of the Beard annually to appoint a committee to visit the lunatic asylums at which patients were maintained at their cost. Usually three guardians visited along with the Clerk. The County Asylum and the Royal Albert Asylum, Lancaster, were so visited and each of the insane persons chargeable to the Board was seen by Messrs. Cook and Plannagan and the Clerk in September, 1901 The cost including conveyances was £3 13s. 6d. Prestwich Asylum was visited in October by Messrs. Bent, Payton, Ritchie, and the Clerk, at a cost of £2 10s. The total number of lunatics chargeable to the Union in 1906 approached 220. Of these the far greater number were at Rainhill and Winwick. Messrs. Bolton, Seton,Jolley, Davies, and Mrs. Wilkinson visited Rainhill, and Messrs. Bent, Brooks, Mather, Payton, and Bolton visited Winwick accompanied by Mr. Shearer as inquiry officer and the Clerk. The total expense, including convey- ances, hotels, &amp;c., for visits to the two institutions and interviewing each inmate chargeable, was £4 15s. sd. A very important meeting was held at Preston in con- nection with the Lancashire Asylums Board and the Chairman and the Clerk represented.the Board at a cost of 16s. each. At an equally important meeting of the Lancashire and Cheshire Guardians held at rhorlton, the Board was similarly represented, the expenses being 11s. 6d. each. In connection with Padgate Cottage Homes, visits to Roman Catholic institutions at Holy- well and Birmingham had cost £5 16s. 4d. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. ASHCROFT': Are those all the deputations for 1906 ?</p>
        <p>The CLERK: All that I know of. It is my rule to give every information and conceal nothing. Mr. Asiacuorr : There have been deputations from the House Committee. The CLERK: I cannot answer for those. I must leave them to Mr. Axvbery.</p>
        <p>Afr.Bormmwho arrived while the clerk was speaking, said he bad no personal feeling against anyone in the matter.lt was merely with a view to trying to keep the expenses down as much as possible that he introduced the matter. He thought that too many members had been included in some deputations. For instance, he under- stood five members in addition to the doctor went to purchase some instruments, when only the doctor knew anything about them. (Laughter.) He was aware that the Douse Committee had power to appointed deputations, but he did not think it had any right to do so without the consent of the Board. The members of the Board hardly ever got to know that they had been and what they had done. The Board ought to be taken into the confidence of the eommittee. Then again in many instances, sub-committees were formed on the principle of "You propose me and I'll propose you." (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>. , Mr. HICKMAN said Mr. Bolton's remarks did not apply to the deputations mentioned by the Clerk as the Board had appointed all these. He had no doubt that information would be forthcoming as to deputations from the House Committee. Mr. JoLLEY said he thought that in view of the offensive insinuations made by Mr. Bolton, his suggestion that he was not animated by personal motives was a little far-fetched. He had macie sugges- tions which were grossly misleading and offensive and which were not in any way based on facts. The master Ix'as prepared to supply the actual expenditure in every case of deputations from the House Committee, and he (Mr. Jolley) moved that Mr. Awbery be now requested to submit to the Board a return showing the exact figures. He had found out from experience that the best way to meet a falsehood was to give the simple truth.</p>
        <p>Mr. !Timm AN seconded, and the Board approved the proposition. Mr. AWBERY thereupon reported that deputations which emanated from the House Committee did not really take place until the Board had approve.] the recommendations of the committee. During 1906 he had only paid expenses on two occasions. On January sth Messts. Mather, Flannagan and Thompson were appointed by the House Committee and authorised by the Board to visit Prescot and Burnley Unions with reference to the appointment of a resident medical officer, and the expenses amounted to £4 is. 2d. On June 19th Mr. Mather and himself incurred expenses in pig-purchasing to the amount of £1 18s. 9d. Those were the only sums he had paid out a petty cash for deputations. The statement as to the purchase of the doctor's inatruments was true, but those who went on the deputation did not ask him or the Clerk for their expenses. _ _ _</p>
        <p>• Mr. FORSHAW : Surely there are more expenses somewhere. This matter seems to be fizzling out. (Laughter.) The matter was allowed to drop withoutefurther comment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>ME. ASFICROFT'S PROTEST.</head>
        <p>ME. ASFICROFT'S PROTEST.</p>
        <p>The agenda contained the folloving notice :—" The Board Meeting on Wednesday, February 20th, by reason of the absence at the Central Poor Law Conference of the Chairman, Vice-Chairman, and Clerk will not be special. The proceedings thereat will be lin: ited to relief and necessary routine matters."</p>
        <p>Mr. Asucßorr 'considered it ridiculous that the Board could not transact its full business when the Chairman and Clerk were away. The Board could do without them. (Laughter). The CHAIRMAN: I daresav if you will wait a little longer we shall both be out of the way. A proposition by Mr. BROOKS that the Standing Order be adhered to and the meeting be a " special "one was defeated. THE DONATION TO THE TKFTRMARY.</p>
        <p>The edii,Woliii-P-I‘Wor:Lai Officers' J ournal "wrote calling attention to the fact that it was recently reported that the Local Government Board had sanctioned the payment of a contribution of £lOO by the Board toThe building fund of the Warrington Infirmary. He would be obliged if the clerk would let him know whether the report was correct as on previous occasions the Board had ruled that Guardians were not empowered to subscribe to such funds and the inform- ation would bp of much value to many other unions. The CLXIIK replied that £lOO had been subscribed by inereasidg thee annual donation to that amount for this year only.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday morning, while working on the top of the tower of St. Thomas's Church, Park-lane, Liverpool James Fordham, a labourer, fell to the ground, a distance of over 50 feet, and was killed. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Justice Jelf, at the Flintshire Assizes, said that the county was the sixth in succession in which be bad been bolding assizes euring the last three weeks, and in all the number of criminal cases amounted only to deVAD. A man's headless body has been washed ashore at Chapel St. Leonards, on the Lincolnshire coast. The only clothing on it was a sock, and there was nothing to lead to indentification. ala</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Heartburn ?</head>
        <p>Heartburn ?</p>
        <p>MR. JOE HUTCHINS. 16, Railway Row, Dudley, R. 5.0., Colliery, Northumberlane.</p>
        <p>"I want to tell you that your Iron-Ox Tablets have completely removed the, severe indigestion from which I suffered for a long time. I used to have terrible attacks of heartburn, accompanied by severe pain in the side and chest. I also suffered from nausea. My appetite was very bad, and gener- ally I was in a miserable way. I read in one of the papers that your Tablets could cure indigestion, and I got a box. I must say they have done far more than I expected. The heartburn and pain have quite disappeared. I digest my food easily, my appetite is much improved, and I feel much stronger." (Signed) JOE HUTCHINS.</p>
        <p>One point in this case which you must not over- look is the fact that Iron-Ox Tablets have made Mr. Hutchins much stronger, after removing the heart- burn, pain and nausea which were caused by indigestion. In the first place Iron-Ox Tablets so strengthened Mr. Hutnhins' digestive organs that he was able to digest his food naturally, and conse- quently the pain and the annoyance ceased. Then they brought back his appetite, and finally they gave him new strength and energy. To-day Mr. Hutchins is a strong, free man—free from pain— free from nausea—free from indigestion. And it was Iron-Ox Tablets that made him so.</p>
        <p>Mv DEAR NIECES AND NEPHEWS,- 4 Last week, when I wrote my letter to you, I was in a very dismal state through that cold which I told you about. This week, lam pleased to say that Uncle Ned's himself again. Gone is that lukewarm, watery gruel; gone, that true and trusty hot-water bottle (the last day it leaked); gone' that awful cold—and gone are three more bottles of Mason's Extract, whilst about eight more are waiting to- go. Who'll write for them? This week, my worthy rela- tives, Uncle Ned has to tender a sincere apology for his omission of Nelly Shenton's name from the ever-growing C.C.L. With all his heart, your relative craves Nelly's forgiveness for this carelessness.</p>
        <p>I expect that this sudden nip of frost • has began to remind you of our Clothing Fund for Needy Mites. Just as I am penning these lines the postman brings me a delightful creation— (is that the word ?)—in green and white, with a blob on top. Guess what it is. Can't? Well, it's a jolly nice, warm Tam o' Shanter cap, and I'll guarantee that the heart of the wee boy or girl who wears it will surely bubble over with pride. Sweet; thanks to the sender, Alice L. Well, I will not take up your time any longer, but ask you, in the words of the penny s.howman, to "pass on to the next caravan "—the efforts of 'our little contributors. UNCLE NZD.</p>
        <p>WELCOME TO Nelly Shenton, Harry Tweedale, Dolly Twee dale, Harold Hall.</p>
