      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>REPLIES TO CONTRIBUTORS.</head>
        <p>REPLIES TO CONTRIBUTORS.</p>
        <p>HILDA B.—The delay is on the Post-office's part. I expect, Hilda, perhaps it has arrived by this time. Thanks for reminding me. SAM S.—l hope the bird is much better now, poor thing. No, I don't know the address of any man who sells canary medicine. WALTER Y:—No, it's not illpgal to send a donkey away in a piano case, providing it 4as room to breathe and wag its tail. Hope it will arrive safe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <p>__+_</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l expect you will think I have forgotten you, but I have not. lam send- ing you a short story, about " Saved by a Cat." I should very much like to see this piece in print, and hope to gain a prize. I read the pieces weekly. I must now close my letter, wishing yeu and all my cousins a happy New Year. With love, from</p>
        <p>. WINNIE ELLIOTT. SAVED BY A CAT.</p>
        <p>A travelling menagerie was once going from .Worcester to s neighbouring town. On thei journey, three little wolves escaped from their cage, and, though there was a great hunt after them, only twg were found. A large wood was near, into which the third one escaped, and hid himself.</p>
        <p>A few days later, driven by hunger, the wolf wept to a cottage neat by for food. The owners were out at work; only the baby was left, asleep in its cradle. The wolf at once made for the cradle; but the cat that was in the room was too quick for him. It sprang on the intruder, and a desperate fight began in the midst of which the baby woke, and began to cry loudly. The mother came rushing in, and saw the cat trying to tear the wolf's eyes out, thus prevent- ing him from attacking the baby. A few blows from a stout stick, brought in by a neighbour, coon killed the wolf, so the baby's life was saved by its pet cat.</p>
        <p>Dear ITnole Ned.—l am sending you a piece of poctry, and a riddle, .whiola I hope to see in print. It its called "THE CAT'S DISAPPOINTMENT."</p>
        <p>A cat caught a sparrow, and was about to de- our it, when the sparrow said, "No gentle/min eats till he washes his face." The cat, struck with this remark, let the sparrow down, and began to wash his face with his paw; but the sparrow flew away. This vexed puss extremely, and he said, "As long as I live, I will eat first and wash my face afterwards"—which all cats do to this day.</p>
        <p>_ RIDDLE. Can you tell what went to South Africa, and stopped there, and was sent back because it never went there? From your loving Niece, FLORENCE STEVENS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>..me••••=••••4•••••.m....•</head>
        <p>..me••••=••••4•••••.m....•</p>
        <p>Teddy: "I wish I hadn't licked Jimmy All- sorts this morning." Mamma: "You see now how wrong it was, don't you, dear ?" Teddy: "Yes, 'cause I didn't know till after- wards that his mother was going to give a children's party."</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am sending you sow lokes for the "Corner," and I should like to see them in print : Domestic : "There's a gentleman wants to see yer on business."</p>
        <p>Master: "Well, ask him to take a chair." Domestic: "He's taking 'em all, and the table, too. He comes from the furniture shop." Two young men, belonging to Gateshead, were discovered trespassing in the grounds at Ravensworth. One of them escaped, but the other was caught by the gamekeeper. On being asked his name, the culprit replied, " Wey, ma nyem's Broon." "What is the name of the fellow who has just run away ?" "Oh, he's ma brother, but Aa'm not ganin to tell thoo his nyeme 1"</p>
        <p>An. Irish landlord, passing through a village, said to the local butcher, "Well, Jim, how's trade ?" "Bad, yer honner," said Jim. "The people are so few and so poor here, that it's hard pushed I am to dispose of a carcase be- fore it gets tainted." "Why not kill half a cow at a time?" suggested the 'Squire. A Sunday School teacher, it is said, remon- strated with one of the boys in her class whom she had discovered in the act of taking the eggs from a bird's nest. "Think how the poor mother-bird will feel 1" she said. " Ho-hol" laughed the boy, "You've got the mother-bird on your hat! I 'apect she wont feel very bad !" Jack's Revenge.—A bluejacket, on a visit to London, observed an old lady standing at the edge of the pavement waiting to cross. Three times she essayed to cross, but retreated, frightened by the traffic. Then Jack, with char- acteristic good nature, took her up in his muscular arms, and carried her across the street. When he set her down, the old lady was almost speechless with indignation and was proceeding to give him a piece of her mind, when Jack, with perfect composure, took her up in his arms again, and, amidst the boisterous laughter of. the crowd, carried her back to, the other side of the street.</p>
        <p>I remain, your affectionte Niece, MARGARET LILIAN PAYNE</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am sending you two pieces of poetry, called, "A Little Girl's Plaint," and " Stand in the Sunshine.- I hope to see these in print soon. With every success to your "Corner," I remain, your loving Niece, ALICE RIVETT. A LITTLE GIRL'S PLAINT. My brother Will, he used to be The Woo:It kind of girl; He wore a little dress like me, And had his hair in curl. We played with dolls and tea-sets then, And every kind of toy; But all those good old times are gone— Will turned into a boy. Mamma has made him lithe suits, With pockets in the pants; And cut off all his yellow curls, And sent them to my aunt's. And Will, he was so pleased, I bilieve, He almost jumped for joy; But I must own I didn't like Will turned into a boy. And now he plays with horrid tops I don't know how to spin, And marbles that he tries to shoot, But never hits nor win; And leap-frog—l can't give a "back" Like Charlie, Frank, or Roy. Oh, no one knows how bad I feel, Since Will has turned a boy. I have to wear frocks just the same, And now they're mostly white; I have to sit. and just ,be good, While Will can climb and fight. But I must keep my dpeesee nice, And wear my hair in curl, And worse—Oh, worst thing of all— I have to stay a girl! STAND IN THE SUNSHINE. Stand in the sunshine sweet, And treasure every ray, Nor seek with stubborn feet The darksome way. Have courage! keep good cheer! Our longest time is brief. To those who hold you dear • Bring no more grief. But cherish blisses Grateful for least delight That to your lot cloth fall, However slight. And 10l all hearts will bring Love, to make glad your day; Blessings untold will spring ' About your way.</p>
        <p>Uncle: "Well, Bobby, what did you learn at school to-day?" Bobby: "I learned that the world is round, and turns on hinges, like that globe in the library." Uncle: "Well, what do you think of that?" Bobby: "I think, uncle, they are asking m 6 to believe a good deal for a small boy."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>WAITING.</head>
        <p>WAITING.</p>
        <p>In London town the streets tre gay, And crowds go quickly by; • It is a glorious summer day, But I sit here and sigh. The pavement's hot, my feet are sore, Yet I must Wait outside the door.</p>
      </div>
