      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 9, 1907.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>WOMAN'S REALM.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS- FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>THE MODISTES' BALI.</p>
        <p>One of the most delightful evenings in Paris</p>
        <p>during the winter season, a Paris correspondent writes, is the modistes' ball. Those charming young women who, the other part of the year, are engaged in fabricating the mode for other people wear this evening their own creations. The effect is most radiant and exhilarating. All the prettiness .of the Rue de la Paix is there, dressed in its brightest, gayest chiffons, twisted and contrived by nimble fingers and clever brains. The "mannequin," or living model in the dressmaking establishment, is always a very delightful young person—at least to look upon— with a superb figure and a carriage-like a queen. She is there always at the ball with her sisters of the "ateliers." The costume is fancy dress, and much ingenuity and real talent in colours and design is shown in the manufacture of the evening toilets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>MAUVE INDOOR GOWN.</head>
        <p>MAUVE INDOOR GOWN.</p>
        <p>The indoor gown which forms the subject of the sketch is daintily fashioned in thin cashmere in a lovely if somewhat unserviceable shade of mauve. This shade, however, could easily be changed for any fancied by the wearer, and practically any woollen fabric, providing it were not too thick. could be employed in place of the cashmere. The bodice has a folded and draped collar, which entirely covers the shoulders, and finishes in front beneath a knot and ends of the</p>
        <p>material. A narrow silk frill edges the collar all round, and also the scarf ends in front. The sleeves are composed of three graduated puffs, the two higher ones being separated by a cuff-like arrangement pdged with the silk frilling, and the sleeve is finished midway between elbow and wrist with another frill. The skirt is well gored, is treated with an inverted box-pleat at the back, and has a broad crossway band of the material, edged top and bottom with the silk frilling, running along the hem; whilst a vest of lace and a tie of dark velvet complete the gown.</p>
