      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>COURT GoIN xs.</head>
        <p>COURT GoIN xs.</p>
        <p>The announcement of Courts on February 22nd and on March Ist' has set modistes to find out what the plans of fashion as to evening dress will be. Gowns worn at Court are destined to be worn throughout the season; and to be alluded ts.) from time to time by ladies unaccustomed, to attendance as "'My Court gown, don't you know!" There is such a pleasure about uttering the phrase and there are many ladies who can have it now as the years 'come round. The first Court will be strictly diplomatic and official, and will be quite a big affair. I believe, says a writer in the Globe, that now the limit of presentations for both has been reached. So far as at present. transpires soft yet handsome fabrics are to be, a feature of the coming season evening dress. There are new chine brocades on a peculiar make of thick rich crepe-de-chine that have the double merit of falling very gracefully and looking very effective and handsome. Naturally for young girls lighter fabri( s will be in vogue as before. There is also a printed panne which promises to be in much favour. Skirts will be plain and full, and modistes say more trained than they have been. Sleeves will be shorter and the puff on the shoulder. is levelled for this season. A hang- ing effect is considered desirable. The long waist is to give place to the rounded, and the reign of the flat-fronted corset, is said to be over.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>BLOUSE OF ODD LENGTHS.</head>
        <p>BLOUSE OF ODD LENGTHS.</p>
        <p>The blouse shewn in our sketch gives a sug- gestion for the make-up of an odd length of Irish poplin, velvet, or other material procured during sale time. The yoke is embroidered in shaded silks to. tone with the material. The lower part of the blouse is laid in fiat box. pleats all the way round, and the little inner yoke and cuffs are of some transparent material</p>
        <p>closely tuc. -. Even three different materials could be used, providing they all toned with one another. The yoke and embroidered band at the edge A ,uff might be in velvet; the ' or WWl3lan n •-• o.•</p>
        <p>((, •-- iiti..' ,--*, '*•-1:4,1c1i .. % • •'',--- r b • • 4'7/ , .4••-ti '., 'Pf „ ri - / i ( . \ , \ " .Iced.</p>
      </div>
