      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
        <p>The Soldiers' Memorial Fund. Sir,—May I be permitted to place the following facts before your readers, in reference to the application which I made at the request of the Memorial Com- mittee a few days ago to the General Purposes Com- mittee of the Council, and which was declined by a majority? When the cost of the memorial, was first estimated the committee felt certain the Corporation would follow in the present case the precedent acted upon in regard to the Philip Carpenter fountain, the fountain in Queen's Gardens, presented by the late Mr. Robert Garnett, and the gates in front of the Town Hall, and the Cromwell statue, presented by Mr. Monks, in all of which cases the cost of the foundations and erection was paid out of the rates by vote of the Council. Unfortunately, however, although the site for the Soldiers' Memorial in Queen's Gardens has been granted by the Council, there is an indisposition at present on the part of a majority of the General Pur- poses Committee to act in this case upon the precedents I already cited. To meet the unexpected additional expense thus incurred, and to provide the further cost of the bronze panels. upon which it has been decided to place the names of all the men who died during the war and who were connected with the South-Lancashire Regi- ment,and to defray such incidental expenses as postages. I advertising, &amp;c.,the sum of ,C 260 or £3OO is still needed. Although our neighbours in St. Helens and Widnes (from both of which towns a large number of men of the South-Laacashire Regiment came) have subscribed to wards the cost of the memorial, they readily acquiesced in the claims put forward that the memorial should be erected in Warrington. The Parks Committee (of which the ex-Mayor is chairman) have made it a condition that they will not accept the custody of the memorial until every penny of the cost attendant upon its erection has been paid. It is with the greatest reluctance that the Committee feel compelled to place these facts before your readers, because they are of opinion that in their attitude towards the Memorial Committee the Corporation are doing an injustice to their constituents, whose generous provision during the late war for the comfort of our soldiers and for their wives and families, and later for their widows and orphan children, was equalled in few towns, and exceeded in none throughout the length and breadth of England. It is, unfortunately, only too true that gratitude is sometimes short-lived, but in this appreciation of the men whose lives were yielded up in the sacred cause of patriotism, we who stayed At home in comfort are only doing the barest justice to our better selves by recording their names in enduring bronze, so that our children's children may know of the part which was played by our honoured -dead in that momentous period of our country's history. The committee, venture to hope that the people of Warrington will respond during the next few days to this urgent appeal, so that when the distinguished soldier, General Sir Redvers Buller, visits us on the 21st. inst., we may ask the Mayor to accept, in the name of the people of Warrington, a memorial which is not only intrinsically of great value, but is a striking tribute to the ability of the eminent sculptor to whom the work was entrusted, and will be a great source of attraction to the gardens in which it is erected.— Yours, &amp;c., oth February, 1007.</p>
        <p>11. ROBERTS, Chairman. !</p>
        <p>BREWSTER SESSIONS. PROPOSED BILLIARD SALOON. Application for Licence Refused. The annual licensing sessions for the County Borough of Warrington were held at the Police Court yesterday. The Mayor presided and the other magistrates present were: Aldermen Tinnion and Bennett, Councillors Ellison and J. Evans, Messrs. J. Fairciough,E. J. Guest J. W. Hayward, S. Wright, W. Roberts, H. Thornton, G. Pilling, W. Reynolds, T. Locker, C. Jackson Holmes, • and J. Maginn. CHIEF CONSTABLE'S REPORT. In his annual report, the Chief Constable said there -- were in the Borough 81 alehouses, 36 beerhouses, and 83 off-licences (shops) which included those premises licensed for wines, spirits, Scc. This gave a total of 203 licenses, a proportion of 316.46 persons (census population) to each licence. There had been a decrease during the year of one alehouse, one wine (on and off) licence, and one off-licence. He had compiled a table with regard to 15 other municipalities approximating iii size to Warrington. The average number of inhabi- tants to each licence in the towns enumerated was 337. In Warrington the corresponding figure was 316.46. 333 persons were charged with drunkenness during the year, a decrease of 1 compared with the previous year. The average number of charges of drunkenness per annum in the. Borough during the past decade was 317.3. In the 15 towns before mentioned, the average number per each 1,000 inhabitants, was 6.70, the corresponding figure for Warrington being 5.1 g. He had once more to report no prosecutions during the year of licensees the houses 'generally having been conducted in a satisfactory manner. Several licensees were formally cautioned for minor irregularities, a record of the same being entered in the register kept for that purpose, and duly signed by the officers report- ing. The mu 11 temporary (14/days) applications for music licences were applied for and granted during the holiday seasons. This system would appear to work satisfactorily, no breaches of the privilege having been brought to his notice. The MAYOR expressed the pleasure of the magistrates at the satisfactory report from the Chief Constable. They hoped that the good conduct would continue. At the last Brewster Sessions, three licences, The Queen's Hotel, Boar's Head and Rope and Anchor Inn were referred to the compensation authority. Yester- day Mr. Longland, on behalf of the licensee of the Queen's Hotel applied for a provisional licence until the amount of compensation had been fixed. Mr. Steel made a similar application in respect of the Boar's Head. •Mr. Browne informed the Bench that the Rope and Anchor Inn had been closed. All existing licences with the exception of off- licenses were renewed, the question of renewing the off-licences being adjourned pending a report from the police. On the application of Mr. A. H. Clive the magistrates granted a billiard licence for Atkinson's Cafe, Bridge- street. PROPOSED BILLIARD SALOON. LICENCE REFUSED. Mr. Browne, on behalf of Mr. George A. Metcalf, Liverpool, applied for a billiard licence for the Jubilee Buildings, Horsemarket-street. His client, he explained, was a billiard table maker, and proposed, if the licence were granted,to place 11 tables in the Jubilee Buildings which he had secured on lease and for which he paid £135 per annum. The premises would be open from 10 a.m. to 11 p.m., and no intoxicating liquors or refreshments of any kind would be allowed. Persons tpuoi noynf rdoetaehtxmrhepweitl Benches ieeutonnol radcwtghphonnoeaosstoo:lfwhab ebnliasaBdslpciwulestiroroewgmuneol permitted. already granttlnecihdoalb: be e used, permittedwhilst ht iol enterst handi- caps a n t sfue such ht.ardeßnlridscoiewmoniflea tr, he l proceededicwehnos el!s tended to the reduction of intemperance as they In answer to Mr. Holloway, who oppcsed the. applica- tionc ate r o for r a behalf class of otfh opolice,customers Mwrh. Metcalf did nlfots 000 acadrehte wgoutld a public-house. Mr. Garnett put in the plans of the buildin The premises, he said,would be divided from those occupied:. by the Catholic )(elm; Men's Club by a partition. Mr. Holloway said they had bad a previous experience of billiard saloons in Warrington. (Mr. Brevne. Seventeen years ago.) He objected to the application because the licence would enable the applicant to keen open until one o'clock. Mr. G. F. F. White (magistrates' clerk) : They have given an undertaking to close at 11 p.m., and if they do not do so it will be a sufficient reason for refusing the licence on a future occasion. Mr. Holloway said another objection was that the saloons became the haunts of gamblers. Mr. Browne: Is that fair? Mr. Holloway said he did not suggest for one moment that the proposed saloon would become the resort of gamblers but it had been the past experience of the police that gambling followed the establishment of such saloons. Superintendent Monaghan said he objected to the licence because the same staircase led to the premises of . the Catholic Club and to the proposed new rooms. He would like the application better if drink was not sold on the Club premises. Mr. Brown : Do you suggest that the committee and members of that highly respectable Club would be parties to letting drink be consumed on these premises? —I don't suppose the heads would. Mr. Browne: Do you suppose the members would ? —I would not like to say. In answer to further questions witness stated that his experience of gamblers was that they were very tricky and would gamble right under Mr.Metcalf's nose. Mr. Steel, who appeared on behalf of several licensees in the neighbourhood of Jubilee Buildings also opposed the application on the grounds that the saloon was not required. The magistrates decided to refuse the application because of the fact that one stairway led both to the Club and to the proposed new premises. Mr. Browne gave notice that he would make a fresh spplication at the adjourned Sessions. Mr. Fenno, on behalf of the London-and North- itpaeelr fin and e:s teimonp t FCi exemption sdf hr ioi nrme respectt hLines rat eltoofafiltheys applied station contribution refresh- ment to t e rr Woei nts rooms. In the past the Bench had reduced the levy by one half. The magistrates decided to reduce the levy to two- thirds. In answer to Mr, Fenno, it was stated that the Bench could give no reason for their action. That amount had been proposed, and on a vote being taken, was accepted y the majority.</p>
