d, because they ! could not get that—but the reform of that which they : knew to be bad. The programme was chiefly a time I limit, disinterested management, and the control of , clubs. Other measures they had at heart were the i reduction of the number of houses, shorter hours • particularly on Saturday and Sunday, and counter I attractions to the public house. (Applause). 1 Mr. H. Veveme, 1e,..P., moved:— ' That this meet- ing expresses its satisfaction that the Govern- ment has announced its intention of introduc- ing ! ine a temperance measure in the next session 'of Parliament and is of opinion that the proposals of the Temperance Legislation League form the basis of : a scheme which is comprehensive and practicable." 1 He said the League was established principally to meet ; the special situation that they had at the present time. I It did not in any way pretend to supplant or indeed to ; improve upon existing temperance organizations in. ; their special sphere, but it was thought desirable, now ' that they had a Government pledged to a substantial I ; measure of temperance reform, to endeavour to impress that Government with the great body of opinion that I I was behind any sane and reasonable proposal for I temperance reform. The League was created specially 1 for that work, and they wanted to submit not an abso- -1 lute programme which the Government must carry out, 1 but some rough outlines of what they hoped would be 1 the Licensing measure of the Government. They had to 1 show that there was an intelligent demand among the ' electorate for something practical lu the way of a ILicensing measure. Roughly speaking, the community ' divided itself into three parts. They had the advance Iguard of temperance advocates who in various degrees I went out into the wilds of drunkenness and endeavoured to reclaim people.At the other extreme they had the very ; ; powerful vested interest of the trade. In numbers the I temperance section probably might be greater than the ; number of people interested in the trade, although with , the recent developments of company life in the drink , trade he was not at all sure that that was not getting less and less true. By the division of capital into I shares and the floating of that capital upon the public, they got an enormous driving force in the shape of , persons interested in the growth of the trade. The two , sections he had mentioned only formed a minority of the community, and in order to justify any important step forward they must get the support of the very large proportion of that element of the community which went between the two extremes. Otherwise they would find they had legislated either over the heads or in advance of the opinion of the community. It was to endeavour to organize in some way the influence of that middle element that the-League was established and it included amongst its supporters eminent men in every branch of science, in every school of theological thought, and of every sbad.e of politics. The first point in the rough outline of the League's proposals was the enactment of a time Per- limit. In order to explain that, they must inquire into P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P.centage. the cause of the special circumstances surrounding the Halifax 25 21 2 2 93 88 450 29 39 175 88-00 drink trade aecompared with any other trade. It was Leeds 22 17 4 1 61 73 356 25 40 170 79-54 early discovered that the sale of drink produced evils Oldham 95 18 6 1 56 77 343 33 43 195 74-00 of an exceptional kind and thereforethe:trade was regu- Warrington 23 16 6 1 63 92 402 38 42 202 71-34 lated in a way w birth did not apply to any other business. Runcorn 2l 15 6 0 58 96 401 29 35 163 7118 Gradually that regulation began to take the form of a Wigan 95 17 7 1 83117 520 39 45 213 70.00 limitation of the number of houses, and authorities Hunslet 94 16 8 0 62 86 382 50 57 271 66-66 were appointed for the purpose of issuing licenses. so Keighley lB 11 6 1 45 61 273 28 42 182 63-88 that a man could not set up the trade at his will, but Broughton 2B 14 8 1 59 87 379 31 36 170 63-04 had to apply for a licence, and some regard was said to Barrow 91 11 9 1 38 69 283 39 57 219 54-76 be paid to the demand by the population. Directly Salford 94 13 11 0 58 77 347 55 52 266 51-16 they limited by law the number of houses or persons Widnes .. I4 13 7 1 25 26 128 32 54 226 4642 engaged in the sale of a commodity, they gave that trade Dewsbury 2l 911 1 43 67 287 47 69 301 45-23 a monopoly value over and above the normal value that Batley l9 810 1 41 40 202 42 56 252 41.73 attached to ordinary trading. They had, con- Leigh oe 912 1 34 64 260 42 53 243 43-18 tended Mr. Vivian, no right to present public Hull NeR 93 10 13 039 56 246 43 53 245 42'47 values to private people who had not created Hull 91 10 14 0 41 58 256 63 89 393 41-66 them. (Applause). They were told now that in grant- Rochdale l9 711 1 24 51 201 30 55 225 39-47 ins' compensation they had to consider the loss to the Swinton 23 914 0 35 46 208 47 61 286 3913 brewers caused by the closing of houses. It was really Hud'sfield 2l 813 0 38 77 307 64 74 350 38-09 a scandal to think that the brewers had so worked their Bradford 23 715 1 52 67 305 54 71 321 3260 way into our administration of justice as to be able to Walefild T lB 512 1 26 42 178 39 74 300 30-55 claim a thing of that kind. (Applause). The monopoly St. Helens'.-19 514 0 36 49 219 39 69 285 26-31 value belonged to the public and, theoretically speaking York l6 412 0 22 36 152 40 61 263 25-00 if they started to-morrow with a clean slate to issue Bramley ..... l7 115 - 1 716 62 44 83 337 8-81 licences, they woVld be instilled in issuing them minus Liverpl. C 22 021 1 10 15 65 119 228 922 2-27 every penny of monopoly value. (Applause). It was the immense driving power which monopoly gave to the trade that had enabled it to acquire such an enormous power corruptly for corrupt purposes in this country. The only thing they had to consider, in his (Mr. Vivian's) opinion was how they could arrive at the goal of giving that monopoly value back to the public. He would not give the trade sixpence of it—(applause)— but legislation had given it to them. The last Act practically gave them a freehold in the licences and thus created a vested interest in them.ln this country we, had always proceeded on the lines of paying due recog- nition to vested interests when they had been the result of public action. The League therefore did not suggest that to-morrow they should say to the brewers by Act of Parliament : "You have no right to the monopoly value and by Monday morning we shall capture it." He thought that was not a principle, even in a case of this kind, that they should get the public mind in the habit of adopting, or they might do it in a very inconvenient way, and therefore he was prepared to recognise a resonable claim which followed the legislation embodied in the last Act. The League, however, said they must have a reasonable time limit, and he personally was prepared to be fairly generous if they began at once. They wanted the new Bill to enact that after a certain time the public should come by their own and have absolute control of the licences and issue them under whet conditions they cared to determine. (Applause.) Mr. Vivian showed how the brewers used the monopoly value to pile up the allurements and attractions to people to frequent the public houses. Apart from the economic aspect, as a mere question of the reduction of drink consumed, this phase was well worthy of the consideration of every temperance reformer, because they would take from the trade an immense driving force which this added value gave. (Applause.) There was no political or municipal election which was free from the contamination of corruption from the drink. (Applause.) He had mit a word to say personally against any publican or brewer for he had no doubt that if any of those present had been brought up in the trade they would do practically what those persons did. It was, continued Mr. Vivian, contrary to public well being and a good code of political morals that a vote in the House of Commons or a municipality should determine a man's poverty or wealth. He ought to be able to exercise it with a single eye to the public interest. Owing to the influence</p>
        <p>Warrington" A " wifl probably have to relinquish the championship of the Lancashire Combination this season. The failure to complete the home fixtures with Oldham "A " and Wigan Highfield has resulted in the loss of two points. Wigan "A. " are going ahead strongly and on Saturday won by 15 points to 8 points at Oldham.</p>
        <p>If the frost permits Warrington will go to Hudders- field to-day and play the return with the team tlley ran up the huge score of 50 points against earlier in the season. Any such easy victory at Fartown need not be looked for, and it will probably take Warring- ton all their time to win, especially as Isherwood and Preston will be absentees. The former is injured slightly and the latter has been displaced by Boardman.</p>
        <p>The team is :—Berry, fnll back ; Hockenhall, Cart- wright, Dickenson and Brooks threequarters ; Lees and Jenkins half backs; Thomas, Boardman; Harmer, Heath, Naylor and Shugars forwards.</p>
        <p>The public generally hoped that Fish would have turned out in thts match,but though his arm is alright he is not selected. Without him the Shrove Tuesday match at Halifax will 1003 e a lot of its attraction. If training is thought necessary he might have had a canter in the " A " team.</p>
        <p>and position of the "trade," somemencoald not do that. By past legislation we had made tens of millions of value depend upon those who were engaged in the Temperance League's programme being defeated. The League wanted to propose something that the average man was prepared to accept as reasonable and fair. One I would almost imagine from some of their temperance , friends, that there was no temperance sentiment among he great mass of artisans who might not be teetotalers. There wore in this country hundreds of thousands of workpeople and others who liked their glass of supper, beer and whose homes were as pure and clean and whose lives were as virtuous as the most extreme teetotaller in the land, and who would, if the case was put to them fairly and squarely, at once see that in getting legisla- tion that would level up the licensing Laws, there would ! I be a tendency to decrease the consumption of drink. He ! believed public sentiment would in the main accept I Sunday closing. (Applause.) There were a number of things which the sentiment of the nation would support ' Parliament in doing, but the League said that in addi- tion they wanted to give localities the power and the I freedom to work above the plan that Parliament had I determined for the nation as a whole. Public-houses should be closed on election days. (Applause.) Mr. Vivian then argued in favour of the League's proposal that localities should have the power to try the dis- interested management of public-houses. The licences would be issued not to brewers but to a company registered in the ordinary way, and whatever the profits amounted to, not more than a fixed rate of interest should be paid, the remainder going to the national_ exchequer. He contended that a house managed in that way by a body of public-spirited citizens would not do the same injury as a private gin-palace with all its. attractions. It would be far better to have this alter- native system than only the present extremes of either no lieence or a licence to a brewer. He believed the best policy to be to give the various districts power to try experiments in temperance reform with a view to determining the lines on which there should be a national solution. (Applause). Archdeacon IVIIDDEN seconded and said that as practical men they must face these questions in a practical manner. They must enlist the support of that middle; part of the community that believed there was a necessity for some atrong measures on t,emperance,but would not go the whole hog with the extremists. He believed that if all the sober and sober-minded men would stand together at this time they would get a measure which, while not being ideal, would do a great deal to make the country sober and free. He would sooner, see Saturday . closing than Sunday closing, although he wanted both, because Saturday drinking was the prolific source of Sunday drinking. He did not want Sunday closing to. be undertaken piecemeal, but he would vote for a national measure. He believed also that public opinion was in favour of a national measure and that it would be put to the test in the Bill. He was glad the league intended to deal with clubs, because in-Liverpool the clubs had proved their greatest difficulties. All places where young men were tempted to drink ought to be under strict regulation,so that it might be knswn what went on in them. (Applause). One of the failures of the temperance party had been that they had not provided counter attractions to the public house. He believed the League was won l ing on right principles and that when the details came to be worked out it would be on straight, intelligent lines that would appeal to the whole com- munity. (Applause.) Mr. C. E. PARKER said that those who were 'perhaps temperance fanatics were glad to be able to take a posi- tion with and receive the co-operation of those who had not been able to go the whole length with them in regard to the demands they had made. He believed the advance guard would accomplish more if instead of going too far forward and being cut off, they would remain a little behind and advance with the main army, entrenching the position as they went. There was a very strong feeling in regard to the question of local veto, and to many of them it seemed that it was advancing in a rather violent way. In England perhaps more than any country in the world the law was honoured and respected. The policy explained by Mr. Vivian would, he thought, gain the support of such a majority of the people that they would have a wise and just Act of Parliament passed. (Applause): .</p>
        <p>• The resOlution was carried. A qEMBER of the audience asked whether by dis- interested management they were going to reduce drunkenness to any appremable extent. Why should they advocate the scheme which had proved a failure in other countries when local veto had been such a succes ? He instanced Norway where local veto had largely taken the place of disinterested management. Then again, who would be disinterested when those who , invested their money received 5 per cent, the brewers and distiller a supplied the liquor, and the managers of the houses were paid salaries? Mr. VIVIAN in reply contended that disinterested management would reduce the consumption of drink, men of great intelligenas and capacity having. reported in favour of the system. The questioner 'matted out that in Norway districts had gone from disintested management to local veto. Could there be any better argument in favour of disinterested mana,,,,ernent-? The people became so surrcunded by the atmosphere and spirit of temperance under disinterested mmagement that they said they would go forward and have local veto. (Applause.) With regard to disinterestedness. they got a holy of public men to invest their money • they limited their interest on capital ; and they, of course, paid the manager and employees their wages. He contended, however, that that interest was nothing similar to the interest of the great driving force of a brewing or distilling company, getting their living and almost unlimited interest out of the bosiness. (Applause.) The Rev. SIDNEY PITT, in moving a vote of thanks to the speakers, said many would hesitate to adopt dis- interested management as a national system, but he thought the experiment ought to be tried. He there- fore asked temperance friends to abstain from opposi- tion in order that a trial might be given, in the hope of a good result. Mr. W. ROBERTS seconded and said temperance people made a fatal mistake in their opposition to Mr. Bruce's Bill thirty years ago. as that measure would have been a great step forward. As temperance reformers the y must be reasonable. They had been shouting to the moon for all these years and when a great measure was brought forward, because it did not go entirely as they would wish it, they refused to support it. fie hoped they would not make such a mistake this time. The country was ripe for a reasonable measure of temper- ance reform, and if they could not get the whole loaf, let them accept half a one. They must not, however, try to thrust local veto down the throats of the people when they were not prepared for it. (Applause.) The vote was heartily accorded.</p>
