      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>PONY'S STRANGE BED.</head>
        <p>PONY'S STRANGE BED.</p>
        <p>Breaking out of a field in the Coventry district, a pony made its way to yard where it had been stabled. Finding the back door of an adjoining house open, the animal went inside and lay down on a bicycle, consider- ably damaging it. The owner of the machine sued the owner of the field for damages, but was non-suited by Judge Ingham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>DEVOTED PUBLIC SERVANT.</head>
        <p>A DEVOTED PUBLIC SERVANT.</p>
        <p>Wairington has lost a devoted public servant by the death of Dr. John Guest Gornall, M.A.., M.B. (Cantab), Medical Officer of Health for the Borough and medical superintendent of the Fever Hospital, who passed away at his residence, Ribblesdale, Grappenhall, on Monday evening. Although the deceased gentleman had been in a very delicate state of health for some time, the end came unexpectedly and created a profound sensation amongst every section of the community, by whom he was held in the greatest respect. Since the serious attack of typhoid fever which threatened his life several years ago, Dr. Gornall had never been of a very robust constitution but be had done his work most conscientiously and indefatigably. Latterly it became evident that his health was rapidly declining and that he must take a rest and change if he was to be restored. At the December meeting of the Town Council, Dr. Gornall was granted prolonged leave of absence in the hope that he would recuperate. A lady physician and surgeon Dr. Gafilkin—was also appointed to assist him in his work which for many reasons had of late years increased very considerably. Dr. Gornall went to Llandudno, but since his return he had been confined to his bed, suffering acutely from Bright's disease and asthma. His eyesight also completely failed him and he gradually sank and passed away on Monday evening. Dr. Gornall's life was a record of unselfish devotion to the ainelioi ation of human suffering, and his was indeed a strenuous career. He was the son of the late Dr. John Hankinson Go:nall, J.P., for many years medical officer of health for the borough. His early education was obtained at the Boteler Grammar School, where he displayed much promise, and subse- quently he proceeded to Cambridge. Shortly after obtaining the degree of M.B.,he returned to Warrington and was appointed to the position of Assistant Medical Officer. He was very soon called upon to assist his father in stamping out the terrible epidemic of small- pox which prevailed in the town in the years 1902-3. How trying the work was may be more fully realized when it is stated that during the whole of the time he lived in the Hope Hospital in Dallam-lane, attending the victims of the disease, and he -hardly ever left the building until the outbreak was over. The epidemic assumed the gravest proportions, there beim-, 674 cases and 65 deaths. Few nobler instances of self- sacrifice can be afforded than the zeal with which the young doctor combated the fell disease, and his services at the time called forth the gratitude of the townspeople. The report which he subsequently pre- seated to the Health Committee was a masterpiece of , its kind, and became a standard work on the subject ,of epidemics. Almost every medical officer in tha country applied for a copy and the Local Government Board asked to be furnished with several. Unfortun- ately Dr. Gornall's labours told heavily upon him and he was stricken down with typhoid fever. His life was despaired of —in fact medical experts held out no hope —but he recovered. He never, however, seemed to regain his usual health and the weakening effects of the attack no doubt made him an easier victim to his fatal illness. Dr. Gornall continued to assist his father until the latter died, and on October Ist, 1901, he succeeded him in the offices of Medical Officer of Health, Medical Superintendent of the Hospital, and Public Analyst, the Council giving him a free hand in dealing with tuberculosis. Dr. Gornall discharged his many onerous duties with conscien- tiousness and skill and his work became so heavy that the Council,ftwo years ago, relieved him of the office of Public Analyst. He, however, had to undertake other responsibilities in connection with the administration of the Education Act and one task was to make a medical examination of the school children. In one year he discovered 500 scholars with defective eyesight. It is impossible to speak too highly of Dr. Gornall's work for .the town. He was always impelled with the deepest sense of duty, and never hesitated to condemn anything which mili- tated against the publiC health. His annual reports were models of what such important documents should be, and contained a mass ( f valuable information. In disposition, he was kindly and unassuming, and his death at the comparatively early age of forty, will come as a personal loss to a very wide circle. The deepest sympathy will be felt with the widow and family of six young crildren in their great bereavement.</p>
        <p>REFERENCES AT THE COUNCIL MEETING. Sympathetic references were made at the meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday. The MAYOR in moving a vote of condolence with the widow and family said It is my painful duty to inform you that our Medical Officer of Health (Dr. Gornall) died at his residence on Monday. Dr. Gornall was appointed Medical Officer of Health on the death of his father, which occurred six years ago. You will remember that Dr. Gornall commence&amp; his official duties with the Corporation during the terrible smallpox epidemic' b which raged in the town from 1892 to 1893. During that period Dr. Gornall not only lived in the hospital, but he had almost entire medical superintendence of the institution and we all know the great zeal and attention he applied in his work. It was no doubt owing to tke great bodily strain the doctor had undergone which rendered him an easy victim to an attack of typhoid fever, with which he was seized during the dis- charge of his public duties. It may be said that Dr. Gornall suffered almost permanent injury to his health in consequence of that attack. He had been ailing for some time,and only recently the Council agreed to give him an extended holiday, to see if rest and quiet would recoup his health. Apparently, the proposed remedy came too late, and the doctor passed away yesterday. I am sure our deep sympathy will go out to the widow and the six little fatherless children. It will be a sore trial to them, and I trust that God will comfort them in their great trouble. I beg to move :—" That the Council record their deep regret at the loss which they and the town have sustained by the death of the Medical Officer of Health, and their warm appreciation of the zeal and devotion displayed by him in the discharge of his public duties."</p>
        <p>Tfie DEPUTY MAYOR (Alderman Bolton) seconded. The late Dr. Gornall was an officer who was very emphatic and enthusiastic in his work. He certainly did his best for his committee and for the health of the town and in such a manner that no one had been ever heard to complain. It was practically overwork that had brought him to the condition in which he had been placed during the past six months. He was sorry the doctor had not taken his holidays six months ago. (Hear, hear). Councillor EVANS supported, and said it was well- known to the members of the Health Committee that the doctor bad been failing in health for some tirne,and whilst they anticipated that he might be laid aside for a considerable time, his death had come suddenly upon them. He was only voicing the sentiments of the Health Committee when he said that they were all sincerely sorry. (Hear, hear). Warrington had lost a worthy citizen, the Corporation a very worthy, truthful, and faithful servant ; in fact, he felt, with other mem- bers of the Health Committee, that the doctor had practically given his life, because he had been con- scientious and faithful to duty. He told the doctor a little time ago that it would be well for him to have a complete rest, and take a long holiday, so that his life might be prolonged. He went for a little time but returned long before he ought to have done and day after day be had been plodding on faithfully doing his best. It was to Dr. Gornall a sore trial that he had so early to leave the public life of the town and fa9o— to him was a great family treasure—his children. He had seen some little of his home life, and Dr. Gornall felt that the members of his family were the joy of his heart. The late Medical Officer of Health shared the good feeling of the Council, the officials,and of the people of the town generally. (Hear, hear). Councillor Dr. JOSEPH said he had known the doctor from his early boyhood. He looked upon him as a splendid instance of a well-educated English medical man, one of the typical instances of a good man with all thWnoble instincts that medical men at their best were supposed to have. He probably received his mortal injuries in his devotion to duty and he regarded him as one of the medical martyrs—men who were as much martyrs as soldiers on the battlefield, because they were engaged ins noble warfare against that. arch- enemy of mankind, disease, especially contagious disease. (Hear, hear.) Councillor STOTT said he appreciated Dr. Guest Gornall, not only as a man, but as a servant of the Cor- poration. He had been surprised at the extent of his knowledge in regard to the working classes, and with the great interest he took in the welfare of their children. They all sympathized, especially family men, when a father was taken away. , The motion was carried in silence, all the members of the Council standing while it was put to the vote.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The funeral, which took place at the Cemetery yester- day, was the occasion of a striking demonstration of respect. Representatives of public bodies and all I sections of the community witnessed the lgst rites, and I an impressive scene was presented. The first part of the service was held in Christ Church, Latchford, which was filled, the congregation including the Mayor and members of the Corporation. The choir was in attend- ance, and the choral portion of the service was beautifully rendered. Mr. T. L. Fry presided at the organ, and as the coffin was borne into the Church he played" 0 rest in the Lord." The Rev. G. C. Briggs, vicar of Christ Church, read the opening sentenoes.and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>SECONDARY SCHOOL CONCERT.</head>
        <p>SECONDARY SCHOOL CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The teachers and pupils of the Warrington Secondary School are to be congratulated upon the success of their annual concert, which was held in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening. The venture was the second of its kind, and the marked improvement upon last year's concert, which was very creditable as an initial effort, must have been gratifying to all concerned. There was a large audience and the programme, which was one of exceptional attractiveness and variety,was followed with great interest and pleasure. The school chorus and orchestra, with a combined membership of 200, figured prominently under the conductorship of Mr. R. H. Albery. The first part of the entertainment, which had been arranged by Mr. R. H. Albery, Mr. J. B. Baxter and Mr. A. E. Hoyle, consisted of vocal and instru- mental music and was fittingly opened with the over- ture "The Chevalier Breton " by the orchestra. Two choice choruses from " Maritana "—" The Angelus " and "Turn on old time "—followed, and the interpreta- tion of these eTer popular compositions was most effec- tive. Violet Brown, who possesses a sweet voice, sang a pretty "Irish Slumber song" which proved so accept- able that an encore was accorded. George Whitfield, attired as old father Neptune, sang a wierd song of the sea with fine gusto. That beautiful composition "Down the vale" was given with feeling by Mary Hackett, and John Greaves was recalled for his exceed- ingly creditable rendering of "The swallows." A ' contingent of boys clad in picturesque old- time costumes sang Yeomen of England" which was one of the successes of the evening. The audience was then carried back in imagination to the days of Queen Elizabeth, that monarch being impersonated by Winifred Brock who comported herself with befitting dignity and sang very expressively. A number of girls gave a charmin,, part song "Two little maids" and executed a graceful dance. The orchestra contributed several selections, the most popular being" The motor ride"—interrupted by a breakdown which was promptly repaired at the village smithy. The arrangement of the second part of the programme, introducing costume and action songs,was in the hands of Miss B.Dawson and Miss E. Wilkinson. A piquant rendering of the trio " Three little maids" by Violet Brown, Mary Hackett, and Gladys Joynson, so delighted the assembly that a repetition was demarried. Equally well received was the duet " A.8.C." by Alice Taylor and Thomas Allender who took the parts of the irate teacher and dull scholar to perfection. One of the most original features of the concert was the "Policeman's chorus," the grotesque actions of the men in blue, who achieved smart captures of a furious motorist and a bold but youthful burglar, keeping the house in a state of merriment. A surprise was in store, for on the conclusion of the chorus some of the con- stables summoned up courage to bring in two baskets which had been mysteriously deposited outside. When these were opened, they were found to contain two living dolls who forthwith sang the well-known 'Doll daet " in a way which took the audience by storm. As the dolls John Greaves and Percy Clark took their parts admirably. The accompaniments to the various songs were well played by Violet Brown. The concluding portion of the enter- tainment (arranged by Miss E. Frost and Miss M. L. Mitchell) consisted of three scenes from Shakespeare's "A midsummer night's dream." The production was far beyond the average for such youthful amateurs, and it is due to all who appeared to sly that the ever- welcome scenes embodying "the most lamentable comedy and most cruel death of Pyramus and Thisbe" were very pleasingly presented. There was hardly any trace of nervousness and the words were distinctly uttered. An exceedingly pretty picture unfolded itself when the Duke of Athens, his bride and their courtiers, were assembled in state, the costumes being bright and taste- ful. The characters were taken by the following : Theseus, Duke of Athens, Marion Collin; Hippolyta, his bride, Lilian Wright; Demetrius, a Noble of Athens Eva Evans ; Helena, his bride, Dora Parsonage ; Lysander, a Noble of Athens, Elsie Swain° ; Hermia, his bride, Jessie Upson; Philostrate, master of revels to Theseus, Pattie Woodcock; Bottom (a weaver), Winifred Brock ; Flute (a bellows-mender), Nellie Hawkesworth ; Quince (a carpenter), Edith Isaac ; 4. Snout (a tinker), Annie Jackson; Starveling (a tailor). Marion Jolley ; Snug (a joiner), Lattice Warburton. While all acquitted themselves well, special praise is due to Winifred Brock who excelled as Bottom the weaver. Nellie Hawkesworth also rose to the occasion as Flute and "Thisbe" and the roles of the other workmen-players were capably sustained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>Band • of Hope.</head>
        <p>Band • of Hope.</p>
        <p>The second entertainment of the season in con- nection with the Workingmen's Mission Band of Hope was held in the schooolroom on Tuesday evening before a large audience. Mr. H. Nels-m presided and gave an interesting address which was much appre- ciated. The first item on the programme was a pianoforte solo by Miss Amy Hough, which was well executed. Songs were rendered by the Misses C. and F. Vane; M. Oakley, Cotterill, and Eva Tanner.' Recitations were given by Emily Knight, Alma Vane, Nellie Eden, Ethel Oakley and Arthur Dennett. dialogue entitled "The Band of Hone Prize " was given by five girls, also gramophone selections by Mr. H. Foden. The last item on the programme was a dialogue entitled " Slightly mixed" which was given by members of the Young Ladies' Class. Mr. F. Atkinson and Mr. T. Tanner were the accompanists. A vote of thanks to the Chairman and artistes proposed by Mr. R. E. Jones brought a very enjoyable meeting to a close.</p>
        <p>Cadishead should be the visitors to Warringt ,n, but at the time of writing the ground is unplayable A GUARANTEED CURE FOR PILES, The following team has been selected :—Tilley, full Itching, Blind, Bleeding, or Protruding. All chemists back ; Jordan, Taylor, Knight and Newall three- are authorised to refund money if Pazo Ointment fails quarters ; Kay and Lambert. half backs ; Forster, to cure Piles. Cures ordinary cases in six days. One .Tollev, Swift, Hewitt, Bradshaw and Molbrook, application gives ease and rest. Pazo Ointment is a forwards. new discovery, and the only Pile remedy soldon a posi tive guarantee. Price 2s. 3d., of all chemist or from,— ' PARIS MEDICINE Co., TEMPLE CITA MBE EC S,LONDON, E.C.</p>
        <p>' The match with Liverpool City will be played at ; Warrington on Monday, February 18th,but the Widnes match has not yet been arranged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-09_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <p>4</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 9, 1907.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL. [By " Ovalist."]</p>
        <p>There has not been much football of late, and some of the teams are in danger of getting rusty for want of use. Fortunately for the Warrington men they had a match on Monday, and replaced the stiffness caused by inactivity with stiffness due to the hard and lumpy ground at Oldham. Not much boside hArd knocks was gained by the visitors, for. Oldham triumphed in decisive fashion, though the score of seventeen points to eight over-represents their superiority. Wartington scored two tries to Oldham's three, the latter's goal-kicking powers making up the balance of points.</p>
        <p>The few enthusiasts who accompanied the Warring- ton men to Spindle(Tom were not rewarded by a very edifying exhibition. Oldham's playing pitch was in a sorry state, and resembled a farm-yard more than a field. What with snow, straw, and lumps of frozen earth the surface was anything but level, and to say it was fit for serious football is to stretch a point. Straight running was almost impossible, and the way the ball bounced simply confounded the 'man who was foolish enough to wait for it. The conditions were, of course, the same for both teams but the home lot seemed much more at home on the uneven surface than the Warrington men,and were also more inclined to take risks. Some of the Warrington lads evidently had a care for their bones and Berry, in particular, did not ,go down to stop a rush as he should do accord- ing to the rules of full back play. In fact the War- rington custodian gave a display very much below his reputation, and some of the Oldham pe.)ple were ask- ing if it was really Berry.</p>
        <p>The first half was a disastrous period for Warring- ton, who found themselves in arrears of eleven points at the interval. Preston early on missed an easy goal shot, and then Fergusson gave him an object- lesson in the art of place kicking. • Thomas opened the scoring with a drop-kick, and Yewlett added a try as the result of Jenkins passing the ball on his own line. Fergusson follJwed with three beautiful goals —the first from the try, the second from his own mark, and the third from a mark at centre. Warrington hardly ever looked like scoring, though Flockenhall twice dropped the ball when he was splendidly placed for getting a try.</p>
        <p>For ten minutes in the second half Warrington made things lively and showed something like their best form. Hockenhall cleverly dodged over after Isherwood had dribbled past the full back,and Preston goaled. The success spurred Warrington on and they swarmed round the Oldham goal, a try coming to the forwards five minutes later almost behind the posts. The place-kick was absurdly easy but Preston sent the ball under the bar and quite disheartened his comrades. The score should have been 10-11, but it failed to materialise, and a passing bout being next intercepted by Dixon when Brooks was clear, turned the scale in favour of Oldham again. Berry got badly kicked and while he yas absent the home forwards rushed the ball over the line. There was a flurry in the snow and Vowles got possession, Fergusson failing to convert an easy one. Oldham had a couple of narrow o,papckq through the smartness of the Warrington centres, but Cash took full advantage of Berry's failure to field the ball and scored the third try at the corner.</p>
        <p>Warrington were fairly beaten. They were weak in attack on the wings and unsteady in defence. Hockenhall was very poor in the first half, but he improved, while Brooks was only moderate all through, and could never hold Cash. Isherwood and Dickenson were the stars of the team, and both played fine foot- ball under difficulties. The halves were well matched, Jenkins and Yewlett being smarter than the two Lees's. "Sammy" was not himself and got a nasty bump on the cranium. "Birdie" Dixon did great service in the kicking department, where he and Thomas quite outshone the Warrington punters, but the Oldham men are not playing a back game in accordance with their old traditions. There is too much of the mark and place for Fergusson, and too little combination. On a fast ground Warrington should beat them and I have no fear of the result at Wilderspool on Saturday next.</p>
        <p>The Ledgue Table up-to-date is as follows : For Agst.</p>
      </div>
