        <p>_ lovea me as aearty as Jack does he would never have gone away. Perhaps I have the better man of the two for a husband, after all ! " Olive could not make out what was wrong be- tween her brother and his wife. There had been a quarrel. Of course, she and Paul discussed it, but could not understand what two who loved each other could fall out about. "And, of course, they do love one another," she said. "Of course," he answered, with becoming innocency. "Why, they are husband and wife. Husband and wife always love one another utterly—just as you and I always will, my sweet." "And never, never quarrel ? " "How could we ? -Why, darling, there will be nothing to quarrel about. The whole world will hp bathed in joy's sunghine," I Pale, unkempt, hollow-eyed, unobservant of the wondering looks of the servants, Jack Brandon made his way to his wife's room, knocked at the door, implored her to see him, , if only for a minute, then waited breathlessly, his heart beating wildly. What he should say he knew not, but if only she would see him, surely, surely, she would forgive. And for the first time he heard her footsteps coming to the door. His heart beat so wildly he thought it must burst. Then the key was turned. He waited an instant, then, almost stumbling, he entered the room. She was standing and looking at him, but not as he had expected—not white-faced, angry-eyed. Her cheeks were flushed, her eyes shone, her lips were a little parted. He did the wisest thing he could, he strode towards her, caught her in his arms, crushed her to his heart, kissed her again and again until, half breathless, her little hands held his ;lead away. "You have half killed me," he said, speaking hurriedly, his heart throbbing to her heart. "How could you when I love you so dearly? If I did wrong, I only fought as one should fight for what is nearest and dearest. Heart of my heart, wife of my bosom, forgive met Forgive me, and take me back ! " She lean't away from him, but he held her still. "I have been thinking," she said, softly, "that one is, perhaps, not to be judged harshly for what one does for love, and so—" she broke off suddenly. "What of Dick ? " she said. "Is he here ? " His clasp tightened. "No—no!" he answered. "He has gone back whence he came. I can't make it up to him, but I will do what lean, and he—he—did not love you as I love you. I would have killed him before he should have come between us—l was your husband—and he let me live. And so--" She looked at him searchingly with Der beautiful shallow eyes. She smiled at him with her lovely mouth. She flushed under his ardent gaze. "And BO I forgive you," she answered. "Aftez all, he was only my lover, while you are part of myself. Say what the world will, it makes difference 1" THD END. 3</p>
        <p>• PAIN. Many of us suffer certain kinds of pain, as if Science could not come to the help of Nature. There is no necessity to suffer from Rheumatism Headache Gout Biliousness Neuralgia Indigestion Backache Blood Sciatica Disorders The pain Caused by these is but Nature's means of letting us know that the Kidneys or Liver are for some reason unable to perform their work efficiently, for all these disorders are produced by the retention in the system of urinary and biliary poisons, which would be thrown off naturally if the kidneys and liver were doing their duty. The health of the liver and of the kidneys is so closely connected that it is almost impossible for the kidneys to be affected and the liver to remain healthy, or vice versa. It is nearly thirty years since scien- tific research directed specially to diseases of the kidneys and liver was rewarded by the discovery of the medi- cine now known throughout the world as Warner's 'Safe' Cure the one curative agent which acts equally on the kidneys and liver. Warner's Safe' Cur 3 cures all diseases of the kidneys and liver. May be had of Chemists, 2/9 and 4/6, or post free for price direct from H. H. WARNER St Co., Ltd., 86 Clerken- well Road, London, E.C. •</p>
