        <p>It is declared by Professor Bottomley that a skirt 'which a lady had trailed on the pavement during an hour's stroll in the West End, haying been sent to a laboratory;" was found to contain sixteen and a half million microbes, many of them the bacteria of con- sumption." We commend this excellent idea to the L.C.C. Highways Committee. What we want is a Clean London—." Daily News." * * * In advocating the St. Elphin's Park scheme on Tuesday at the Town Council, Alderman Bennett flelivered a lengthy oration which wound up with quite poetic fervour. During the concluding stanza he said he believed " there were some starved and stunted souls who cared nothing for the ashes of their fathers and the temples of their Gods.' 'There was such a hearty laugh at this point that considerable colour was given to the Alderman's assumption. .Later, Alderman Bolton openly scoffed at such things and said be did not care -even if William the Conqueror had walked over the ,-spire of the Parish Church. The Deputy Mayor had ever a nice way of putting things! * * * Those so-called evening newspapers published in the early morning, containing merely a rehash of the news from the morning papers and the racing tips for the current day, which have hitherto relied mainly for their circulation upon the smaller fry of the sporting fraternity engaged in street-betting, are feeling the effects of the working of the new Betting Act to a serious extent. One of the evening papers in London, has already dropped its early (sporting) edition, and another, it is said, contemplates doing so. The latter is also about to abandon—if it has not already done so —one of its principal distributing offices in a populous working-class district. At this office it was the practice to stamp in the results of the races, as they were telephoned from the head office, but apparently there is no longer a sufficient demand for racing news to justify the retention of the office. Certainly there is not the same numercial interest taken in tips and winners that there was. The Betting Act is unquestionably resnonsible for the dwindling number of street bookmakers, with a consequent 'reduction of their poor "victims."</p>
