        <p>nearly as possible, and the yaite and eilflis chiffon in a lighter shade. This method of intro- ducing cloth into the blouse would make it a good model for the make up of a short dres3 length, and a whole dress would thus be procured. DIIESSES FOR DAYTIME. Tailor-made gowns are the fashion, but there is a wide line of demarcation drawn, says the Queen, between the severe tailor-made morning dress and the elaborate tailor-made reception gowns, enlivened by much embroidery and lace. Wine-colour, grey, and deep purple shades are still worn. On all sides we find that the long- trained skirt is coming in again; but if you wear a Princess gown you ,must have a plain skirt, and • with a short jacket a full skirt is necessary. The favourite trimmings on skirts are braids and broad bands of velvet, some of the skirts being bound at the hem to a width of Bin. or 9in. with velvet, and some of the Empire dresses in cloth have the fulness starting from the centre of the back, no belt being worn, though the natural lines of the waist are indicated; the skirt is severely plain, and untrimmed from the waist-line. Some of the colours employed for these dressy afternoon gowns are altogether charming—rose pink, bird's-egg blue, salmon colour, sapphire, deep coral, coffee shades, and grey are all very well worn. TO-DAY'S RECIPE. CALF'S LIVER SATJTE.—Cut about Vb. of calf's liver in slices a in. thick, trim into nice outlet. shaped pieces, rinse in cold water, and dry in a clean cloth. Mix some chopped ham, onion, and narslev with bread-crumbs, and din each niece of flyer into beatened egg, tlien in tne seasoned bread-crumbs, and fry in deep fat till thoroughly roooked. Serve very hot with slices of fried 'bacon. Pour a thick brown gravy over.</p>
